        id: 8,
        title: "Archive & Compression — Production Backup Strategies",
        type: "theory",
        duration: "35 min",
        content: `
## Archive & Compression — Backup Essentials

Production'da backup — eng muhim vazifalardan biri. "Backup mavjud" degani yetarli emas — "backup restore qilish mumkin" bo'lishi kerak!

### Part 1: Tar — Tape Archive

#### Basic Tar Operations
\`\`\`bash
# CREATE archive (no compression)
tar -cvf archive.tar /path/to/dir
# c = create
# v = verbose (show progress)
# f = file (archive name)

# CREATE with compression
tar -czvf archive.tar.gz /path       # gzip (most common)
tar -cjvf archive.tar.bz2 /path      # bzip2 (better compression)
tar -cJvf archive.tar.xz /path       # xz (best compression)

# EXTRACT
tar -xvf archive.tar                 # x = extract
tar -xzvf archive.tar.gz             # extract gzip
tar -xjvf archive.tar.bz2            # extract bzip2
tar -xJvf archive.tar.xz             # extract xz

# Extract to specific directory
tar -xzvf archive.tar.gz -C /destination/

# LIST contents (without extracting)
tar -tvf archive.tar.gz              # t = list (table of contents)
tar -tzvf archive.tar.gz | grep "*.conf"  # Find specific files

# APPEND to existing archive (uncompressed only!)
tar -rvf archive.tar newfile.txt     # r = append
\`\`\`

#### Advanced Tar Options
\`\`\`bash
# Exclude files/directories
tar -czvf backup.tar.gz /home \\
    --exclude='*.tmp' \\
    --exclude='*.cache' \\
    --exclude='node_modules' \\
    --exclude='.git'

# Exclude using pattern file
echo "*.log" > exclude.txt
echo "tmp/" >> exclude.txt
tar -czvf backup.tar.gz -X exclude.txt /data

# Incremental backup (only changed files)
tar -czgvf full-backup.tar.gz --listed-incremental=backup.snar /data
tar -czgvf incremental1.tar.gz --listed-incremental=backup.snar /data

# Archive with permissions preserved
tar -cpzvf backup.tar.gz /etc        # p = preserve permissions

# Archive with absolute paths removed
tar -czvf backup.tar.gz -C /var/www html/

# Show archive size while creating
tar -czvf backup.tar.gz /data | pv -s $(du -sb /data | awk '{print $1}')

# Multi-volume archives (split large archives)
tar -czvf backup.tar.gz -L 1000M /data  # 1GB chunks
\`\`\`

### Part 2: Compression Tools Comparison

| Tool | Ext | Speed | Ratio | CPU | Memory | Best For |
|------|-----|-------|-------|-----|--------|----------|
| gzip | .gz | Fast | Good | Low | Low | Daily backups, logs |
| bzip2 | .bz2 | Medium | Better | Medium | Medium | Weekly archives |
| xz | .xz | Slow | Best | High | High | Long-term storage |
| lz4 | .lz4 | Very Fast | Poor | Very Low | Low | Real-time streaming |
| zstd | .zst | Fast | Great | Low-Med | Low | Modern all-purpose |

#### Real Compression Benchmarks (1GB file)
\`\`\`bash
# Test file
dd if=/dev/urandom of=testfile bs=1M count=1000

# Gzip (default level 6)
time gzip testfile               # ~10s, 60% compressed
time gzip -1 testfile            # Fast (level 1)
time gzip -9 testfile            # Best compression (level 9)

# Bzip2
time bzip2 testfile              # ~30s, 70% compressed

# Xz
time xz testfile                 # ~90s, 75% compressed

# Zstd (modern, best balance)
time zstd testfile               # ~5s, 65% compressed
\`\`\`

### Part 3: Individual File Compression

#### Gzip
\`\`\`bash
# Compress (removes original by default!)
gzip file.txt                    # Creates file.txt.gz

# Compress and keep original
gzip -k file.txt
gzip -c file.txt > file.txt.gz

# Decompress
gunzip file.txt.gz
gzip -d file.txt.gz

# View compressed file without extracting
zcat file.txt.gz                 # View content
zgrep "error" file.log.gz        # Grep in compressed
zless file.txt.gz                # Page through

# Compress all files in directory
gzip *.log

# Compression level (1=fastest, 9=best)
gzip -1 file.txt                 # Fast
gzip -9 file.txt                 # Best compression
\`\`\`

#### Bzip2
\`\`\`bash
# Compress
bzip2 file.txt                   # Creates file.txt.bz2
bzip2 -k file.txt                # Keep original

# Decompress
bunzip2 file.txt.bz2
bzip2 -d file.txt.bz2

# View
bzcat file.txt.bz2
bzgrep "pattern" file.log.bz2
\`\`\`

#### Xz
\`\`\`bash
# Compress (best ratio for long-term archives)
xz file.txt                      # Creates file.txt.xz
xz -k file.txt                   # Keep original
xz -9 file.txt                   # Max compression (very slow!)

# Decompress
unxz file.txt.xz
xz -d file.txt.xz

# View
xzcat file.txt.xz
xzgrep "pattern" file.log.xz

# Multi-threaded compression (faster!)
xz -T 4 file.txt                 # Use 4 CPU cores
\`\`\`

#### Modern: Zstd (Recommended)
\`\`\`bash
# Zstandard — fast + good compression (used by Facebook)
zstd file.txt                    # Creates file.txt.zst
zstd -k file.txt                 # Keep original
zstd -19 file.txt                # Max level (1-19)

# Decompress
unzstd file.txt.zst
zstd -d file.txt.zst

# View
zstdcat file.txt.zst

# Multi-threaded
zstd -T4 file.txt
\`\`\`

### Part 4: Production Backup Strategies

#### Strategy 1: Full Daily Backup
\`\`\`bash
#!/bin/bash
# Simple daily backup of /etc and /home

DATE=$(date +%Y-%m-%d)
BACKUP_DIR="/backup/daily"
LOG_FILE="/var/log/backup.log"

mkdir -p $BACKUP_DIR

echo "[$(date)] Starting backup..." | tee -a $LOG_FILE

# Backup /etc
tar -czf $BACKUP_DIR/etc-$DATE.tar.gz /etc 2>&1 | tee -a $LOG_FILE

# Backup /home (exclude cache)
tar -czf $BACKUP_DIR/home-$DATE.tar.gz \\
    --exclude='*/. cache' \\
    --exclude='*/.local/share/Trash' \\
    --exclude='*/Downloads' \\
    /home 2>&1 | tee -a $LOG_FILE

# Retention: keep 7 days
find $BACKUP_DIR -name "*.tar.gz" -mtime +7 -delete

echo "[$(date)] Backup completed" | tee -a $LOG_FILE
\`\`\`

#### Strategy 2: Incremental Backup (Full + Incrementals)
\`\`\`bash
#!/bin/bash
# Full backup on Sunday, incremental Mon-Sat

DATE=$(date +%Y-%m-%d)
DAY=$(date +%u)  # 1=Monday, 7=Sunday
BACKUP_DIR="/backup/incremental"
SNAR_FILE="$BACKUP_DIR/backup.snar"

mkdir -p $BACKUP_DIR

if [ $DAY -eq 7 ]; then
    # Sunday: Full backup
    rm -f $SNAR_FILE  # Reset incremental state
    tar -czgvf $BACKUP_DIR/full-$DATE.tar.gz \\
        --listed-incremental=$SNAR_FILE \\
        /var/www
    echo "Full backup created"
else
    # Mon-Sat: Incremental
    tar -czgvf $BACKUP_DIR/incr-$DATE.tar.gz \\
        --listed-incremental=$SNAR_FILE \\
        /var/www
    echo "Incremental backup created"
fi
\`\`\`

#### Strategy 3: 3-2-1 Backup Rule
\`\`\`bash
# 3 copies, 2 different media, 1 offsite

#!/bin/bash
DATE=$(date +%Y-%m-%d)

# Copy 1: Local disk
tar -czf /backup/local/data-$DATE.tar.gz /data

# Copy 2: External USB (different media)
tar -czf /mnt/usb/data-$DATE.tar.gz /data

# Copy 3: Remote server (offsite)
tar -czf - /data | ssh backup-server "cat > /backup/data-$DATE.tar.gz"

# Or use rsync for incremental offsite backup
rsync -avz --delete /data/ backup-server:/backup/data/
\`\`\`

### Part 5: Real Production Scenarios

#### Scenario 1: Disaster Recovery — Restore Full System
\`\`\`bash
# Backup entire system (excluding volatile dirs)
tar -czpvf /backup/system-full-$(date +%Y%m%d).tar.gz \\
    --exclude=/proc \\
    --exclude=/sys \\
    --exclude=/dev \\
    --exclude=/tmp \\
    --exclude=/run \\
    --exclude=/mnt \\
    --exclude=/media \\
    --exclude=/backup \\
    /

# Restore (boot from live USB first!)
cd /mnt/recovery
tar -xzpvf /backup/system-full-20260130.tar.gz -C /

# Recreate missing directories
mkdir -p /mnt/recovery/{proc,sys,dev,tmp,run,mnt,media}
\`\`\`

#### Scenario 2: Database Backup
\`\`\`bash
# MySQL/MariaDB
mysqldump -u root -p --all-databases | gzip > /backup/mysql-$(date +%Y%m%d).sql.gz

# PostgreSQL
pg_dumpall -U postgres | gzip > /backup/postgres-$(date +%Y%m%d).sql.gz

# MongoDB
mongodump --gzip --archive=/backup/mongo-$(date +%Y%m%d).gz
\`\`\`

#### Scenario 3: Log Archival
\`\`\`bash
# Archive old logs monthly
find /var/log -name "*.log" -mtime +30 -print0 | \\
    tar -czf /archive/logs-$(date +%Y%m).tar.gz --null -T -

# Or per-application
tar -czf /archive/nginx-logs-$(date +%Y%m).tar.gz /var/log/nginx/*.log.gz
find /var/log/nginx -name "*.log.gz" -mtime +30 -delete
\`\`\`

### Part 6: Backup Verification

\`\`\`bash
# ALWAYS verify backups!

# Test 1: Check archive integrity
tar -tzf backup.tar.gz > /dev/null
echo $?  # Should be 0

# Test 2: Compare with source
tar -dzf backup.tar.gz -C /path/to/source

# Test 3: Extract to test location
mkdir -p /tmp/restore-test
tar -xzf backup.tar.gz -C /tmp/restore-test
diff -r /original /tmp/restore-test

# Test 4: Automated verification script
#!/bin/bash
BACKUP_FILE="/backup/latest.tar.gz"

if tar -tzf $BACKUP_FILE > /dev/null 2>&1; then
    echo "✅ Backup integrity OK"
    # Test restore
    RESTORE_DIR=$(mktemp -d)
    tar -xzf $BACKUP_FILE -C $RESTORE_DIR
    if [ $? -eq 0 ]; then
        echo "✅ Restore test OK"
        rm -rf $RESTORE_DIR
    else
        echo "❌ Restore failed!"
        exit 1
    fi
else
    echo "❌ Backup corrupted!"
    exit 1
fi
\`\`\`

### Part 7: Encryption

\`\`\`bash
# Encrypt backup with gpg
tar -czf - /data | gpg -c -o /backup/data.tar.gz.gpg

# Decrypt and extract
gpg -d /backup/data.tar.gz.gpg | tar -xzf -

# Encrypt with openssl
tar -czf - /data | openssl enc -aes-256-cbc -e -out /backup/data.tar.gz.enc

# Decrypt
openssl enc -aes-256-cbc -d -in /backup/data.tar.gz.enc | tar -xzf -
\`\`\`

### Common Mistakes to Avoid

\`\`\`bash
# ❌ WRONG: Backing up to same disk
tar -czf /data/backup.tar.gz /data     # If /data fails, backup gone!

# ✅ CORRECT: Backup to different disk/location
tar -czf /backup/data.tar.gz /data

# ❌ WRONG: No verification
tar -czf backup.tar.gz /data
# What if it's corrupted?

# ✅ CORRECT: Always verify
tar -czf backup.tar.gz /data && tar -tzf backup.tar.gz > /dev/null

# ❌ WRONG: No retention policy
# Disk fills up with old backups

# ✅ CORRECT: Delete old backups
find /backup -name "*.tar.gz" -mtime +30 -delete

# ❌ WRONG: Running out of space mid-backup
tar -czf /small-partition/huge-backup.tar.gz /huge-data

# ✅ CORRECT: Check space first
df -h /backup
du -sh /data
\`\`\`

### Quick Reference Card

\`\`\`bash
# TAR
tar -czvf archive.tar.gz /path       # Create gzip archive
tar -xzvf archive.tar.gz             # Extract gzip archive
tar -xzvf archive.tar.gz -C /dest    # Extract to /dest
tar -tzvf archive.tar.gz             # List contents
tar -czvf backup.tar.gz --exclude='*.log' /data

# COMPRESS
gzip file                            # file → file.gz
gunzip file.gz                       # file.gz → file
bzip2 file                           # Better compression
xz file                              # Best compression

# VIEW COMPRESSED
zcat file.gz                         # View gzip
bzcat file.bz2                       # View bzip2
xzcat file.xz                        # View xz

# COMMON PATTERNS
tar -czf /backup/etc-$(date +%Y%m%d).tar.gz /etc
tar -czf - /data | ssh remote "cat > backup.tar.gz"
tar -czf backup.tar.gz --exclude='node_modules' /project
\`\`\`

> **LFCS Tip:** You WILL be asked to create archives in the exam. Practice: \`tar -czvf\` (create), \`tar -xzvf\` (extract), \`tar -tzvf\` (list). Remember: "c x t" + "z" (gzip) + "v" (verbose) + "f" (file)!

### Best Practices

1. **Test restores regularly** — backup is only good if restore works
2. **3-2-1 Rule** — 3 copies, 2 media types, 1 offsite
3. **Automate** — use cron for daily/weekly backups
4. **Monitor** — alert if backup fails
5. **Encrypt** — for sensitive data
6. **Document** — write restore procedures
        `,
