      {
        id: 1,
        title: "User Management Fundamentals",
        type: "theory",
        duration: "45 min",
        content: `
## Linux User Management — Production Grade

### Part 1: Core Concepts

#### Key Files

| File | Purpose | Example | Permissions |
|------|---------|---------|-------------|
| \`/etc/passwd\` | User accounts | \`admin:x:1000:1000:Admin User:/home/admin:/bin/bash\` | 644 (world-readable) |
| \`/etc/shadow\` | Password hashes | \`admin:$6$salt$hash:19000:0:99999:7:::\` | 000 (root only) |
| \`/etc/group\` | Groups | \`developers:x:1001:admin,john\` | 644 |
| \`/etc/gshadow\` | Group passwords | Rarely used | 000 |
| \`/etc/login.defs\` | Default policies | UID/GID ranges, password aging | 644 |
| \`/etc/default/useradd\` | useradd defaults | Home dir, shell, expiry | 600 |

#### /etc/passwd Format (7 fields)

\`\`\`
username:password:UID:GID:GECOS:home_dir:shell
  │         │      │   │    │       │        │
  │         │      │   │    │       │        └─ Login shell (/bin/bash, /bin/zsh, /sbin/nologin)
  │         │      │   │    │       └─ Home directory path
  │         │      │   │    └─ Comment field (full name, phone, room, etc.)
  │         │      │   └─ Primary Group ID (default group)
  │         │      └─ User ID (unique number)
  │         └─ x = password stored in /etc/shadow (for security)
  └─ Username (1-32 characters, lowercase preferred)
\`\`\`

**Example:**
\`\`\`bash
john:x:1001:1001:John Developer,Room 404,ext 5555:/home/john:/bin/bash
\`\`\`

#### /etc/shadow Format (9 fields)

\`\`\`
username:password:lastchg:min:max:warn:inactive:expire:reserved
\`\`\`

| Field | Meaning | Example |
|-------|---------|---------|
| username | Login name | john |
| password | Hashed password | \`$6$salt$hash\` ($6=SHA-512) |
| lastchg | Days since 1970 when password was last changed | 19800 |
| min | Min days before password can be changed | 0 |
| max | Max days password is valid | 90 |
| warn | Days before expiry to warn user | 7 |
| inactive | Days after expiry before account disabled | 30 |
| expire | Account expiration date (days since 1970) | 20000 |
| reserved | For future use | (empty) |

**Example:**
\`\`\`bash
john:$6$rounds=5000$salt$hash:19800:0:90:7:30::
     └─ SHA-512 hash            └─ Last changed  │  │  │  └─ Inactive days
                                                  │  │  └─ Warn days
                                                  │  └─ Max age
                                                  └─ Min age
\`\`\`

### Part 2: User Creation & Management

#### Creating Users — Best Practices

\`\`\`bash
# 1. Basic user creation
useradd -m -s /bin/bash -c "John Developer" john
#       │  │             │                   └─ username
#       │  │             └─ GECOS comment
#       │  └─ login shell
#       └─ create home directory

# 2. Production user with all options
useradd -m -s /bin/bash -c "Jane DevOps" \\
        -G developers,docker,wheel \\
        -e 2026-12-31 \\
        -f 30 \\
        -u 2001 \\
        jane
#       └─ secondary groups
#          └─ account expiry date (YYYY-MM-DD)
#             └─ days after password expiry to disable account
#                └─ custom UID

# 3. Service account (no login)
useradd -r -s /sbin/nologin -M -c "Nginx Web Server" nginx
#       │  │                │
#       │  │                └─ do NOT create home directory
#       │  └─ no shell (no interactive login)
#       └─ system account (UID < 1000)

# 4. Temporary contractor account
useradd -m -s /bin/bash -c "Bob Contractor" \\
        -e $(date -d "+90 days" +%Y-%m-%d) \\
        -f 0 \\
        contractor_bob
# Auto-expires in 90 days, disabled immediately after password expiry

# 5. Create user with specific UID/GID (multi-server consistency)
useradd -m -u 5000 -g 5000 -s /bin/bash appuser
# Ensures same UID/GID across servers → NFS shares work correctly
\`\`\`

#### User Modification

\`\`\`bash
# Add to group (APPEND mode — critical!)
usermod -aG sudo john
usermod -aG developers,docker,kubernetes john

# ⚠️ WITHOUT -a flag, replaces ALL groups!
usermod -G sudo john  # DANGER: removes from other groups!

# Change shell
usermod -s /bin/zsh john
usermod -s /sbin/nologin service_account  # Disable interactive login

# Lock/Unlock account
usermod -L john   # Lock (adds ! to password hash)
usermod -U john   # Unlock

# Change home directory
usermod -d /new/home -m john  # -m = move files

# Change username
usermod -l newname oldname

# Set account expiry
usermod -e 2026-12-31 john
usermod -e "" john  # Remove expiry

# Change UID (dangerous — can break file ownership!)
usermod -u 2000 john
find / -user 1001 -exec chown 2000 {} \\;  # Fix orphaned files
\`\`\`

#### User Deletion — Safe Method

\`\`\`bash
# ⚠️ Production workflow for user deletion

# Step 1: Lock account first
usermod -L john
usermod -s /sbin/nologin john

# Step 2: Kill user processes
pkill -u john
pkill -9 -u john  # Force kill if needed

# Step 3: Backup home directory
tar -czf /backups/john-$(date +%Y%m%d).tar.gz /home/john

# Step 4: Find all user files
find / -user john -ls > /tmp/john-files.txt 2>/dev/null

# Step 5: Delete user
userdel -r john  # -r = remove home & mail spool

# Step 6: Check for orphaned files (UID with no username)
find / -uid 1001 -ls 2>/dev/null
# If found: chown to another user or delete
\`\`\`

### Part 3: Password Management

#### Setting Passwords

\`\`\`bash
# Interactive (prompts for password)
passwd john

# Non-interactive (scripting)
echo "john:P@ssw0rd123" | chpasswd

# Generate random password
openssl rand -base64 12 | passwd --stdin john

# Lock/unlock password
passwd -l john  # Lock
passwd -u john  # Unlock
passwd -S john  # Status

# Force password change on next login
passwd -e john
chage -d 0 john  # Same effect
\`\`\`

#### Password Aging Policies

\`\`\`bash
# View current settings
chage -l john

# Set password aging
chage -M 90 john    # Max 90 days
chage -m 7 john     # Min 7 days (prevent rapid changes)
chage -W 14 john    # Warn 14 days before expiry
chage -I 30 john    # Inactive 30 days after expiry
chage -E 2026-12-31 john  # Account expiry date

# Interactive configuration
chage john

# Apply policy to all users
for user in $(getent passwd | awk -F: '$3 >= 1000 && $3 < 65534 {print $1}'); do
  chage -M 90 -m 7 -W 14 -I 30 "$user"
done
\`\`\`

#### System-wide Password Policy

\`\`\`bash
# Edit /etc/login.defs
PASS_MAX_DAYS   90
PASS_MIN_DAYS   7
PASS_MIN_LEN    12
PASS_WARN_AGE   14

# Enforce password complexity (install libpam-pwquality)
# Edit /etc/security/pwquality.conf
minlen = 12
dcredit = -1   # At least 1 digit
ucredit = -1   # At least 1 uppercase
lcredit = -1   # At least 1 lowercase
ocredit = -1   # At least 1 special char
maxrepeat = 2  # Max 2 repeated characters
\`\`\`

### Part 4: Group Management

\`\`\`bash
# Create group
groupadd developers
groupadd -g 3000 custom_gid_group

# Modify group
groupmod -n newname oldname
groupmod -g 4000 developers

# Delete group
groupdel developers

# Add user to group
gpasswd -a john developers   # Alternative to usermod -aG

# Remove user from group
gpasswd -d john developers

# List group members
getent group developers
# OR
grep ^developers: /etc/group | cut -d: -f4

# List all groups a user belongs to
groups john
id john
\`\`\`

### Part 5: Production Scenarios

#### Scenario 1: Onboarding New Developer

\`\`\`bash
#!/bin/bash
# onboard-developer.sh

USERNAME="$1"
FULLNAME="$2"

# Create user
useradd -m -s /bin/bash -c "$FULLNAME" "$USERNAME"

# Set temporary password (force change)
echo "$USERNAME:ChangeMe123!" | chpasswd
passwd -e "$USERNAME"

# Add to development groups
usermod -aG developers,docker,sudo "$USERNAME"

# Create SSH directory
mkdir -p /home/$USERNAME/.ssh
chmod 700 /home/$USERNAME/.ssh

# Set password policy
chage -M 90 -m 7 -W 14 -I 30 "$USERNAME"

# Send welcome email
echo "Account created. Login: $USERNAME, Password: ChangeMe123! (expires on first login)" | \\
  mail -s "Welcome to DevTeam" "$USERNAME@company.com"
\`\`\`

#### Scenario 2: Audit Inactive Accounts

\`\`\`bash
# Find users who haven't logged in for 90+ days
lastlog | awk '$4 ~ /^Never/ || $4 == "" || (systime() - mktime($6 " " $5 " " $9 " 00 00 00")) > 90*86400 {print $1}'

# Find locked accounts
passwd -S -a | grep " L "

# Find accounts with no password set
passwd -S -a | grep " NP "

# Find accounts expiring soon (next 30 days)
awk -F: -v now=$(date +%s) -v warn=$((30*86400)) \\
  '{exp=$8*86400; if (exp>0 && exp-now<warn && exp-now>0) print $1, (exp-now)/86400 " days"}' /etc/shadow
\`\`\`

#### Scenario 3: Bulk User Creation from CSV

\`\`\`bash
# users.csv format:
# username,fullname,groups,expiry_date
# alice,Alice Admin,"sudo,developers",2026-12-31
# bob,Bob Developer,"developers,docker",2026-12-31

while IFS=, read -r username fullname groups expiry; do
  useradd -m -s /bin/bash -c "$fullname" -e "$expiry" "$username"
  echo "$username:TempPass123!" | chpasswd
  passwd -e "$username"

  # Add to groups
  IFS=',' read -ra GROUPS <<< "$groups"
  for group in "\${GROUPS[@]}"; do
    usermod -aG "$group" "$username"
  done
done < users.csv
\`\`\`

#### Scenario 4: Troubleshooting "Permission Denied" Login

\`\`\`bash
# User can't login — debugging steps

# 1. Check account status
passwd -S john
# Output: "john LK ..." → Locked!

# 2. Check expiry
chage -l john
# Account expires: Jan 30, 2026 (past date!)

# 3. Check shell
grep ^john: /etc/passwd
# Shell: /sbin/nologin → Can't login interactively!

# 4. Check home directory
ls -ld /home/john
# drwx------ root root → Wrong owner!
chown -R john:john /home/john

# 5. Check password expiry
chage -l john
# Password expires: Jan 01, 2026 → Expired!
chage -M 999999 john  # Extend expiry
\`\`\`

### Part 6: System vs Service Accounts

| Type | UID Range | Shell | Home | Purpose | Example |
|------|-----------|-------|------|---------|---------|
| Root | 0 | /bin/bash | /root | Superuser | root |
| System | 1-999 | /sbin/nologin | /var/lib/service | Daemons | nginx, mysql, www-data |
| Regular | 1000-60000 | /bin/bash | /home/user | Humans | john, admin |
| Nobody | 65534 | /sbin/nologin | /nonexistent | Unprivileged tasks | nobody |

\`\`\`bash
# List system accounts
awk -F: '$3 < 1000 {print $1}' /etc/passwd

# List regular users
awk -F: '$3 >= 1000 && $3 < 65534 {print $1}' /etc/passwd

# Count users
wc -l /etc/passwd
\`\`\`

### Part 7: Common Mistakes & Best Practices

#### ❌ Common Mistakes

\`\`\`bash
# 1. Forgetting -a flag (replaces all groups!)
usermod -G docker john  # ⚠️ Removes from sudo, developers, etc.
usermod -aG docker john # ✅ Appends to existing groups

# 2. Deleting user without backup
userdel -r john  # ⚠️ PERMANENT deletion!

# 3. Using weak passwords
echo "john:123456" | chpasswd  # ⚠️ Dictionary attack!

# 4. Not checking for running processes
userdel -r john  # ⚠️ User still logged in → errors!
pkill -u john && userdel -r john  # ✅

# 5. Creating user without home directory
useradd john  # ⚠️ No -m flag → no /home/john
useradd -m john  # ✅
\`\`\`

#### ✅ Best Practices

1. **Always use \`-m\`** when creating users → home directory creation
2. **Always use \`-a\`** with \`usermod -G\` → append, don't replace
3. **Lock before delete** → prevent active sessions
4. **Backup home directories** → never lose data
5. **Use \`chage\`** → enforce password policies
6. **Service accounts**: \`-r -s /sbin/nologin -M\`
7. **Use \`visudo\`** → never edit \`/etc/sudoers\` directly

### Part 8: Quick Reference

\`\`\`bash
# User info
id john                    # UID, GID, groups
finger john                # Full user info
last john                  # Login history
lastlog                    # All users' last login
w                          # Currently logged in

# Bulk operations
getent passwd              # All users (including LDAP/NIS)
getent group               # All groups
compgen -u                 # All usernames
cut -d: -f1 /etc/passwd    # All usernames (local only)

# Password hashing algorithms
$1$ → MD5 (weak, avoid)
$5$ → SHA-256
$6$ → SHA-512 (recommended)
$y$ → yescrypt (modern, Ubuntu 22.04+)
\`\`\`

### Part 9: Real Production Example — AWS EC2

\`\`\`bash
# Standard AWS EC2 setup script
#!/bin/bash

# 1. Create admin user
useradd -m -s /bin/bash -c "Admin User" admin
echo "admin:$(openssl rand -base64 12)" | chpasswd
passwd -e admin
usermod -aG sudo admin

# 2. Create application user
useradd -r -s /sbin/nologin -M -c "Web Application" webapp
usermod -aG www-data webapp

# 3. SSH key setup
mkdir -p /home/admin/.ssh
curl -s https://github.com/adminuser.keys >> /home/admin/.ssh/authorized_keys
chmod 700 /home/admin/.ssh
chmod 600 /home/admin/.ssh/authorized_keys
chown -R admin:admin /home/admin/.ssh

# 4. Disable root SSH
sed -i 's/^PermitRootLogin yes/PermitRootLogin no/' /etc/ssh/sshd_config
systemctl reload sshd
\`\`\`
        `,
        keyPoints: [
          "useradd -m — home directory yaratadi (majburiy!)",
          "usermod -aG — append mode (muhim: -a flag!)",
          "/etc/shadow — password hashes (root-only 000 permissions)",
          "chage — password aging policies",
          "Service accounts: -r -s /sbin/nologin -M",
          "Lock before delete: usermod -L, pkill -u, backup",
          "Bulk operations: getent, awk, scripting"
        ]
      },
      {
        id: 2,
        title: "File Permissions Deep Dive",
        type: "theory",
        duration: "50 min",
        content: `
## Linux File Permissions — Mastery Level

### Part 1: Understanding Permission Bits

#### File Listing Anatomy

\`\`\`bash
$ ls -l script.sh
-rwxrw-r-- 1 admin developers 4096 Jan 30 10:00 script.sh
│└┬┘└┬┘└┬┘ │ │     │          │    │           └─ filename
││ │ │ │ │ │ │     │          │    └─ modification time
││ │ │ │ │ │ │     │          └─ size in bytes
││ │ │ │ │ │ │     └─ group owner
││ │ │ │ │ │ └─ user owner
││ │ │ │ │ └─ number of hard links
││ │ │ │ └─ others permissions (r--)
││ │ │ └─ group permissions (rw-)
││ │ └─ user/owner permissions (rwx)
│└─ file type
│
└─ File types:
   - = regular file
   d = directory
   l = symbolic link
   c = character device
   b = block device
   s = socket
   p = named pipe (FIFO)
\`\`\`

#### Permission Values — Bit Calculation

| Permission | Letter | Octal | Binary | On File | On Directory |
|------------|--------|-------|--------|---------|--------------|
| Read | r | 4 | 100 | View content | List files (ls) |
| Write | w | 2 | 010 | Modify content | Create/delete files |
| Execute | x | 1 | 001 | Run as program | Enter directory (cd) |
| None | - | 0 | 000 | No access | No access |

**Combining permissions:**
\`\`\`
rwx = 4 + 2 + 1 = 7
rw- = 4 + 2 + 0 = 6
r-x = 4 + 0 + 1 = 5
r-- = 4 + 0 + 0 = 4
--- = 0 + 0 + 0 = 0
\`\`\`

#### Directory Permissions — Critical Understanding

\`\`\`bash
# Execute permission on directory = access/traverse permission

mkdir /test
chmod 644 /test  # rw-r--r-- (no execute!)

cd /test         # ❌ Permission denied!
ls /test         # ✅ Works (read permission)
touch /test/file # ❌ Permission denied (no execute → can't access)

chmod 755 /test  # rwxr-xr-x
cd /test         # ✅ Works
ls /test         # ✅ Works
touch /test/file # ✅ Works (owner has write)
\`\`\`

**Directory permission combinations:**

| Perms | ls | cd | Create | Delete | Read file | Modify file |
|-------|----|----|--------|--------|-----------|-------------|
| r-- | ✅ | ❌ | ❌ | ❌ | ❌ | ❌ |
| --x | ❌ | ✅ | ❌ | ❌ | ✅* | ✅* |
| r-x | ✅ | ✅ | ❌ | ❌ | ✅ | ✅ |
| -wx | ❌ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅* | ✅* |
| rwx | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ |

*If you know the filename

### Part 2: Chmod — Two Modes

#### Symbolic Mode (Human-Readable)

\`\`\`bash
# Syntax: chmod [who][operator][permissions] file
# who: u (user), g (group), o (others), a (all)
# operator: + (add), - (remove), = (set exactly)
# permissions: r, w, x

# Add execute for user
chmod u+x script.sh

# Remove write from group
chmod g-w file.txt

# Set others to read only (remove everything else)
chmod o=r file.txt

# Add read for all (user, group, others)
chmod a+r file.txt

# Complex: user=rwx, group=rx, others=none
chmod u=rwx,g=rx,o= file.sh

# Multiple operations
chmod u+x,g-w,o-r file.txt

# Recursive
chmod -R u+rwX,go+rX,go-w /shared/docs
# Note: Capital X = add execute only to directories and already-executable files
\`\`\`

#### Octal Mode (Fast & Precise)

\`\`\`bash
# Format: chmod [special][user][group][others] file

# Common patterns
chmod 755 script.sh      # rwxr-xr-x — scripts
chmod 644 file.txt       # rw-r--r-- — readable files
chmod 600 secret.key     # rw------- — private files (SSH keys)
chmod 700 ~/.ssh         # rwx------ — private directory
chmod 775 /shared/dir    # rwxrwxr-x — shared directory
chmod 666 /tmp/temp.txt  # rw-rw-rw- — world-writable file (dangerous!)
chmod 777 /tmp/public    # rwxrwxrwx — world-writable dir (very dangerous!)

# Recursive
chmod -R 755 /var/www/html
\`\`\`

### Part 3: Special Permissions (SUID, SGID, Sticky Bit)

#### SUID — Set User ID (octal: 4000)

\`\`\`bash
# Execute file as file OWNER (not as current user)

# Example: /usr/bin/passwd
ls -l /usr/bin/passwd
# -rwsr-xr-x root root → notice 's' instead of 'x'
#    ^
# When user 'john' runs passwd:
# - Effective UID = 0 (root)
# - Can modify /etc/shadow (only root can write)

# Set SUID
chmod u+s /path/to/binary
chmod 4755 /path/to/binary  # Same thing

# Security risk: find SUID binaries
find / -perm -4000 -type f 2>/dev/null

# Legitimate SUID binaries:
# /usr/bin/passwd, /usr/bin/sudo, /usr/bin/su, /usr/bin/mount
# Suspicious: anything in /tmp, /home, /var/www
\`\`\`

#### SGID — Set Group ID (octal: 2000)

**On files:**
\`\`\`bash
# Execute as file GROUP
chmod g+s /path/to/binary
chmod 2755 /path/to/binary
\`\`\`

**On directories (more common):**
\`\`\`bash
# New files inherit directory GROUP (not creator's group)

mkdir /shared/project
chown :developers /shared/project
chmod 2775 /shared/project  # drwxrwsr-x
#                                   ^

# Test
sudo -u alice touch /shared/project/file1.txt
ls -l /shared/project/file1.txt
# -rw-r--r-- alice developers file1.txt
#                  └─ inherited from directory, not alice's primary group!
\`\`\`

#### Sticky Bit (octal: 1000)

\`\`\`bash
# On directory: only file OWNER can delete their own files
# (even if directory is world-writable)

chmod +t /shared/temp
chmod 1777 /shared/temp  # drwxrwxrwt
#                                  ^

# Example: /tmp
ls -ld /tmp
# drwxrwxrwt 12 root root 4096 Jan 30 /tmp

# Test
sudo -u alice touch /tmp/alice-file.txt
sudo -u bob rm /tmp/alice-file.txt
# ❌ rm: cannot remove '/tmp/alice-file.txt': Operation not permitted

# Without sticky bit, bob COULD delete alice's file (directory is world-writable)
\`\`\`

#### Combined Special Permissions

\`\`\`bash
# All three: SUID + SGID + Sticky
chmod 7755 file  # -rwsr-sr-t

# SUID + SGID
chmod 6755 file  # -rwsr-sr-x

# SGID + Sticky (common for shared directories)
chmod 3775 /shared/team  # drwxrwsr-t
\`\`\`

### Part 4: Ownership Management

\`\`\`bash
# Change owner
chown john file.txt
chown john:developers file.txt  # owner + group
chown :developers file.txt      # only group

# Recursive
chown -R john:developers /home/john

# Preserve symlinks (don't follow)
chown -h john symlink

# Change group only
chgrp developers file.txt
chgrp -R developers /shared/project

# Copy ownership from another file
chown --reference=/source/file /dest/file
\`\`\`

### Part 5: Umask — Default Permissions

\`\`\`bash
# Umask = permissions to REMOVE from defaults
# Default file: 666 (rw-rw-rw-)
# Default dir:  777 (rwxrwxrwx)

# View current umask
umask
# 0022

# Calculation
#   File default: 666
#   Umask:       -022
#   ────────────────
#   Result:       644 (rw-r--r--)
#
#   Dir default:  777
#   Umask:       -022
#   ────────────────
#   Result:       755 (rwxr-xr-x)

# Common umask values
umask 022  # Files: 644, Dirs: 755 (default on most systems)
umask 027  # Files: 640, Dirs: 750 (group-readable, no others access)
umask 077  # Files: 600, Dirs: 700 (private, no group/others access)
umask 002  # Files: 664, Dirs: 775 (shared, group-writable)

# Set umask permanently
echo "umask 027" >> ~/.bashrc

# System-wide
echo "umask 027" >> /etc/profile
\`\`\`

### Part 6: Production Scenarios

#### Scenario 1: Web Server Document Root

\`\`\`bash
# nginx/apache serving /var/www/html

# Requirements:
# - Web server (user: www-data) can READ all files
# - Web server can EXECUTE (traverse) all directories
# - Developers (group: developers) can write files
# - Others: read-only

mkdir -p /var/www/html
chown -R www-data:developers /var/www/html
chmod 2775 /var/www/html  # SGID → new files inherit 'developers' group
find /var/www/html -type d -exec chmod 2775 {} \\;
find /var/www/html -type f -exec chmod 664 {} \\;

# Make scripts executable
find /var/www/html -type f -name "*.sh" -exec chmod 775 {} \\;
\`\`\`

#### Scenario 2: Shared Project Directory

\`\`\`bash
# Team: developers group
# Requirements:
# - All team members can read/write/execute
# - Others: no access
# - New files automatically belong to developers group

mkdir /projects/alpha
chown root:developers /projects/alpha
chmod 2770 /projects/alpha  # rwxrws--- (SGID + no others access)

# Set default ACL for finer control
setfacl -d -m g:developers:rwx /projects/alpha
setfacl -d -m o::--- /projects/alpha
\`\`\`

#### Scenario 3: Secure SSH Key Permissions

\`\`\`bash
# SSH is VERY strict about key permissions

# Private key (id_rsa)
chmod 600 ~/.ssh/id_rsa  # rw------- (MUST be 600 or 400)
# SSH refuses to use keys with wrong permissions!

# Public key
chmod 644 ~/.ssh/id_rsa.pub  # rw-r--r--

# authorized_keys
chmod 600 ~/.ssh/authorized_keys

# .ssh directory
chmod 700 ~/.ssh  # rwx------

# Full setup
mkdir -p ~/.ssh
chmod 700 ~/.ssh
ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C "user@example.com"
chmod 600 ~/.ssh/id_ed25519
chmod 644 ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub
\`\`\`

#### Scenario 4: Security Audit — Find Dangerous Permissions

\`\`\`bash
# 1. Find SUID/SGID files
find / -type f \\( -perm -4000 -o -perm -2000 \\) -exec ls -ldb {} \\; 2>/dev/null

# 2. Find world-writable files (security risk!)
find / -type f -perm -0002 ! -path "/proc/*" ! -path "/sys/*" 2>/dev/null

# 3. Find world-writable directories WITHOUT sticky bit (very dangerous!)
find / -type d -perm -0002 ! -perm -1000 2>/dev/null

# 4. Find files with no owner (orphaned files)
find / -nouser -o -nogroup 2>/dev/null

# 5. Find overly permissive files in sensitive locations
find /etc /root /home -type f -perm -0006 2>/dev/null

# 6. Find scripts with SUID (very dangerous!)
find / -type f -perm -4000 -name "*.sh" 2>/dev/null
\`\`\`

#### Scenario 5: Fixing Common Permission Issues

\`\`\`bash
# Problem 1: "Permission denied" when running script
chmod +x script.sh

# Problem 2: Can't delete file in directory (sticky bit)
ls -ld /shared/dir  # drwxrwxrwt → sticky bit set
# Solution: become file owner or directory owner

# Problem 3: Can't create files in directory
ls -ld /projects
# drwxr-xr-x → group/others have no write permission
chmod g+w /projects  # Add write for group

# Problem 4: Wrong umask → files created with wrong permissions
umask 027  # Secure default

# Problem 5: SSH key rejected
chmod 600 ~/.ssh/id_rsa
chmod 700 ~/.ssh
\`\`\`

### Part 7: Advanced Permission Techniques

#### Recursive Permission Changes (Safe Method)

\`\`\`bash
# ❌ WRONG: chmod -R 755 /var/www (makes all files executable!)

# ✅ CORRECT:
# Directories: 755, Files: 644
find /var/www -type d -exec chmod 755 {} \\;
find /var/www -type f -exec chmod 644 {} \\;

# Make only .sh scripts executable
find /var/www -type f -name "*.sh" -exec chmod 755 {} \\;
\`\`\`

#### Preserving Permissions in Backups

\`\`\`bash
# tar preserves permissions by default
tar -czf backup.tar.gz /important/data

# rsync preserves permissions with -a
rsync -a /source/ /destination/

# cp with -p flag
cp -p source.txt dest.txt
\`\`\`

### Part 8: Quick Reference

#### Common Permission Patterns

| Use Case | Octal | Symbolic | Explanation |
|----------|-------|----------|-------------|
| Script/binary | 755 | rwxr-xr-x | Owner: full, Others: read+execute |
| Config file | 644 | rw-r--r-- | Owner: write, Others: read |
| Private key | 600 | rw------- | Owner-only access |
| Shared dir | 2775 | rwxrwsr-x | SGID, group-writable |
| Temp dir | 1777 | rwxrwxrwt | Sticky bit, world-writable |
| Private dir | 700 | rwx------ | Owner-only access |
| Log file | 640 | rw-r----- | Owner: write, Group: read |

#### Permission Checking Logic

\`\`\`bash
# 1. Are you root? → Full access (bypasses all checks)
# 2. Are you the owner? → Check user permissions (rwx)
# 3. Are you in the group? → Check group permissions (rwx)
# 4. None of the above? → Check others permissions (rwx)
\`\`\`

#### Capital X in chmod

\`\`\`bash
# lowercase x = add execute to ALL files
chmod -R a+x /dir  # ⚠️ Makes EVERY file executable (wrong!)

# uppercase X = add execute ONLY to directories and already-executable files
chmod -R a+X /dir  # ✅ Safe (only affects dirs and scripts)
\`\`\`
        `,
        keyPoints: [
          "r=4, w=2, x=1 (binary bits: 100, 010, 001)",
          "Directory execute = access/traverse permission (critical!)",
          "SUID=4000, SGID=2000, Sticky=1000",
          "Umask = permissions to SUBTRACT from defaults",
          "SGID on dir → new files inherit directory group",
          "Sticky bit → only owner can delete (used in /tmp)",
          "chmod -R a+X → safe recursive (only dirs get execute)",
          "SSH keys MUST be 600 or 400"
        ]
      },
