        title: "ACL — Access Control Lists",
        type: "theory",
        duration: "40 min",
        content: `
## ACL — Fine-Grained Permissions Beyond Unix Basics

### Part 1: Why ACL?

Standard Unix permissions cheklangan:
- **Faqat 3 kategoriya**: owner, group, others
- **Faqat 1 group** per file
- **Yetarli emas** ko'p userli muhitlarda

**Example problem:**
\`\`\`bash
# File: project-spec.pdf
# Requirement:
# - alice (owner): rwx
# - developers group: r-x
# - bob (not in developers): rw-
# - qa team: r--

# ❌ Standard permissions can't express this!
chmod 755 project-spec.pdf  # Doesn't give bob special access
\`\`\`

**Solution: ACL**
\`\`\`bash
# ✅ ACL can handle multiple users/groups with different permissions
setfacl -m u:alice:rwx,g:developers:rx,u:bob:rw,g:qa:r project-spec.pdf
\`\`\`

### Part 2: ACL Commands — Complete Reference

#### View ACL

\`\`\`bash
# View ACL
getfacl /path/to/file

# Example output:
# file: project-spec.pdf
# owner: alice
# group: developers
# user::rwx              ← owner (alice)
# user:bob:rw-           ← specific user ACL
# group::r-x             ← owning group (developers)
# group:qa:r--           ← specific group ACL
# mask::rwx              ← effective permission limit
# other::r--             ← others

# View multiple files
getfacl file1 file2 dir/

# Compact output (skip comments)
getfacl -c file

# Numeric UIDs/GIDs instead of names
getfacl -n file
\`\`\`

#### Set ACL

\`\`\`bash
# Set ACL for specific user
setfacl -m u:john:rwx /shared/file
setfacl -m user:john:rwx /shared/file  # Same thing

# Set ACL for specific group
setfacl -m g:developers:rx /shared/dir
setfacl -m group:developers:rx /shared/dir

# Multiple entries at once
setfacl -m u:alice:rwx,u:bob:rw,g:qa:r /shared/file

# Recursive
setfacl -R -m u:john:rx /shared/project

# Follow symlinks (-L) or don't (-P, default)
setfacl -RL -m u:john:rx /shared/project
\`\`\`

#### Remove ACL

\`\`\`bash
# Remove specific ACL entry
setfacl -x u:john /shared/file
setfacl -x g:qa /shared/file

# Remove ALL ACL entries (reset to standard permissions)
setfacl -b /shared/file

# Remove default ACL only (on directories)
setfacl -k /shared/dir
\`\`\`

### Part 3: Default ACL (Inheritance)

\`\`\`bash
# Default ACL = template for new files created in directory

# Set default ACL on directory
setfacl -d -m g:developers:rwx /projects/alpha
#       └─ -d = default

# Now, any NEW file created in /projects/alpha will have:
# - Standard permissions from umask
# - PLUS inherited ACL: g:developers:rwx

# Example:
touch /projects/alpha/newfile.txt
getfacl /projects/alpha/newfile.txt
# user::rw-
# group::rwx              ← inherited from default ACL!
# group:developers:rwx    ← inherited from default ACL!
# mask::rwx
# other::r--

# Set both current AND default ACL
setfacl -m g:developers:rwx /projects/alpha           # Current
setfacl -d -m g:developers:rwx /projects/alpha        # Default (inheritance)

# OR combine in one command
setfacl -R -m g:developers:rwx -d -m g:developers:rwx /projects/alpha
\`\`\`

### Part 4: ACL Mask — Effective Permissions

ACL Mask = **maksimal ruxsat** ACL uchun (owner va others dan tashqari).

\`\`\`bash
# Set user ACL
setfacl -m u:bob:rwx file.txt

# View ACL
getfacl file.txt
# user::rw-
# user:bob:rwx           ← Bob's ACL says "rwx"
# mask::rw-              ← BUT mask limits to "rw-"
# other::r--

# Bob's EFFECTIVE permission = bob:rwx AND mask:rw- = rw-
# (Intersection of ACL and mask)

# Set mask explicitly
setfacl -m m::rx file.txt
# Now bob's effective permission = bob:rwx AND mask:rx = r-x

# Recalculate mask automatically
setfacl --mask-auto file.txt
\`\`\`

**When is mask recalculated?**
- Automatically when you set ACL (unless you use \`-n\`)
- Mask = union of all group + user ACL permissions

### Part 5: Production Scenarios

#### Scenario 1: Multi-Team Project Directory

\`\`\`bash
# Requirement:
# - /projects/webapp
# - developers: full access (rwx)
# - qa: read + execute (rx)
# - manager (user): read-only (r)
# - devops group: full access (rwx)
# - All new files inherit these ACLs

mkdir -p /projects/webapp
chown root:developers /projects/webapp

# Set current ACL
setfacl -m g:developers:rwx /projects/webapp
setfacl -m g:qa:rx /projects/webapp
setfacl -m u:manager:r /projects/webapp
setfacl -m g:devops:rwx /projects/webapp

# Set default ACL (inheritance)
setfacl -d -m g:developers:rwx /projects/webapp
setfacl -d -m g:qa:rx /projects/webapp
setfacl -d -m u:manager:r /projects/webapp
setfacl -d -m g:devops:rwx /projects/webapp

# Verify
getfacl /projects/webapp

# Test inheritance
sudo -u alice touch /projects/webapp/test.txt
getfacl /projects/webapp/test.txt
# Should show inherited ACLs
\`\`\`

#### Scenario 2: Shared Logs Directory

\`\`\`bash
# /var/log/app-logs
# - app user (owner): write logs
# - developers: read logs
# - qa: read logs
# - security team: read logs

mkdir -p /var/log/app-logs
chown app:app /var/log/app-logs
chmod 700 /var/log/app-logs  # Start restrictive

# Add ACLs
setfacl -m g:developers:rx /var/log/app-logs
setfacl -m g:qa:rx /var/log/app-logs
setfacl -m g:security:rx /var/log/app-logs

# Default ACL for new log files
setfacl -d -m g:developers:r /var/log/app-logs
setfacl -d -m g:qa:r /var/log/app-logs
setfacl -d -m g:security:r /var/log/app-logs

# Test
sudo -u app touch /var/log/app-logs/app.log
ls -l /var/log/app-logs/app.log
# -rw-r-----+ app app app.log  ← notice '+' indicating ACL
\`\`\`

#### Scenario 3: Backup Before Changing ACL

\`\`\`bash
# Save ACL to file
getfacl -R /projects/alpha > /backup/alpha-acl-backup.txt

# Restore ACL later
setfacl --restore=/backup/alpha-acl-backup.txt

# OR use tar with ACL support
tar --acls -czf /backup/alpha.tar.gz /projects/alpha

# Restore with ACL
tar --acls -xzf /backup/alpha.tar.gz
\`\`\`

#### Scenario 4: Copy ACL from One File to Another

\`\`\`bash
# Method 1: Via backup/restore
getfacl source-file.txt | setfacl --set-file=- dest-file.txt

# Method 2: Manual
getfacl source-file.txt > /tmp/acl.txt
setfacl --set-file=/tmp/acl.txt dest-file.txt

# Copy ACL recursively
getfacl -R /source/dir | setfacl --restore=-
\`\`\`

### Part 6: Troubleshooting ACL

#### Problem 1: ACL Not Working

\`\`\`bash
# Check if filesystem supports ACL
mount | grep " / "
# Should show: ext4 rw,relatime,acl

# If no "acl" option, remount:
mount -o remount,acl /

# Make permanent (add to /etc/fstab):
# /dev/sda1  /  ext4  defaults,acl  0  1
\`\`\`

#### Problem 2: Identifying Files with ACL

\`\`\`bash
# ls -l shows '+' for ACL files
ls -l /shared/
# -rw-r--r--+ alice developers file.txt  ← '+' indicates ACL

# Find all files with ACL
find /shared -exec ls -ld {} \\; | grep "^[-d].*+"

# OR use getfacl
find /shared -type f -exec getfacl {} \\; 2>/dev/null | grep "^# file:"
\`\`\`

#### Problem 3: ACL Mask Limiting Permissions

\`\`\`bash
# User ACL says rwx, but effective is rw-

getfacl file.txt
# user:bob:rwx           ← ACL entry
# mask::rw-              ← mask limits to rw-!

# Fix: recalculate mask
setfacl -m m::rwx file.txt

# OR let setfacl auto-calculate
setfacl --mask-auto file.txt
\`\`\`

### Part 7: ACL vs Standard Permissions

| Feature | Standard Permissions | ACL |
|---------|---------------------|-----|
| Users per file | 1 (owner) | Unlimited |
| Groups per file | 1 | Unlimited |
| Syntax | Simple (\`chmod 755\`) | Complex (\`setfacl -m ...\`) |
| Portability | Universal | Requires ACL support |
| Backup | Any tool | Needs \`--acls\` flag |
| Performance | Faster | Slightly slower |
| ls output | Direct (rwxr-xr-x) | Shows '+' only |

**When to use ACL:**
- Multiple users need different permissions
- Multiple groups need access
- Complex permission requirements

**When NOT to use ACL:**
- Simple scenarios (standard permissions are enough)
- Legacy systems without ACL support
- Performance-critical paths

### Part 8: Advanced ACL Techniques

#### ACL with Mask Calculation

\`\`\`bash
# Prevent automatic mask recalculation (-n flag)
setfacl -n -m u:bob:rwx file.txt

# Manually set mask
setfacl -m m::r file.txt

# Now bob's effective permission = bob:rwx AND mask:r = r
\`\`\`

#### Remove All ACLs, Keep Standard Permissions

\`\`\`bash
# Method 1: setfacl -b
setfacl -b file.txt

# Method 2: Reapply standard permissions
chmod 644 file.txt
setfacl -b file.txt
\`\`\`

#### ACL on Symlinks

\`\`\`bash
# By default, ACL applies to TARGET of symlink
setfacl -m u:bob:rwx symlink  # Modifies target!

# To modify symlink itself (rare):
setfacl -h -m u:bob:rwx symlink
\`\`\`

### Part 9: Security Audit — Find ACL Files

\`\`\`bash
# Find all files with ACL in directory tree
find /projects -type f -exec ls -ld {} \\; 2>/dev/null | grep "^-.*+"

# Show ACL details for all ACL files
find /projects -type f | while read f; do
  if [[ $(getfacl -c "$f" 2>/dev/null | wc -l) -gt 3 ]]; then
    echo "=== $f ==="
    getfacl -c "$f"
  fi
done

# Remove ACL from all files (reset to standard)
find /projects -type f -exec setfacl -b {} \\;
\`\`\`

### Part 10: Quick Reference

\`\`\`bash
# View ACL
getfacl file

# Set user ACL
setfacl -m u:user:perms file

# Set group ACL
setfacl -m g:group:perms file

# Set default ACL (directory)
setfacl -d -m u:user:perms dir

# Remove ACL entry
setfacl -x u:user file

# Remove ALL ACL
setfacl -b file

# Recursive
setfacl -R -m u:user:perms dir

# Backup ACL
getfacl -R dir > backup.acl

# Restore ACL
setfacl --restore=backup.acl

# Check filesystem ACL support
mount | grep acl
tune2fs -l /dev/sda1 | grep acl
\`\`\`

### Part 11: Real-World Production Example

\`\`\`bash
#!/bin/bash
# setup-shared-project.sh

PROJECT="/projects/webapp"
OWNER="admin"
GROUP="developers"

# Create directory
mkdir -p "$PROJECT"
chown "$OWNER:$GROUP" "$PROJECT"
chmod 770 "$PROJECT"

# Current ACL
setfacl -m g:developers:rwx "$PROJECT"
setfacl -m g:qa:rx "$PROJECT"
setfacl -m g:devops:rwx "$PROJECT"
setfacl -m u:manager:r "$PROJECT"

# Default ACL (inheritance)
setfacl -d -m g:developers:rwx "$PROJECT"
setfacl -d -m g:qa:rx "$PROJECT"
setfacl -d -m g:devops:rwx "$PROJECT"
setfacl -d -m u:manager:r "$PROJECT"
setfacl -d -m o::--- "$PROJECT"  # No access for others

# Backup ACL
getfacl -R "$PROJECT" > "/backup/$(basename $PROJECT)-acl-$(date +%Y%m%d).txt"

echo "✅ ACL configured for $PROJECT"
getfacl "$PROJECT"
\`\`\`
        `,
        keyPoints: [
          "ACL = unlimited users/groups per file",
          "setfacl -m u:user:rwx — user ACL",
          "setfacl -d — default ACL (inheritance)",
          "+ in 'ls -l' — ACL mavjud",
          "Mask = ACL effective permission limiti",
          "getfacl backup → setfacl --restore",
          "Filesystem ACL support kerak (mount -o acl)",
          "tar --acls for ACL-aware backups"
        ]
      },
      {
        id: 5,
        title: "Sudo Configuration",
        type: "theory",
        duration: "45 min",
        content: `
## Sudo — Superuser Do (Enterprise Edition)

### Part 1: Sudo Basics

**Sudo allows:**
- Non-root users to run specific commands as root
- Fine-grained permission control
- Audit logging (who ran what)
- Time-limited authentication (default: 15 minutes)

**Why sudo instead of root login?**
1. ✅ **Accountability** — logs show who ran what
2. ✅ **Least privilege** — users get only needed permissions
3. ✅ **No shared passwords** — each user has own password
4. ✅ **Safer** — typos won't destroy system (you think before \`sudo rm -rf\`)

### Part 2: Sudoers File Format

\`\`\`bash
# ALWAYS edit with visudo (syntax checking!)
sudo visudo

# Format:
# who   where=(as_whom)  TAGS: what
user   host=(runas)      NOPASSWD: commands
\`\`\`

#### Syntax Breakdown

\`\`\`bash
admin   ALL=(ALL:ALL)   ALL
│       │   │    │      │
│       │   │    │      └─ Commands allowed (ALL = everything)
│       │   │    └─ Run as group (ALL = any group)
│       │   └─ Run as user (ALL = any user)
│       └─ On which hosts (ALL = any host)
└─ Username or %groupname
\`\`\`

### Part 3: Common Sudoers Entries

#### Basic Examples

\`\`\`bash
# User gets full root access
admin   ALL=(ALL:ALL)   ALL

# User can run specific command
john    ALL=(ALL)       /usr/bin/systemctl restart nginx

# User can run multiple commands
jane    ALL=(ALL)       /usr/bin/systemctl restart nginx, \\
                        /usr/bin/systemctl status nginx

# Group (% prefix) gets access
%wheel      ALL=(ALL:ALL)   ALL
%sudo       ALL=(ALL:ALL)   ALL
%admin      ALL=(ALL:ALL)   ALL

# No password required
admin   ALL=(ALL)       NOPASSWD: ALL

# Specific command without password
%developers ALL=(ALL)   NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/docker
\`\`\`

#### Advanced Examples

\`\`\`bash
# Run as specific user
john    ALL=(postgres)  /usr/bin/psql

# Run as specific user and group
jane    ALL=(www-data:www-data) /usr/sbin/nginx

# Multiple users
bob, alice, charlie ALL=(ALL) /sbin/reboot

# Specific host
john    webserver1=(ALL) /usr/bin/systemctl restart nginx

# Command with arguments
jane    ALL=(ALL)   /usr/bin/systemctl restart nginx
# ⚠️ This allows ONLY: sudo systemctl restart nginx
# NOT: sudo systemctl restart apache2

# Wildcard arguments
jane    ALL=(ALL)   /usr/bin/systemctl restart *
# ✅ This allows: sudo systemctl restart <anything>

# Multiple commands
%devops ALL=(ALL)   /usr/bin/systemctl *, \\
                    /usr/bin/docker *, \\
                    /usr/sbin/reboot
\`\`\`

### Part 4: Sudoers.d Directory (Best Practice)

Instead of editing \`/etc/sudoers\` directly, use modular files:

\`\`\`bash
# Create separate file for developers
sudo visudo -f /etc/sudoers.d/developers

# Content:
# Allow developers to manage docker without password
%developers ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/docker

# Save and exit (:wq in vim)
\`\`\`

**Benefits:**
- Easier management (one file per team/purpose)
- Safer (corrupted file won't break entire sudo)
- Modular (can be deployed via config management)

**File naming rules:**
- No \`.\` in filename (e.g., \`developers.conf\` won't work!)
- No \`~\` in filename
- Alphanumeric and underscore only: \`developers_team\` ✅

\`\`\`bash
# ✅ Good filenames
/etc/sudoers.d/developers
/etc/sudoers.d/docker_users
/etc/sudoers.d/10_admins

# ❌ Bad filenames (will be ignored!)
/etc/sudoers.d/developers.conf
/etc/sudoers.d/backup~
/etc/sudoers.d/file.old
\`\`\`

### Part 5: Sudo Aliases

Simplify complex configurations with aliases:

\`\`\`bash
# Edit /etc/sudoers
sudo visudo

# User aliases
User_Alias ADMINS = john, jane, bob
User_Alias DEVOPS = alice, charlie

# Host aliases
Host_Alias WEBSERVERS = web1, web2, web3
Host_Alias DATABASES = db1, db2

# Command aliases
Cmnd_Alias SERVICES = /usr/bin/systemctl start *, \\
                      /usr/bin/systemctl stop *, \\
                      /usr/bin/systemctl restart *, \\
                      /usr/bin/systemctl status *

Cmnd_Alias NETWORKING = /usr/sbin/ip, \\
                        /usr/bin/nmcli, \\
                        /usr/bin/ss

Cmnd_Alias DOCKER = /usr/bin/docker, /usr/bin/docker-compose

# Runas aliases
Runas_Alias WEBUSERS = www-data, nginx, apache

# Use aliases
ADMINS ALL=(ALL) SERVICES
DEVOPS WEBSERVERS=(ALL) DOCKER
john DATABASES=(postgres) /usr/bin/psql
\`\`\`

### Part 6: Sudo Tags

#### NOPASSWD — No Password Required

\`\`\`bash
# User doesn't need to enter password
%developers ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/docker

# Mix NOPASSWD and normal
john ALL=(ALL) /sbin/reboot, \\
               NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/systemctl status *
# Status check = no password
# Reboot = password required
\`\`\`

#### PASSWD — Require Password (Default)

\`\`\`bash
# Explicit (rarely needed, it's default)
john ALL=(ALL) PASSWD: /sbin/reboot
\`\`\`

#### NOEXEC — Prevent Shell Escape

\`\`\`bash
# Prevent commands that can spawn shells
john ALL=(ALL) NOEXEC: /usr/bin/less, /usr/bin/vi

# Without NOEXEC:
# sudo vi → :!/bin/bash → root shell escape!
\`\`\`

#### SETENV — Allow Environment Variables

\`\`\`bash
# Allow user to set environment variables
john ALL=(ALL) SETENV: /usr/local/bin/deploy.sh

# User can run:
sudo FOO=bar /usr/local/bin/deploy.sh
\`\`\`

### Part 7: Common Sudo Commands

\`\`\`bash
# List allowed commands for current user
sudo -l
sudo -ll  # Verbose (shows defaults)

# Run command as root
sudo command

# Run command as specific user
sudo -u postgres psql
sudo -u www-data whoami

# Run command as specific group
sudo -g developers whoami

# Interactive root shell (login shell)
sudo -i
# Changes to /root, sources .profile, etc.

# Shell as root (non-login shell)
sudo -s
# Stays in current directory, keeps environment

# Preserve environment
sudo -E command

# Run previous command with sudo
sudo !!

# Edit file as root (uses $EDITOR or vi)
sudoedit /etc/hosts
# Safer than: sudo vim /etc/hosts

# Kill sudo session (forget password)
sudo -k

# Validate sudoers file
visudo -c
\`\`\`

### Part 8: Production Scenarios

#### Scenario 1: DevOps Team — Service Management

\`\`\`bash
# /etc/sudoers.d/devops

# Allow devops group to manage services without password
Cmnd_Alias SERVICES = /usr/bin/systemctl start *, \\
                      /usr/bin/systemctl stop *, \\
                      /usr/bin/systemctl restart *, \\
                      /usr/bin/systemctl status *, \\
                      /usr/bin/systemctl reload *

%devops ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: SERVICES
\`\`\`

#### Scenario 2: Developers — Docker Access

\`\`\`bash
# /etc/sudoers.d/developers

# Developers can use docker without password
%developers ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/docker, \\
                                /usr/bin/docker-compose

# Alternative: add to docker group (no sudo needed)
# usermod -aG docker username
\`\`\`

#### Scenario 3: Database Admin — Postgres Access

\`\`\`bash
# /etc/sudoers.d/dba

# DBA can run psql as postgres user
%dba ALL=(postgres) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/psql, \\
                              /usr/bin/pg_dump, \\
                              /usr/bin/pg_restore
\`\`\`

#### Scenario 4: Limited Admin — Specific Tasks Only

\`\`\`bash
# /etc/sudoers.d/limited_admin

# User can ONLY restart nginx, view logs, and reboot
john ALL=(ALL) /usr/bin/systemctl restart nginx, \\
               /usr/bin/systemctl status nginx, \\
               /usr/bin/journalctl -u nginx, \\
               /sbin/reboot
\`\`\`

#### Scenario 5: Backup User — Read-Only Access

\`\`\`bash
# /etc/sudoers.d/backup

# Backup user can read any file (for backups)
backup ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /bin/tar, /bin/rsync

# Better: use specific wrapper script
backup ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/local/bin/backup.sh
\`\`\`

### Part 9: Security Best Practices

#### ✅ DO

\`\`\`bash
# ✅ Use visudo
sudo visudo

# ✅ Use sudoers.d for modularity
sudo visudo -f /etc/sudoers.d/team_name

# ✅ Limit to specific commands
john ALL=(ALL) /usr/bin/systemctl restart nginx

# ✅ Use NOEXEC for editors
john ALL=(ALL) NOEXEC: /usr/bin/vi

# ✅ Require password for dangerous commands
john ALL=(ALL) /sbin/reboot  # No NOPASSWD!

# ✅ Log sudo usage
# (Enabled by default in /var/log/auth.log or /var/log/secure)
\`\`\`

#### ❌ DON'T

\`\`\`bash
# ❌ Never edit /etc/sudoers with regular editor
nano /etc/sudoers  # DANGER! Syntax error = sudo broken!

# ❌ Don't give unrestricted NOPASSWD
john ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: ALL  # Same as root without password!

# ❌ Don't use wildcards carelessly
john ALL=(ALL) /bin/*  # Allows /bin/bash → root shell!

# ❌ Don't allow shell commands without NOEXEC
john ALL=(ALL) /usr/bin/vi, /usr/bin/less, /usr/bin/find
# User can escape to shell!

# ❌ Don't use ALL for specific tasks
john ALL=(ALL) ALL  # Too much privilege!
\`\`\`

### Part 10: Troubleshooting Sudo

#### Problem 1: "sudo: command not found"

\`\`\`bash
# Check if sudo is installed
which sudo
# If not: apt install sudo  OR  yum install sudo

# Check PATH
echo $PATH
# Sudo usually in /usr/bin/sudo
\`\`\`

#### Problem 2: "user is not in the sudoers file"

\`\`\`bash
# Add user to sudo group (Debian/Ubuntu)
usermod -aG sudo username

# OR wheel group (RHEL/CentOS)
usermod -aG wheel username

# OR add manually to sudoers
visudo
# Add: username ALL=(ALL:ALL) ALL
\`\`\`

#### Problem 3: Syntax Error in Sudoers

\`\`\`bash
# If you broke /etc/sudoers:

# Method 1: Boot to recovery mode
# - Reboot
# - Select recovery mode in GRUB
# - Drop to root shell
# visudo  # Fix errors

# Method 2: Use pkexec (if available)
pkexec visudo

# Method 3: Mount root filesystem from live USB
# Fix /etc/sudoers

# Check syntax before saving
visudo -c
\`\`\`

#### Problem 4: Sudo Asks for Password Every Time

\`\`\`bash
# Check timestamp timeout (default: 15 minutes)
sudo -k  # Reset timestamp

# Change timeout in /etc/sudoers
Defaults timestamp_timeout=30  # 30 minutes
Defaults timestamp_timeout=0   # Always ask
Defaults timestamp_timeout=-1  # Never ask (dangerous!)
\`\`\`

#### Problem 5: Can't Run Specific Command

\`\`\`bash
# Check what you're allowed to run
sudo -l

# Example output:
# User john may run the following commands on webserver:
#     (ALL) /usr/bin/systemctl restart nginx

# Debug: run with -v (verbose)
sudo -v
\`\`\`

### Part 11: Sudo Logs

\`\`\`bash
# Debian/Ubuntu
tail -f /var/log/auth.log | grep sudo

# RHEL/CentOS
tail -f /var/log/secure | grep sudo

# Example log entry:
# Jan 30 10:15:32 server sudo: john : TTY=pts/0 ; PWD=/home/john ; USER=root ; COMMAND=/usr/bin/systemctl restart nginx

# Parse sudo logs
grep sudo /var/log/auth.log | awk '{print $5, $6, $NF}'

# Count sudo usage per user
grep sudo /var/log/auth.log | awk '{print $6}' | sort | uniq -c
\`\`\`

### Part 12: Advanced Sudo Configuration

#### Defaults Directives

\`\`\`bash
# Edit /etc/sudoers
sudo visudo

# Password timeout (minutes)
Defaults timestamp_timeout=30

# Require password for every command
Defaults timestamp_timeout=0

# Environment variables to keep
Defaults env_keep += "HTTP_PROXY HTTPS_PROXY NO_PROXY"

# Secure PATH
Defaults secure_path="/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin"

# Log input/output of sudo commands
Defaults log_input, log_output
Defaults iolog_dir=/var/log/sudo-io

# Insults when wrong password :)
Defaults insults

# Lecture when first sudo
Defaults lecture=always
Defaults lecture_file=/etc/sudo_lecture.txt

# Mail on errors
Defaults mailto="admin@example.com"
Defaults mail_badpass

# Set editor for visudo
Defaults editor=/usr/bin/vim
\`\`\`

### Part 13: Quick Reference

\`\`\`bash
# Edit sudoers
sudo visudo
sudo visudo -f /etc/sudoers.d/filename

# Check syntax
visudo -c

# List allowed commands
sudo -l

# Run as different user
sudo -u username command

# Interactive root shell
sudo -i

# Preserve environment
sudo -E command

# Edit file safely
sudoedit /etc/hosts

# Forget password (invalidate timestamp)
sudo -k

# Validate timestamp (refresh timeout)
sudo -v
\`\`\`

### Part 14: Real Production Example

\`\`\`bash
# /etc/sudoers.d/webapp_team

# Webapp team configuration
User_Alias WEBAPP_DEVS = alice, bob, charlie
Host_Alias WEBAPP_SERVERS = web1, web2, web3

Cmnd_Alias WEBAPP_SERVICES = /usr/bin/systemctl restart nginx, \\
                             /usr/bin/systemctl reload nginx, \\
                             /usr/bin/systemctl status nginx

Cmnd_Alias WEBAPP_LOGS = /usr/bin/journalctl -u nginx, \\
                         /usr/bin/tail /var/log/nginx/*

Cmnd_Alias WEBAPP_DEPLOY = /usr/local/bin/deploy-webapp.sh

# Rules
WEBAPP_DEVS WEBAPP_SERVERS=(www-data) NOPASSWD: WEBAPP_SERVICES
WEBAPP_DEVS WEBAPP_SERVERS=(ALL) NOPASSWD: WEBAPP_LOGS
WEBAPP_DEVS WEBAPP_SERVERS=(ALL) WEBAPP_DEPLOY

# Defaults for this group
Defaults!WEBAPP_SERVICES !lecture
Defaults!WEBAPP_LOGS timestamp_timeout=60
\`\`\`
        `,
