        title: "Systemd Deep Dive",
        type: "theory",
        duration: "55 min",
        content: `
## Systemd — Modern Init System (Complete Guide)

### Part 1: What is Systemd?

**Systemd** = System daemon, modern init system that replaced SysV init.

**Replaced:**
- SysV init scripts (\`/etc/init.d/\`)
- Upstart (Ubuntu's attempt)
- Various service managers

**Main author:** Lennart Poettering (Red Hat)

**First appeared:** 2010
**Adopted by:** RHEL 7+, CentOS 7+, Ubuntu 15.04+, Debian 8+, Arch, Fedora

#### Why Systemd?

| Feature | SysV Init | Systemd |
|---------|-----------|---------|
| Startup speed | Sequential | Parallel |
| Service dependencies | Manual scripts | Declarative |
| Logging | /var/log/* | journald (centralized) |
| On-demand starting | No | Yes (socket activation) |
| Cgroups integration | No | Yes |
| Timers | cron | systemd timers |

### Part 2: Core Concepts

#### Units — The Building Blocks

Everything in systemd is a **unit**. Unit = resource that systemd knows how to manage.

**Unit types:**

| Type | Extension | Purpose | Example |
|------|-----------|---------|---------|
| Service | .service | Daemon/process | nginx.service |
| Socket | .socket | IPC/network socket | docker.socket |
| Mount | .mount | Filesystem mount | home.mount |
| Automount | .automount | Auto-mount on access | mnt-data.automount |
| Timer | .timer | Scheduled tasks (cron alternative) | backup.timer |
| Target | .target | Group of units (runlevel) | multi-user.target |
| Path | .path | File/directory watching | mailqueue.path |
| Device | .device | Device management | dev-sda.device |
| Swap | .swap | Swap space | dev-sda2.swap |
| Slice | .slice | Resource control | user.slice |
| Scope | .scope | Externally created processes | session-1.scope |

### Part 3: Systemctl — The Control Command

#### Service Management (Common Operations)

\`\`\`bash
# Start/Stop/Restart
systemctl start nginx
systemctl stop nginx
systemctl restart nginx          # Stop + Start
systemctl reload nginx           # Reload config (no downtime, SIGHUP)
systemctl reload-or-restart nginx # Try reload, fallback to restart

# Status
systemctl status nginx           # Detailed status
systemctl status nginx -l        # Full output (no truncation)
systemctl status nginx -n 50     # Last 50 log lines
systemctl is-active nginx        # active/inactive
systemctl is-failed nginx        # failed/active
systemctl is-enabled nginx       # enabled/disabled

# Enable/Disable (Boot-time behavior)
systemctl enable nginx           # Start on boot
systemctl disable nginx          # Don't start on boot
systemctl enable --now nginx     # Enable + start immediately
systemctl disable --now nginx    # Disable + stop immediately
systemctl is-enabled nginx       # Check status

# Mask/Unmask (Prevent service from starting)
systemctl mask nginx             # Can't start (even manually!)
systemctl unmask nginx           # Re-enable starting

# Show configuration
systemctl show nginx             # All properties
systemctl show nginx -p ExecStart # Specific property
systemctl cat nginx              # Show unit file content
\`\`\`

#### Listing & Filtering Units

\`\`\`bash
# List all units
systemctl list-units             # Active units
systemctl list-units --all       # All (including inactive)
systemctl list-units --type=service # Only services
systemctl list-units --type=timer   # Only timers
systemctl list-units --state=running # Only running
systemctl list-units --state=failed  # Only failed

# List unit files (installed, not just active)
systemctl list-unit-files
systemctl list-unit-files --type=service
systemctl list-unit-files | grep enabled

# Failed units
systemctl --failed               # All failed units
systemctl --failed --type=service # Only failed services

# Reset failed state
systemctl reset-failed           # All
systemctl reset-failed nginx     # Specific service
\`\`\`

#### Dependencies

\`\`\`bash
# View dependencies
systemctl list-dependencies nginx         # Tree view
systemctl list-dependencies nginx --all   # Recursive (full tree)
systemctl list-dependencies nginx --reverse # What depends on nginx

# Verify dependencies
systemd-analyze verify nginx.service
\`\`\`

### Part 4: Unit File Anatomy

#### File Locations

| Location | Priority | Purpose |
|----------|----------|---------|
| \`/etc/systemd/system/\` | Highest | Custom unit files |
| \`/run/systemd/system/\` | Medium | Runtime units |
| \`/usr/lib/systemd/system/\` | Lowest | Package-installed units |

\`\`\`bash
# Best practice: custom units in /etc/systemd/system/
# Example: /etc/systemd/system/myapp.service
\`\`\`

#### Unit File Structure

\`\`\`ini
# /etc/systemd/system/webapp.service

[Unit]
Description=Web Application Server
Documentation=https://docs.example.com/webapp
After=network.target network-online.target postgresql.service
Wants=network-online.target
Requires=postgresql.service

[Service]
Type=simple
User=webapp
Group=webapp
WorkingDirectory=/opt/webapp
Environment="NODE_ENV=production"
Environment="PORT=3000"
EnvironmentFile=/etc/webapp/env
ExecStartPre=/opt/webapp/bin/pre-start.sh
ExecStart=/opt/webapp/bin/start.sh
ExecReload=/bin/kill -HUP \$MAINPID
ExecStop=/opt/webapp/bin/stop.sh
ExecStopPost=/opt/webapp/bin/cleanup.sh
Restart=on-failure
RestartSec=10
KillMode=mixed
TimeoutStartSec=60
TimeoutStopSec=30
StandardOutput=journal
StandardError=journal
SyslogIdentifier=webapp

# Security
PrivateTmp=true
NoNewPrivileges=true
ReadOnlyPaths=/etc /usr
ProtectSystem=strict
ProtectHome=true

# Resource limits
LimitNOFILE=65536
CPUQuota=80%
MemoryLimit=2G

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
Alias=app.service
\`\`\`

#### [Unit] Section

\`\`\`ini
[Unit]
Description=Human-readable description
Documentation=man:nginx(8) https://nginx.org/docs
After=network.target       # Start AFTER these units
Before=webapp.service      # Start BEFORE these units
Wants=postgresql.service   # Weak dependency (doesn't fail if missing)
Requires=network.target    # Strong dependency (fails if missing)
Conflicts=apache2.service  # Can't run simultaneously
ConditionPathExists=/etc/myapp/config.yml # Only start if file exists
AssertPathIsDirectory=/var/lib/myapp      # Fail if not a directory
\`\`\`

**Dependency types:**
- **After/Before** — ordering (doesn't imply dependency!)
- **Wants** — weak dependency (recommended start)
- **Requires** — strong dependency (must start)
- **BindsTo** — like Requires + stops when dependency stops
- **Conflicts** — mutually exclusive
- **Condition\*** — silent skip if condition not met
- **Assert\*** — fail with error if condition not met

#### [Service] Section — Type

\`\`\`ini
[Service]
Type=simple    # Default: ExecStart is main process
Type=forking   # Traditional daemon: forks into background
Type=oneshot   # Short-lived task (runs once)
Type=notify    # Process sends sd_notify() when ready
Type=dbus      # Ready when gets D-Bus name
Type=idle      # Delay start until all jobs finished
\`\`\`

**Examples:**

\`\`\`ini
# Type=simple (most common)
Type=simple
ExecStart=/usr/bin/myapp

# Type=forking (traditional daemons like nginx)
Type=forking
PIDFile=/run/nginx.pid
ExecStart=/usr/sbin/nginx

# Type=oneshot (for tasks, not daemons)
Type=oneshot
RemainAfterExit=yes
ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/setup.sh
\`\`\`

#### [Service] Section — Execution

\`\`\`ini
[Service]
# Commands
ExecStartPre=/usr/bin/check-requirements.sh  # Run before start
ExecStart=/usr/bin/myapp                     # Main command
ExecStartPost=/usr/bin/notify-started.sh     # Run after start
ExecReload=/bin/kill -HUP \$MAINPID           # Reload command
ExecStop=/usr/bin/myapp --shutdown           # Stop command
ExecStopPost=/usr/bin/cleanup.sh             # Cleanup after stop

# User/Group
User=appuser
Group=appgroup
SupplementaryGroups=developers docker

# Working directory
WorkingDirectory=/opt/myapp

# Environment
Environment="FOO=bar"
Environment="BAZ=qux"
EnvironmentFile=/etc/myapp/env               # Load from file

# Restart behavior
Restart=no             # Never restart (default)
Restart=on-failure     # Restart if exits non-zero
Restart=on-abnormal    # Restart if killed by signal
Restart=on-abort       # Restart if terminated abnormally
Restart=always         # Always restart
RestartSec=5           # Wait 5 seconds before restart

# Timeouts
TimeoutStartSec=60     # Max time to start
TimeoutStopSec=30      # Max time to stop gracefully
TimeoutSec=90          # Sets both Start and Stop

# PID
PIDFile=/run/myapp.pid
\`\`\`

#### [Install] Section

\`\`\`ini
[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target    # Enable in this target
RequiredBy=nginx.service      # Must be enabled with nginx
Alias=app.service             # Alternative name
Also=myapp-helper.service     # Enable this service too
\`\`\`

### Part 5: Targets (Runlevels Replacement)

**Targets** = groups of units (similar to old runlevels).

\`\`\`bash
# View current target
systemctl get-default
# Output: multi-user.target

# Set default target
systemctl set-default graphical.target
systemctl set-default multi-user.target

# Switch to target (immediately)
systemctl isolate rescue.target
systemctl isolate multi-user.target

# List all targets
systemctl list-units --type=target
systemctl list-units --type=target --all
\`\`\`

**Common targets:**

| Target | Old Runlevel | Description | Services |
|--------|--------------|-------------|----------|
| poweroff.target | 0 | Shutdown | None |
| rescue.target | 1, S | Single-user mode | Minimal |
| multi-user.target | 2, 3, 4 | Multi-user, no GUI | Full server |
| graphical.target | 5 | Multi-user + GUI | Desktop |
| reboot.target | 6 | Reboot | None |
| emergency.target | - | Emergency shell | Absolutely minimal |

\`\`\`bash
# Check what target a service is wanted by
systemctl show nginx.service -p WantedBy
# WantedBy=multi-user.target
\`\`\`

### Part 6: Journal — Systemd Logging

\`\`\`bash
# View all logs
journalctl

# Follow logs (like tail -f)
journalctl -f

# Specific service
journalctl -u nginx
journalctl -u nginx -f             # Follow nginx logs

# Time filtering
journalctl --since "2026-01-30 10:00:00"
journalctl --since "1 hour ago"
journalctl --since today
journalctl --since yesterday
journalctl --until "2026-01-30 12:00:00"

# Priority filtering
journalctl -p err                  # Only errors
journalctl -p warning              # Warning and above

# Kernel messages
journalctl -k
journalctl -k -b                   # This boot only

# Boot logs
journalctl -b                      # Current boot
journalctl -b -1                   # Previous boot
journalctl --list-boots            # List all boots

# Output formats
journalctl -o json                 # JSON format
journalctl -o json-pretty
journalctl -o verbose

# Disk usage
journalctl --disk-usage

# Cleanup
journalctl --vacuum-size=500M      # Keep max 500MB
journalctl --vacuum-time=30d       # Keep max 30 days

# Verify integrity
journalctl --verify
\`\`\`

### Part 7: Creating Custom Service

**Example: Node.js web app**

\`\`\`bash
# Step 1: Create unit file
sudo vim /etc/systemd/system/nodeapp.service
\`\`\`

\`\`\`ini
[Unit]
Description=Node.js Web Application
After=network.target

[Service]
Type=simple
User=nodeapp
WorkingDirectory=/opt/nodeapp
ExecStart=/usr/bin/node /opt/nodeapp/server.js
Restart=on-failure
RestartSec=10
StandardOutput=journal
StandardError=journal
SyslogIdentifier=nodeapp

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
\`\`\`

\`\`\`bash
# Step 2: Reload systemd
sudo systemctl daemon-reload

# Step 3: Start service
sudo systemctl start nodeapp

# Step 4: Check status
sudo systemctl status nodeapp

# Step 5: Enable on boot
sudo systemctl enable nodeapp

# Step 6: View logs
sudo journalctl -u nodeapp -f
\`\`\`

### Part 8: Production Scenarios

#### Scenario 1: Service Won't Start

\`\`\`bash
# Check status
systemctl status myapp
# ● myapp.service - My Application
#    Loaded: loaded (/etc/systemd/system/myapp.service; enabled)
#    Active: failed (Result: exit-code)

# View full logs
journalctl -u myapp -n 100 --no-pager

# Check dependencies
systemctl list-dependencies myapp --failed

# Verify unit file syntax
systemd-analyze verify /etc/systemd/system/myapp.service

# Check file permissions
ls -l /etc/systemd/system/myapp.service
ls -l /opt/myapp/bin/start.sh

# Test command manually
sudo -u appuser /opt/myapp/bin/start.sh
\`\`\`

#### Scenario 2: Service Crashes Repeatedly

\`\`\`bash
# Check crash logs
journalctl -u myapp --since "10 minutes ago"

# Increase restart interval
# Edit /etc/systemd/system/myapp.service:
[Service]
Restart=on-failure
RestartSec=30          # Wait 30 seconds
StartLimitBurst=5      # Max 5 restart attempts
StartLimitIntervalSec=60  # Within 60 seconds

# Reload
systemctl daemon-reload
systemctl restart myapp
\`\`\`

#### Scenario 3: Override Vendor Unit File

\`\`\`bash
# Don't edit /usr/lib/systemd/system/nginx.service!
# Use override instead:

systemctl edit nginx
# Opens editor for override file: /etc/systemd/system/nginx.service.d/override.conf

# Add custom settings:
[Service]
Environment="CUSTOM_VAR=value"
LimitNOFILE=100000

# Save and reload
systemctl daemon-reload
systemctl restart nginx

# View effective configuration
systemctl cat nginx
\`\`\`

#### Scenario 4: Service Takes Too Long to Stop

\`\`\`bash
# Problem: systemctl stop myapp takes 90 seconds (default timeout)

# Solution: Adjust timeout in unit file
[Service]
TimeoutStopSec=10      # Kill after 10 seconds
KillMode=mixed         # SIGTERM to main, then SIGKILL to all

# OR force kill immediately
systemctl kill -s SIGKILL myapp
\`\`\`

### Part 9: Best Practices

#### ✅ DO

- Use \`systemctl edit\` for overrides (not direct file editing)
- Always \`systemctl daemon-reload\` after editing unit files
- Use \`Type=notify\` for apps that support it (sd_notify)
- Set resource limits (\`MemoryLimit\`, \`CPUQuota\`)
- Enable security features (\`PrivateTmp\`, \`ProtectSystem\`)
- Use \`journalctl\` for centralized logging
- Test unit files with \`systemd-analyze verify\`

#### ❌ DON'T

- Don't edit vendor files in \`/usr/lib/systemd/system/\`
- Don't forget \`daemon-reload\` after changes
- Don't use \`Type=forking\` if \`Type=simple\` works
- Don't use absolute paths without testing
- Don't run services as root unless necessary

### Part 10: Quick Reference

\`\`\`bash
# Service management
systemctl start|stop|restart|reload service
systemctl status service
systemctl enable|disable service

# Unit files
systemctl cat service              # View unit file
systemctl edit service             # Override (recommended)
systemctl daemon-reload            # Reload unit files

# Logs
journalctl -u service              # Service logs
journalctl -u service -f           # Follow logs
journalctl --since "1 hour ago"    # Time filter

# Dependencies
systemctl list-dependencies service

# Targets
systemctl get-default              # Current default target
systemctl set-default target       # Change default
systemctl isolate target           # Switch immediately

# Debugging
systemctl --failed                 # Failed units
systemd-analyze verify unit        # Verify unit file
\`\`\`
        `,
        keyPoints: [
          "systemctl daemon-reload — ALWAYS after editing unit files",
          "systemctl enable --now — enable + start atomically",
          "journalctl -u service -f — real-time logs",
          "systemctl edit — creates override (recommended)",
          "Type=simple — most common (default)",
          "Restart=on-failure — auto-restart on crash",
          "systemd-analyze verify — test unit files",
          "/etc/systemd/system/ — custom units (highest priority)"
        ]
      },
