        id: 2,
        title: "Process Management",
        type: "theory",
        duration: "45 min",
        content: `
## Process Management — Production Guide

### Part 1: Process Fundamentals

#### What is a Process?

**Process** = running instance of a program. Har bir process:
- **PID** (Process ID) — unique raqam
- **Parent** (PPID) — qaysi process yaratgan
- **User** — qaysi user egasi
- **State** — running, sleeping, zombie, stopped
- **Memory** — qancha RAM ishlatayapti
- **CPU** — qancha CPU time olgan

#### Process States

| State | Code | Ma'nosi | Example |
|-------|------|---------|---------|
| Running | R | CPU ishlatayapti | Hozir execute bo'layapti |
| Sleeping | S | Interruptible sleep | I/O kutayapti |
| Uninterruptible | D | Uninterruptible sleep | Disk I/O (kill qilib bo'lmaydi!) |
| Stopped | T | Stopped (suspended) | Ctrl+Z bosilgan |
| Zombie | Z | Terminated but not reaped | Parent cleanup qilmagan |

### Part 2: Viewing Processes

#### ps Command — Process Snapshot

\`\`\`bash
# Basic usage
ps aux                    # All processes (BSD style)
ps -ef                    # All processes (UNIX style)
ps -ejH                   # Process tree format
ps axjf                   # Tree with more details

# Filtering
ps -u username            # Specific user
ps -C nginx               # Specific command name
ps -p 1234,5678           # Specific PIDs
ps --ppid 1               # Children of PID 1

# Sorting
ps aux --sort=-%mem       # Memory usage (highest first)
ps aux --sort=-%cpu       # CPU usage
ps aux --sort=-rss        # Resident memory size
ps aux --sort=start_time  # Oldest first

# Custom output format
ps -eo pid,ppid,cmd,%mem,%cpu,user
ps -eo pid,ppid,cmd,stat,rss,vsz --sort=-%mem | head -20

# Real production example
ps aux | grep -E 'nginx|mysql|java' | awk '{print $1,$2,$3,$4,$11}'
\`\`\`

#### top/htop — Real-Time Monitoring

\`\`\`bash
# top interactive commands
top
# Press while running:
# 1     — Show all CPUs separately
# M     — Sort by memory
# P     — Sort by CPU
# k     — Kill a process (enter PID)
# r     - Renice a process
# f     — Select display fields
# W     — Save configuration
# q     — Quit

# top with options
top -u nginx              # Show only nginx user
top -p 1234,5678          # Monitor specific PIDs
top -d 1                  # Update every 1 second
top -b -n 1               # Batch mode (for scripts)

# htop (better alternative)
htop
# F2 — Setup
# F3 — Search
# F4 — Filter
# F5 — Tree view
# F6 — Sort by
# F9 — Kill
# F10 — Quit
\`\`\`

#### Process Tree

\`\`\`bash
# pstree — visual tree
pstree                    # Simple tree
pstree -p                 # With PIDs
pstree -a                 # With arguments
pstree -u                 # With user transitions
pstree 1234               # Subtree from specific PID

# systemd-cgls — cgroup tree
systemd-cgls              # Full cgroup hierarchy
systemd-cgls /system.slice/nginx.service
\`\`\`

### Part 3: Signals — Production Deep Dive

#### Common Signals

| Signal | Number | Action | Catchable? | Use Case |
|--------|--------|--------|------------|----------|
| SIGTERM | 15 | Terminate | Yes | Graceful shutdown (default kill) |
| SIGKILL | 9 | Kill | No | Force kill (last resort!) |
| SIGHUP | 1 | Hangup | Yes | Reload config (nginx, apache) |
| SIGINT | 2 | Interrupt | Yes | Ctrl+C |
| SIGQUIT | 3 | Quit | Yes | Ctrl+\\ (core dump) |
| SIGSTOP | 19 | Stop | No | Pause process |
| SIGCONT | 18 | Continue | Yes | Resume process |
| SIGUSR1/2 | 10/12 | User-defined | Yes | Custom app signals |

\`\`\`bash
# Send signals
kill PID                  # SIGTERM (15) - graceful
kill -15 PID              # Same as above
kill -TERM PID            # Same (by name)
kill -9 PID               # SIGKILL (force)
kill -HUP PID             # SIGHUP (reload)

# Kill by name
pkill nginx               # Kill all nginx processes
pkill -9 -u username      # Force kill all user's processes
pkill -SIGHUP nginx       # Reload nginx

# killall (kills by exact name)
killall nginx
killall -u username       # All processes of user

# Signal list
kill -l                   # List all signals
\`\`\`

#### Production Signal Scenarios

**Scenario 1: Graceful Nginx Reload**

\`\`\`bash
# Wrong way (downtime!)
systemctl restart nginx

# Right way (zero downtime)
nginx -t                  # Test config first!
systemctl reload nginx    # or: kill -HUP $(cat /var/run/nginx.pid)

# What happens:
# 1. Nginx gets SIGHUP
# 2. Reloads config
# 3. Spawns new workers
# 4. Old workers finish current requests
# 5. Old workers exit gracefully
\`\`\`

**Scenario 2: Stuck Process**

\`\`\`bash
# 1. Try graceful first
kill 1234                 # SIGTERM

# 2. Wait a bit
sleep 5

# 3. Check if still running
ps -p 1234

# 4. Force kill if needed
kill -9 1234

# Production script version
#!/bin/bash
PID=$1
kill $PID && sleep 5 && ps -p $PID > /dev/null && kill -9 $PID
\`\`\`

**Scenario 3: Uninterruptible Sleep (D state)**

\`\`\`bash
# Find stuck processes
ps aux | awk '$8 ~ /D/ {print}'

# Common cause: NFS hang, broken disk
# Cannot be killed! (SIGKILL ham ishlamaydi)
# Solution:
# 1. Fix underlying issue (umount NFS, fix disk)
# 2. Reboot server (oxirgi yo'l)

# Example: stuck NFS
mount | grep nfs          # Find NFS mounts
umount -l /mnt/nfs        # Lazy umount
\`\`\`

### Part 4: Background Jobs & Nohup

\`\`\`bash
# Run in background
command &
sleep 100 &

# Job control
jobs                      # List jobs ([1]+ Running...)
jobs -l                   # With PIDs
fg %1                     # Bring job 1 to foreground
bg %1                     # Send job 1 to background
Ctrl+Z                    # Suspend current job
Ctrl+C                    # Kill current job

# Disown (remove from shell's job table)
disown %1                 # Job 1 won't receive SIGHUP on logout
disown -a                 # All jobs

# nohup (immune to hangups)
nohup ./long-script.sh &
# Output goes to nohup.out
# Process survives SSH disconnect

# Production pattern
nohup ./app.sh > /var/log/app.log 2>&1 &
echo $! > /var/run/app.pid
\`\`\`

#### screen/tmux — Better than nohup

\`\`\`bash
# screen
screen -S myapp           # Create named session
# Run your commands
Ctrl+A, D                 # Detach
screen -r myapp           # Re-attach
screen -ls                # List sessions

# tmux (modern alternative)
tmux new -s myapp         # Create session
Ctrl+B, D                 # Detach
tmux attach -t myapp      # Re-attach
tmux ls                   # List sessions

# Why better than nohup?
# - Interactive
# - Multiple windows
# - Shared sessions (pair programming)
# - Session logging
\`\`\`

### Part 5: Process Priority (nice/renice)

#### Nice Values

- Range: **-20** (highest priority) to **+19** (lowest)
- Default: **0**
- Only root can decrease nice (increase priority)
- Lower nice = higher priority

\`\`\`bash
# Start with specific nice
nice -n 10 ./cpu-intensive-task.sh    # Lower priority
nice -n -5 ./important-task.sh        # Higher (root only)

# Change running process
renice -n 5 -p 1234                   # Set nice to 5
renice -n -10 -p 1234                 # Root only
renice -n 10 -u username              # All user's processes

# Real example: backup script (low priority)
nice -n 19 tar czf /backup/data.tar.gz /var/www/
\`\`\`

#### ionice — I/O Priority

\`\`\`bash
# ionice classes:
# 0 = none
# 1 = realtime (root only)
# 2 = best-effort (default)
# 3 = idle (only when system is idle)

# Examples
ionice -c 3 ./backup.sh               # Idle I/O (won't slow down system)
ionice -c 2 -n 4 ./data-processing.sh # Best-effort, priority 4

# Combined with nice
nice -n 19 ionice -c 3 rsync -av /data/ /backup/
\`\`\`

### Part 6: Production Troubleshooting

#### Scenario 1: High CPU Usage

\`\`\`bash
# Find CPU hogs
top -b -n 1 | head -20
ps aux --sort=-%cpu | head -10

# Detailed process info
top -H -p 1234            # Thread-level view
ps -p 1234 -L -o pid,tid,psr,%cpu,comm  # Thread details

# CPU affinity (bind to specific CPU)
taskset -c 0,1 ./app      # Use only CPU 0 and 1
taskset -p 0x3 1234       # Bitmask (CPUs 0,1)

# Limit CPU with systemd
systemctl set-property nginx.service CPUQuota=50%
\`\`\`

#### Scenario 2: High Memory Usage

\`\`\`bash
# Find memory hogs
ps aux --sort=-%mem | head -10
ps -eo pid,ppid,cmd,%mem,rss --sort=-%mem | head -20

# Detailed memory breakdown
pmap 1234                 # Memory map
pmap -x 1234              # Extended format
cat /proc/1234/status     # Detailed info
cat /proc/1234/smaps      # Per-mapping breakdown

# OOM (Out of Memory) history
dmesg | grep -i oom
grep -i 'killed process' /var/log/messages
\`\`\`

#### Scenario 3: Zombie Processes

\`\`\`bash
# Find zombies
ps aux | grep 'Z'
ps -eo stat,pid,ppid,comm | grep '^Z'

# Zombies can't be killed! (already dead)
# Kill the parent instead
ps -o ppid= -p 1234       # Get parent PID
kill $(ps -o ppid= -p 1234)

# Production pattern
#!/bin/bash
# Clean up zombies by restarting parent service
ZOMBIES=$(ps aux | awk '$8 ~ /Z/ {print $2}')
if [ -n "$ZOMBIES" ]; then
  PARENT=$(ps -o ppid= -p $ZOMBIES | head -1)
  systemctl restart $(systemctl status $PARENT | grep 'Loaded:' | awk '{print $2}')
fi
\`\`\`

#### Scenario 4: Process Stuck in D State

\`\`\`bash
# Find D-state processes
ps aux | awk '$8=="D"'

# Check what they're waiting for
cat /proc/1234/stack      # Kernel stack trace
cat /proc/1234/wchan      # Wait channel

# Common causes:
# 1. NFS mount hang
mount -t nfs | grep nfs
umount -l /mnt/nfs        # Lazy unmount

# 2. Failed disk
dmesg | grep -i 'i/o error'
smartctl -a /dev/sda

# 3. Broken iSCSI/SAN
iscsiadm -m session       # Check iSCSI sessions
\`\`\`

### Part 7: Tools Comparison

| Need | Tool | Best For |
|------|------|----------|
| Quick check | \`ps aux\` | Snapshot of all processes |
| Real-time monitoring | \`top\`/\`htop\` | Interactive monitoring |
| Process tree | \`pstree\` | Understanding relationships |
| Resource limits | \`systemd-cgtop\` | Cgroup-based monitoring |
| Detailed analysis | \`/proc/PID/*\` | Deep debugging |
| Historical data | \`atop\`/\`sar\` | Performance history |

### Part 8: Best Practices

1. **Always try SIGTERM before SIGKILL**
   \`\`\`bash
   kill $PID && sleep 5 || kill -9 $PID
   \`\`\`

2. **Use systemd for service management (not direct kill)**
   \`\`\`bash
   # Wrong
   pkill nginx

   # Right
   systemctl stop nginx
   \`\`\`

3. **Monitor zombie accumulation**
   \`\`\`bash
   # Alert if >10 zombies
   ZOMBIES=$(ps aux | grep -c ' Z ')
   [ $ZOMBIES -gt 10 ] && alert_admin
   \`\`\`

4. **Use nice/ionice for batch jobs**
   \`\`\`bash
   nice -n 19 ionice -c 3 ./nightly-backup.sh
   \`\`\`

5. **Set resource limits in systemd**
   \`\`\`ini
   [Service]
   MemoryMax=2G
   CPUQuota=50%
   TasksMax=100
   \`\`\`

### Production Checklist

- [ ] Monitor for zombie processes
- [ ] Set up alerts for D-state processes
- [ ] Configure OOM killer priorities
- [ ] Use systemd resource limits
- [ ] Document signal handling for your apps
- [ ] Test graceful shutdown procedures
- [ ] Set up process monitoring (e.g., Prometheus node_exporter)
        `,
