        content: `
## Cron & Scheduled Tasks — Complete Guide

### Part 1: Cron Fundamentals

#### What is Cron?

**Cron** = time-based job scheduler in Unix-like systems.

**Use cases:**
- Backup scripts
- Log rotation
- Database maintenance
- Monitoring scripts
- Report generation
- Certificate renewal (Let's Encrypt)

**Cron daemon:** \`crond\` (RHEL/CentOS) or \`cron\` (Debian/Ubuntu)

\`\`\`bash
# Check cron service
systemctl status crond    # RHEL/CentOS
systemctl status cron     # Ubuntu/Debian

# Enable/start
systemctl enable --now crond
\`\`\`

### Part 2: Crontab Syntax

#### Crontab Format

\`\`\`
┌───────────── minute (0 - 59)
│ ┌───────────── hour (0 - 23)
│ │ ┌───────────── day of month (1 - 31)
│ │ │ ┌───────────── month (1 - 12)
│ │ │ │ ┌───────────── day of week (0 - 6) (Sunday=0 or 7)
│ │ │ │ │
* * * * * command to execute
\`\`\`

#### Special Characters

| Character | Meaning | Example |
|-----------|---------|---------|
| \`*\` | Any value | Every minute/hour/day |
| \`,\` | Value list | \`1,15,30\` = 1st, 15th, 30th |
| \`-\` | Range | \`1-5\` = Monday to Friday |
| \`/\` | Step values | \`*/15\` = every 15 minutes |

#### Common Patterns

\`\`\`bash
# Every minute
* * * * * command

# Every hour at minute 0
0 * * * * command

# Every day at midnight
0 0 * * * command

# Every day at 2:30 AM
30 2 * * * command

# Every Monday at 9 AM
0 9 * * 1 command

# First day of month at midnight
0 0 1 * * command

# Every 15 minutes
*/15 * * * * command

# Every weekday at 6 PM
0 18 * * 1-5 command

# Every hour from 9 AM to 5 PM on weekdays
0 9-17 * * 1-5 command

# Twice a day (8 AM and 8 PM)
0 8,20 * * * command
\`\`\`

#### Special Strings (Shortcuts)

| String | Equivalent | Meaning |
|--------|-----------|---------|
| \`@reboot\` | - | Run once at startup |
| \`@yearly\` | \`0 0 1 1 *\` | Once a year (Jan 1, midnight) |
| \`@annually\` | \`0 0 1 1 *\` | Same as @yearly |
| \`@monthly\` | \`0 0 1 * *\` | Once a month (1st, midnight) |
| \`@weekly\` | \`0 0 * * 0\` | Once a week (Sunday, midnight) |
| \`@daily\` | \`0 0 * * *\` | Once a day (midnight) |
| \`@midnight\` | \`0 0 * * *\` | Same as @daily |
| \`@hourly\` | \`0 * * * *\` | Once an hour |

\`\`\`bash
# Examples
@daily /usr/local/bin/backup.sh
@reboot /usr/local/bin/startup-script.sh
@hourly /usr/local/bin/check-services.sh
\`\`\`

### Part 3: Managing Crontabs

#### User Crontabs

\`\`\`bash
# Edit your crontab
crontab -e

# List your crontab
crontab -l

# Remove your crontab
crontab -r

# Remove with confirmation
crontab -i -r
\`\`\`

#### Root Managing Other Users

\`\`\`bash
# Edit user's crontab (as root)
crontab -e -u username

# List user's crontab
crontab -l -u username

# Remove user's crontab
crontab -r -u username
\`\`\`

#### Crontab Locations

\`\`\`bash
# User crontabs
/var/spool/cron/username          # RHEL/CentOS
/var/spool/cron/crontabs/username # Debian/Ubuntu

# System crontabs
/etc/crontab                      # Main system crontab
/etc/cron.d/                      # Drop-in crontab files
/etc/cron.hourly/                 # Hourly scripts
/etc/cron.daily/                  # Daily scripts
/etc/cron.weekly/                 # Weekly scripts
/etc/cron.monthly/                # Monthly scripts
\`\`\`

### Part 4: Production Examples

#### Example 1: Database Backup

\`\`\`bash
# /etc/cron.d/db-backup
# Daily database backup at 2 AM
0 2 * * * postgres /usr/local/bin/backup-postgres.sh

# backup-postgres.sh
#!/bin/bash
DATE=$(date +\%Y-\%m-\%d)
BACKUP_DIR=/backup/postgres
mkdir -p $BACKUP_DIR

pg_dump mydb | gzip > $BACKUP_DIR/mydb-$DATE.sql.gz

# Keep only last 7 days
find $BACKUP_DIR -name "*.sql.gz" -mtime +7 -delete

# Log
echo "$(date): Backup completed" >> /var/log/db-backup.log
\`\`\`

#### Example 2: Log Rotation (Custom)

\`\`\`bash
# /etc/cron.d/log-rotation
# Rotate logs daily at 1 AM
0 1 * * * root /usr/local/bin/rotate-app-logs.sh

# rotate-app-logs.sh
#!/bin/bash
LOG_DIR=/var/log/myapp
DATE=$(date +\%Y\%m\%d)

# Rotate main log
mv $LOG_DIR/app.log $LOG_DIR/app-$DATE.log
touch $LOG_DIR/app.log
chmod 644 $LOG_DIR/app.log

# Compress old logs
gzip $LOG_DIR/app-$DATE.log

# Delete logs older than 30 days
find $LOG_DIR -name "app-*.log.gz" -mtime +30 -delete

# Reload app to reopen log file
systemctl reload myapp
\`\`\`

#### Example 3: Certificate Renewal

\`\`\`bash
# /etc/cron.d/certbot-renew
# Check for certificate renewal twice daily
0 0,12 * * * root certbot renew --quiet --post-hook "systemctl reload nginx"
\`\`\`

#### Example 4: Monitoring & Alerts

\`\`\`bash
# /etc/cron.d/monitoring
# Check disk space every 5 minutes
*/5 * * * * root /usr/local/bin/check-disk.sh

# check-disk.sh
#!/bin/bash
THRESHOLD=90
ALERT_EMAIL="admin@example.com"

df -H | grep -vE '^Filesystem|tmpfs|cdrom' | awk '{ print $5 " " $6 }' | while read output; do
  USAGE=$(echo $output | awk '{ print $1}' | sed 's/%//g')
  PARTITION=$(echo $output | awk '{ print $2 }')

  if [ $USAGE -ge $THRESHOLD ]; then
    echo "Disk $PARTITION is at $USAGE%" | mail -s "Disk Alert: $HOSTNAME" $ALERT_EMAIL
  fi
done
\`\`\`

#### Example 5: AWS S3 Sync

\`\`\`bash
# /etc/cron.d/s3-sync
# Sync to S3 every hour
0 * * * * backup /usr/local/bin/sync-to-s3.sh

# sync-to-s3.sh
#!/bin/bash
SOURCE=/var/www/uploads
BUCKET=s3://my-backup-bucket/uploads
LOG=/var/log/s3-sync.log

echo "$(date): Starting S3 sync" >> $LOG
aws s3 sync $SOURCE $BUCKET --delete >> $LOG 2>&1
echo "$(date): Sync completed" >> $LOG
\`\`\`

### Part 5: Environment & Best Practices

#### Cron Environment

Cron runs with minimal environment:
- PATH is usually just \`/usr/bin:/bin\`
- No shell variables from .bashrc, .profile
- HOME is set to user's home
- SHELL is /bin/sh (not bash!)

\`\`\`bash
# Set environment in crontab
PATH=/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin
SHELL=/bin/bash
MAILTO=admin@example.com

# Or source profile
0 2 * * * . ~/.bash_profile; /home/user/script.sh

# Or use full paths
0 2 * * * /usr/local/bin/python3 /opt/app/script.py
\`\`\`

#### Output & Logging

\`\`\`bash
# Suppress all output
* * * * * command > /dev/null 2>&1

# Log stdout and stderr
* * * * * command >> /var/log/cron.log 2>&1

# Separate stdout and stderr
* * * * * command >> /var/log/cron.log 2>> /var/log/cron-error.log

# Send output to email (set MAILTO)
MAILTO=admin@example.com
* * * * * command

# Disable email
MAILTO=""
* * * * * command
\`\`\`

#### Best Practices

1. **Always use absolute paths**
   \`\`\`bash
   # Wrong
   0 2 * * * backup.sh

   # Right
   0 2 * * * /usr/local/bin/backup.sh
   \`\`\`

2. **Make scripts idempotent**
   \`\`\`bash
   # Check if already running
   LOCKFILE=/var/run/backup.lock
   if [ -f $LOCKFILE ]; then
     echo "Backup already running"
     exit 1
   fi
   touch $LOCKFILE
   trap "rm -f $LOCKFILE" EXIT

   # Do backup...
   \`\`\`

3. **Log everything**
   \`\`\`bash
   LOG=/var/log/backup.log
   echo "$(date): Starting backup" >> $LOG
   # ... backup commands ...
   echo "$(date): Backup completed" >> $LOG
   \`\`\`

4. **Handle errors**
   \`\`\`bash
   #!/bin/bash
   set -e  # Exit on error
   set -o pipefail  # Catch errors in pipes

   # Or explicit error handling
   if ! backup-command; then
     echo "Backup failed" | mail -s "Backup Error" admin@example.com
     exit 1
   fi
   \`\`\`

5. **Use flock for mutual exclusion**
   \`\`\`bash
   # Prevent overlapping executions
   * * * * * flock -n /var/run/backup.lock /usr/local/bin/backup.sh
   \`\`\`

### Part 6: Systemd Timers (Modern Alternative)

#### Why Systemd Timers?

**Advantages over cron:**
- Better logging (journalctl)
- Dependencies (wait for network, services)
- Resource limits (CPU, memory)
- More flexible scheduling
- Missed runs can be caught up

#### Timer Example

\`\`\`ini
# /etc/systemd/system/backup.timer
[Unit]
Description=Daily Backup Timer

[Timer]
OnCalendar=daily
OnCalendar=*-*-* 02:00:00
Persistent=true
Unit=backup.service

[Install]
WantedBy=timers.target
\`\`\`

\`\`\`ini
# /etc/systemd/system/backup.service
[Unit]
Description=Daily Backup Service
After=network-online.target
Wants=network-online.target

[Service]
Type=oneshot
ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/backup.sh
User=backup
Group=backup
StandardOutput=journal
StandardError=journal
\`\`\`

\`\`\`bash
# Enable and start timer
systemctl enable backup.timer
systemctl start backup.timer

# Check status
systemctl status backup.timer
systemctl list-timers

# View logs
journalctl -u backup.service
journalctl -u backup.service --since today
\`\`\`

#### OnCalendar Syntax

\`\`\`bash
# Common patterns
OnCalendar=daily              # Every day at midnight
OnCalendar=weekly             # Every Monday at midnight
OnCalendar=monthly            # 1st of month at midnight
OnCalendar=*-*-* 02:00:00     # Every day at 2 AM
OnCalendar=Mon,Fri 10:00      # Monday and Friday at 10 AM
OnCalendar=*-*-1 00:00:00     # First of every month
OnCalendar=*-01,07-01 00:00   # Jan 1 and July 1

# Multiple times
OnCalendar=*-*-* 02:00:00
OnCalendar=*-*-* 14:00:00

# Test syntax
systemd-analyze calendar "Mon,Fri 10:00"
systemd-analyze calendar daily
\`\`\`

### Part 7: Anacron

**Anacron** = for systems that don't run 24/7 (laptops, desktops)

Differs from cron:
- Runs missed jobs on boot
- Doesn't assume system is always on
- Period specified in days

\`\`\`bash
# /etc/anacrontab
# period  delay  job-identifier  command
1         5      cron.daily      run-parts /etc/cron.daily
7         10     cron.weekly     run-parts /etc/cron.weekly
30        15     cron.monthly    run-parts /etc/cron.monthly

# period: days between runs
# delay: minutes to wait after boot
\`\`\`

### Part 8: at Command (One-Time Jobs)

\`\`\`bash
# Schedule one-time job
at 10:00 PM
at> /usr/local/bin/backup.sh
at> Ctrl+D

# Schedule with time spec
at now + 1 hour
at 2:30 AM tomorrow
at noon tomorrow
at 10:00 AM 07/20/2026

# List jobs
atq
at -l

# Remove job
atrm 1
at -r 1

# View job details
at -c 1
\`\`\`

### Part 9: Troubleshooting Cron

\`\`\`bash
# Check cron service
systemctl status crond
systemctl status cron

# View cron logs
tail -f /var/log/cron           # RHEL/CentOS
journalctl -u cron -f           # Systemd systems
grep CRON /var/log/syslog       # Ubuntu/Debian

# Check mail (cron output)
mail
cat /var/mail/$USER

# Test cron job manually
sudo -u username /path/to/script.sh

# Verify crontab syntax
crontab -l | grep -v '^#'

# Check access
cat /etc/cron.allow
cat /etc/cron.deny
\`\`\`

### Common Issues

| Problem | Solution |
|---------|----------|
| Job not running | Check cron service, syntax, permissions |
| Environment issues | Use absolute paths, set PATH in crontab |
| Script works manually but not in cron | Source .bash_profile or set environment |
| No email notifications | Check MAILTO, check mail service |
| Overlapping executions | Use flock or check for lockfile |

### Production Checklist

- [ ] Use absolute paths in all cron jobs
- [ ] Implement proper logging
- [ ] Add error handling and notifications
- [ ] Prevent overlapping executions (flock)
- [ ] Rotate cron job logs
- [ ] Test jobs manually before adding to cron
- [ ] Document cron jobs in /etc/cron.d/ with comments
- [ ] Consider systemd timers for new deployments
- [ ] Monitor cron job execution (e.g., Dead Man's Snitch)
        `,
        keyPoints: [
          "Crontab format: minute hour day month weekday command",
          "Use /etc/cron.d/ for system-wide scheduled tasks",
          "Systemd timers — modern alternative with better logging",
          "Anacron — for systems that don't run 24/7",
          "at — one-time scheduled tasks",
          "Always use absolute paths in cron jobs",
          "flock prevents overlapping executions"
        ]
