        title: "Boot Process & Systemd Troubleshooting",
        type: "theory",
        duration: "35 min",
        content: `
## Linux Boot Process & Systemd Troubleshooting

### Part 1: Boot Process Overview

#### The Boot Sequence

\`\`\`
1. BIOS/UEFI
   ↓
2. Bootloader (GRUB2)
   ↓
3. Kernel
   ↓
4. initramfs (initial RAM filesystem)
   ↓
5. systemd (PID 1)
   ↓
6. Targets (runlevels)
   ↓
7. Login
\`\`\`

### Part 2: BIOS/UEFI

#### BIOS (Legacy)

- **BIOS** = Basic Input/Output System
- Firmware interface between hardware and OS
- Reads MBR (Master Boot Record) — first 512 bytes
- Loads bootloader from MBR

#### UEFI (Modern)

- **UEFI** = Unified Extensible Firmware Interface
- Replaces BIOS
- Uses GPT (GUID Partition Table) instead of MBR
- EFI System Partition (ESP) — /boot/efi
- Secure Boot support

\`\`\`bash
# Check if system is UEFI or BIOS
[ -d /sys/firmware/efi ] && echo "UEFI" || echo "BIOS"

# View UEFI boot entries
efibootmgr -v

# ESP mount point
ls -la /boot/efi/
\`\`\`

### Part 3: GRUB2 Bootloader

#### GRUB2 Configuration

\`\`\`bash
# Main config file (DO NOT edit directly!)
/boot/grub2/grub.cfg          # RHEL/CentOS (BIOS)
/boot/efi/EFI/redhat/grub.cfg # RHEL/CentOS (UEFI)
/boot/grub/grub.cfg           # Ubuntu/Debian

# User-editable configs
/etc/default/grub             # Main settings
/etc/grub.d/                  # Scripts that generate grub.cfg

# After editing /etc/default/grub, regenerate:
grub2-mkconfig -o /boot/grub2/grub.cfg              # RHEL/CentOS (BIOS)
grub2-mkconfig -o /boot/efi/EFI/redhat/grub.cfg     # RHEL/CentOS (UEFI)
update-grub                                          # Ubuntu/Debian
\`\`\`

#### Common GRUB Settings

\`\`\`bash
# /etc/default/grub

GRUB_TIMEOUT=5                # Seconds to wait for selection
GRUB_DEFAULT=0                # Default kernel (0=first, saved=remember last)
GRUB_DISABLE_SUBMENU=true     # Flat menu instead of submenu
GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX="crashkernel=auto rhgb quiet"  # Kernel parameters

# Remove 'quiet' to see boot messages
GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX="crashkernel=auto rhgb"

# Don't forget to regenerate!
grub2-mkconfig -o /boot/grub2/grub.cfg
\`\`\`

#### GRUB Boot Menu — Interactive

**At boot time, press Esc or Shift to enter GRUB menu:**

- **e** — edit boot parameters (temporary)
- **c** — GRUB command line
- **Enter** — boot selected entry

**Common kernel parameters:**

\`\`\`bash
# Single user mode (root shell, no password)
systemd.unit=rescue.target
# or on older systems:
single

# Emergency mode (minimal system)
systemd.unit=emergency.target

# Change root password (if forgotten)
# 1. Press 'e' on kernel entry
# 2. Find line starting with 'linux' or 'linux16'
# 3. Add at end: rd.break
# 4. Press Ctrl+X to boot
# 5. At prompt:
mount -o remount,rw /sysroot
chroot /sysroot
passwd root
touch /.autorelabel  # SELinux relabel on next boot
exit
exit

# Disable SELinux temporarily
selinux=0

# Specify alternate root
root=/dev/sda2
\`\`\`

### Part 4: Kernel & initramfs

#### Kernel

\`\`\`bash
# List installed kernels
rpm -qa | grep kernel        # RHEL/CentOS
dpkg -l | grep linux-image   # Ubuntu/Debian

# Kernel files
ls -lh /boot/vmlinuz-*

# Current running kernel
uname -r
uname -a

# Kernel ring buffer (boot messages)
dmesg
dmesg | less
dmesg | grep -i error
journalctl -k  # Kernel messages from current boot
journalctl -k -b -1  # Previous boot
\`\`\`

#### initramfs (Initial RAM Filesystem)

- Temporary root filesystem in RAM
- Contains drivers needed to mount real root filesystem
- Decompresses and loads kernel modules

\`\`\`bash
# initramfs files
ls -lh /boot/initramfs-*      # RHEL/CentOS
ls -lh /boot/initrd.img-*     # Ubuntu/Debian

# Rebuild initramfs (if corrupted or after driver changes)
dracut -f                     # RHEL/CentOS (force rebuild current)
dracut -f /boot/initramfs-$(uname -r).img $(uname -r)
update-initramfs -u           # Ubuntu/Debian

# View initramfs contents
lsinitrd /boot/initramfs-$(uname -r).img
lsinitrd | grep -i driver
\`\`\`

### Part 5: Systemd Init (PID 1)

#### Systemd Targets (Runlevels)

| Old Runlevel | Systemd Target | Purpose |
|--------------|----------------|---------|
| 0 | poweroff.target | Shutdown |
| 1, S | rescue.target | Single-user mode |
| 2, 3, 4 | multi-user.target | Multi-user, no GUI |
| 5 | graphical.target | Multi-user with GUI |
| 6 | reboot.target | Reboot |

\`\`\`bash
# Current target
systemctl get-default

# Change default target
systemctl set-default multi-user.target
systemctl set-default graphical.target

# Switch to different target (current session)
systemctl isolate rescue.target
systemctl isolate multi-user.target

# Reboot/shutdown via targets
systemctl isolate reboot.target
systemctl isolate poweroff.target
\`\`\`

#### Boot Analysis

\`\`\`bash
# Boot time analysis
systemd-analyze
# Output: Startup finished in 2.1s (kernel) + 8.3s (userspace) = 10.4s

# Blame — slowest services
systemd-analyze blame

# Critical chain — dependency tree
systemd-analyze critical-chain

# Critical chain for specific service
systemd-analyze critical-chain nginx.service

# Plot boot process (generates SVG)
systemd-analyze plot > boot.svg

# Verify unit file
systemd-analyze verify nginx.service
\`\`\`

### Part 6: Rescue & Emergency Modes

#### Rescue Mode (rescue.target)

- Single-user mode
- Minimal services
- Root filesystem mounted
- Requires root password

\`\`\`bash
# Boot into rescue mode
systemctl rescue

# Or from GRUB, add kernel parameter:
systemd.unit=rescue.target

# Exit rescue mode
systemctl default
# or
exit
\`\`\`

#### Emergency Mode (emergency.target)

- Even more minimal than rescue
- Root filesystem mounted read-only
- Almost no services
- No networking

\`\`\`bash
# Boot into emergency mode
systemctl emergency

# Or from GRUB:
systemd.unit=emergency.target

# Root is read-only, remount:
mount -o remount,rw /
\`\`\`

### Part 7: Common Boot Problems

#### Problem 1: Kernel Panic

**Symptoms:** System freezes at boot with "Kernel panic"

**Solutions:**
\`\`\`bash
# 1. Boot with older kernel (select in GRUB menu)

# 2. Boot with minimal parameters
# In GRUB, edit kernel line, remove: rhgb quiet
# Add: systemd.unit=rescue.target

# 3. Rebuild initramfs
mount -o remount,rw /
dracut -f
reboot

# 4. Check kernel logs
journalctl -k -b -1  # Previous boot
\`\`\`

#### Problem 2: Forgotten Root Password

\`\`\`bash
# 1. Reboot, press 'e' in GRUB on kernel entry
# 2. Find line starting with 'linux' or 'linux16'
# 3. Add at end: rd.break
# 4. Press Ctrl+X to boot
# 5. Commands:
mount -o remount,rw /sysroot
chroot /sysroot
passwd root
touch /.autorelabel  # IMPORTANT for SELinux!
exit
exit
# System will reboot and relabel (takes time)
\`\`\`

#### Problem 3: /etc/fstab Error

**Symptoms:** Boot fails with "cannot mount filesystem" or drops to emergency mode

**Solution:**
\`\`\`bash
# 1. Boot into emergency mode (systemd.unit=emergency.target)
# 2. Remount root
mount -o remount,rw /

# 3. Check /etc/fstab for errors
cat /etc/fstab

# 4. Comment out problematic line
vi /etc/fstab
# (add # at beginning of bad line)

# 5. Reboot
systemctl reboot

# Common fstab errors:
# - Wrong UUID
# - Filesystem doesn't exist
# - Wrong mount options
# - Typos

# Verify UUID
blkid
lsblk -f
\`\`\`

#### Problem 4: Service Fails to Start

\`\`\`bash
# View failed services
systemctl --failed

# Detailed status
systemctl status nginx.service

# View logs
journalctl -u nginx.service
journalctl -u nginx.service -b  # Current boot only
journalctl -u nginx.service --since "10 minutes ago"

# Follow logs in real-time
journalctl -u nginx.service -f

# Reset failed state
systemctl reset-failed nginx.service
\`\`\`

#### Problem 5: System Hangs During Boot

\`\`\`bash
# 1. Add to kernel parameters: systemd.log_level=debug
# 2. Boot and see where it hangs

# Or boot without quiet to see messages:
# Remove 'rhgb quiet' from kernel parameters

# Check for problematic services
systemctl list-dependencies graphical.target
systemctl list-dependencies multi-user.target

# Mask problematic service (prevent from starting)
systemctl mask problematic.service
\`\`\`

### Part 8: Journal & Logs

\`\`\`bash
# Boot logs
journalctl -b                 # Current boot
journalctl -b -1              # Previous boot
journalctl -b -2              # 2 boots ago

# List boots
journalctl --list-boots

# Kernel messages
journalctl -k
journalctl -k -b -1

# Time-based queries
journalctl --since "2026-01-01"
journalctl --since "1 hour ago"
journalctl --since today
journalctl --since "2026-01-01" --until "2026-01-31"

# Priority filtering
journalctl -p err            # Errors only
journalctl -p warning        # Warning and above

# Follow in real-time
journalctl -f
journalctl -u nginx.service -f

# Persistent journal (survives reboot)
mkdir -p /var/log/journal
systemctl restart systemd-journald

# Journal size
journalctl --disk-usage
journalctl --vacuum-size=500M
journalctl --vacuum-time=7d
\`\`\`

### Part 9: Systemd Troubleshooting Toolkit

\`\`\`bash
# Service management
systemctl status nginx
systemctl cat nginx           # View unit file
systemctl show nginx          # All properties
systemctl list-dependencies nginx

# Reload systemd (after editing unit files)
systemctl daemon-reload

# Edit unit file (creates override)
systemctl edit nginx.service  # Creates /etc/systemd/system/nginx.service.d/override.conf
systemctl edit --full nginx.service  # Edits copy in /etc/systemd/system/

# View active configuration (with overrides)
systemctl cat nginx.service

# Dependency analysis
systemd-analyze verify nginx.service
systemd-analyze critical-chain nginx.service

# Resource usage
systemd-cgtop                 # Like top, but for cgroups
systemctl status nginx        # Shows memory/CPU in status

# Show which services are enabled
systemctl list-unit-files --type=service --state=enabled
\`\`\`

### Production Troubleshooting Checklist

**System won't boot:**
- [ ] Try booting with older kernel
- [ ] Boot into rescue/emergency mode
- [ ] Check /etc/fstab for errors
- [ ] Review journalctl -k -b -1 (previous boot)
- [ ] Check disk space (df -h)

**Service fails to start:**
- [ ] systemctl status <service>
- [ ] journalctl -u <service>
- [ ] Check configuration files
- [ ] Verify dependencies (list-dependencies)
- [ ] Check file permissions
- [ ] Review SELinux/AppArmor denials

**Performance issues:**
- [ ] systemd-analyze blame
- [ ] systemd-analyze critical-chain
- [ ] systemd-cgtop
- [ ] Check for failed services (systemctl --failed)

**After system changes:**
- [ ] systemctl daemon-reload (after unit file changes)
- [ ] systemd-analyze verify <unit>
- [ ] Test in non-production first
- [ ] Have rescue plan ready
        `,
        keyPoints: [
          "Boot order: BIOS/UEFI → GRUB → Kernel → initramfs → systemd",
          "rd.break — change root password",
          "rescue.target — single-user mode",
          "emergency.target — minimal mode, root read-only",
          "systemd-analyze — boot performance analysis",
          "journalctl -b -1 — previous boot logs",
          "/etc/fstab errors — common boot failure cause"
        ]
      },
      {
        id: 5,
        title: "Process & Service Lab",
        type: "lab",
        duration: "40 min",
        content: `
## Hands-On Lab: Process & Service Management

### Lab Overview

**Skills practiced:**
- Managing processes with signals
- Creating and managing systemd services
- Configuring cron jobs and systemd timers
- Troubleshooting boot and service issues
- Resource monitoring and limits

**Prerequisites:**
- Access to a RHEL/CentOS/Rocky Linux system (or Ubuntu)
- Root or sudo access
- Basic bash scripting knowledge

---

### Exercise 1: Process Management (10 min)

**Task:** Practice managing processes with different signals and priorities.

\`\`\`bash
# 1. Create a CPU-intensive process
cat > /tmp/cpu-hog.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash
while true; do
  echo "Computing..." > /dev/null
done
EOF
chmod +x /tmp/cpu-hog.sh

# 2. Run it in background with low priority
nice -n 19 /tmp/cpu-hog.sh &
echo $! > /tmp/cpu-hog.pid

# 3. Monitor it
top -p $(cat /tmp/cpu-hog.pid)
# Press 'q' to quit

# 4. Check its nice value
ps -o pid,ni,cmd -p $(cat /tmp/cpu-hog.pid)

# 5. Change priority
sudo renice -n 10 -p $(cat /tmp/cpu-hog.pid)

# 6. Verify change
ps -o pid,ni,cmd -p $(cat /tmp/cpu-hog.pid)

# 7. Send different signals
kill -SIGSTOP $(cat /tmp/cpu-hog.pid)  # Pause
ps -o pid,stat,cmd -p $(cat /tmp/cpu-hog.pid)  # Should show 'T'

kill -SIGCONT $(cat /tmp/cpu-hog.pid)  # Resume
ps -o pid,stat,cmd -p $(cat /tmp/cpu-hog.pid)  # Should show 'R' or 'S'

# 8. Graceful termination
kill $(cat /tmp/cpu-hog.pid)
sleep 2
ps -p $(cat /tmp/cpu-hog.pid) || echo "Process terminated"

# 9. Force kill if still running
kill -9 $(cat /tmp/cpu-hog.pid) 2>/dev/null

# Cleanup
rm -f /tmp/cpu-hog.sh /tmp/cpu-hog.pid
\`\`\`

**Questions:**
1. What's the difference between nice and renice?
2. Why can't you kill a process in D state?
3. When should you use SIGKILL vs SIGTERM?

---

### Exercise 2: Creating a Systemd Service (15 min)

**Task:** Create a custom systemd service that runs a monitoring script.

\`\`\`bash
# 1. Create the monitoring script
sudo tee /usr/local/bin/system-monitor.sh > /dev/null << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash
LOG_FILE=/var/log/system-monitor.log

while true; do
  echo "=== $(date) ===" >> $LOG_FILE
  echo "CPU Load: $(uptime | awk -F'load average:' '{print $2}')" >> $LOG_FILE
  echo "Memory: $(free -h | grep Mem | awk '{print $3 "/" $2}')" >> $LOG_FILE
  echo "Disk: $(df -h / | tail -1 | awk '{print $5}')" >> $LOG_FILE
  echo "" >> $LOG_FILE
  sleep 60
done
EOF

sudo chmod +x /usr/local/bin/system-monitor.sh

# 2. Create the systemd service unit
sudo tee /etc/systemd/system/system-monitor.service > /dev/null << 'EOF'
[Unit]
Description=System Resource Monitor
After=network.target
Documentation=man:system-monitor(8)

[Service]
Type=simple
ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/system-monitor.sh
Restart=on-failure
RestartSec=10
User=root
StandardOutput=journal
StandardError=journal

# Security hardening
PrivateTmp=true
NoNewPrivileges=true
ProtectSystem=strict
ProtectHome=true
ReadWritePaths=/var/log

# Resource limits
MemoryMax=100M
CPUQuota=10%

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
EOF

# 3. Reload systemd and start service
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl start system-monitor.service

# 4. Check status
sudo systemctl status system-monitor.service

# 5. View logs
sudo journalctl -u system-monitor.service -n 20

# 6. Enable auto-start on boot
sudo systemctl enable system-monitor.service

# 7. Test restart behavior
sudo systemctl restart system-monitor.service
sleep 2
sudo systemctl status system-monitor.service

# 8. View output file
sudo tail -20 /var/log/system-monitor.log

# 9. Test failure recovery
PID=$(systemctl show -p MainPID --value system-monitor.service)
sudo kill -9 $PID
sleep 15
sudo systemctl status system-monitor.service  # Should auto-restart

# 10. Stop and disable when done
sudo systemctl stop system-monitor.service
sudo systemctl disable system-monitor.service
\`\`\`

**Challenge Tasks:**
1. Modify the service to run as a non-root user
2. Add email notifications when the service fails
3. Configure the service to start only after network is online

