### Exercise 3: Cron Jobs (10 min)

**Task:** Set up automated log cleanup with cron.

\`\`\`bash
# 1. Create cleanup script
sudo tee /usr/local/bin/log-cleanup.sh > /dev/null << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash
LOG_DIR=/var/log/myapp
ARCHIVE_DIR=/var/log/myapp/archive
DAYS_TO_KEEP=7

# Create directories if they don't exist
mkdir -p $LOG_DIR $ARCHIVE_DIR

# Create test logs if needed
for i in {1..10}; do
  touch -d "$i days ago" $LOG_DIR/app-$(date -d "$i days ago" +\%Y\%m\%d).log
done

# Archive old logs
find $LOG_DIR -name "*.log" -type f -mtime +$DAYS_TO_KEEP -exec mv {} $ARCHIVE_DIR/ \\;

# Compress archived logs
find $ARCHIVE_DIR -name "*.log" -type f ! -name "*.gz" -exec gzip {} \\;

# Delete very old archives (30 days)
find $ARCHIVE_DIR -name "*.gz" -type f -mtime +30 -delete

# Log completion
echo "$(date): Cleanup completed" >> /var/log/log-cleanup.log
EOF

sudo chmod +x /usr/local/bin/log-cleanup.sh

# 2. Test the script
sudo /usr/local/bin/log-cleanup.sh
ls -lh /var/log/myapp/
ls -lh /var/log/myapp/archive/

# 3. Add to cron (runs daily at 2 AM)
echo "0 2 * * * root /usr/local/bin/log-cleanup.sh" | sudo tee /etc/cron.d/log-cleanup

# 4. Verify crontab syntax
cat /etc/cron.d/log-cleanup

# 5. Test cron execution (run immediately)
sudo run-parts --test /etc/cron.d/

# 6. View cron logs
sudo tail -f /var/log/cron  # RHEL/CentOS
# or
sudo journalctl -u cron -f  # Systemd systems
\`\`\`

**Alternative: Systemd Timer**

\`\`\`bash
# Create timer unit
sudo tee /etc/systemd/system/log-cleanup.timer > /dev/null << 'EOF'
[Unit]
Description=Daily Log Cleanup Timer

[Timer]
OnCalendar=daily
OnCalendar=*-*-* 02:00:00
Persistent=true

[Install]
WantedBy=timers.target
EOF

# Create service unit
sudo tee /etc/systemd/system/log-cleanup.service > /dev/null << 'EOF'
[Unit]
Description=Log Cleanup Service

[Service]
Type=oneshot
ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/log-cleanup.sh
StandardOutput=journal
EOF

# Enable and start timer
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl enable --now log-cleanup.timer

# Check timer status
sudo systemctl list-timers log-cleanup.timer
sudo systemctl status log-cleanup.timer

# Trigger manually (for testing)
sudo systemctl start log-cleanup.service
sudo journalctl -u log-cleanup.service
\`\`\`

