## Network Configuration & Troubleshooting — Production Guide

### Part 1: Network Basics

#### OSI Model (Quick Review)

| Layer | Name | Protocol Examples | Linux Tools |
|-------|------|------------------|-------------|
| 7 | Application | HTTP, DNS, SSH | curl, dig, ssh |
| 6 | Presentation | SSL/TLS | openssl |
| 5 | Session | NetBIOS | - |
| 4 | Transport | TCP, UDP | ss, netstat |
| 3 | Network | IP, ICMP | ip, ping, traceroute |
| 2 | Data Link | Ethernet, ARP | ip link, arp |
| 1 | Physical | Cables, NICs | ethtool, mii-tool |

### Part 2: IP Command — Modern Network Configuration

#### Interface Management

\`\`\`bash
# View all interfaces
ip addr show
ip a                     # Short form
ip -c a                  # Colorized output
ip -br addr              # Brief format

# Specific interface
ip addr show dev eth0
ip addr show dev ens33

# Show only IPv4 or IPv6
ip -4 addr
ip -6 addr

# Interface statistics
ip -s link               # Packet statistics
ip -s -s link            # Detailed statistics

# MAC addresses
ip link show
ip link show dev eth0
\`\`\`

#### IP Address Configuration

\`\`\`bash
# Add IP address (temporary - lost on reboot)
ip addr add 192.168.1.100/24 dev eth0
ip addr add 10.0.0.50/8 dev eth0 label eth0:1  # With label (alias)

# Add with broadcast
ip addr add 192.168.1.100/24 brd 192.168.1.255 dev eth0

# Delete IP address
ip addr del 192.168.1.100/24 dev eth0

# Flush all IPs from interface
ip addr flush dev eth0
ip addr flush dev eth0 scope global  # Only global IPs
\`\`\`

#### Link (Interface) Management

\`\`\`bash
# Bring interface up/down
ip link set eth0 up
ip link set eth0 down

# Change MTU
ip link set eth0 mtu 9000      # Jumbo frames

# Change MAC address (spoofing)
ip link set eth0 down
ip link set eth0 address 00:11:22:33:44:55
ip link set eth0 up

# Enable/disable promiscuous mode
ip link set eth0 promisc on
ip link set eth0 promisc off

# Show link status
ip link show dev eth0
ethtool eth0             # Detailed NIC info
\`\`\`

### Part 3: Routing

#### View Routes

\`\`\`bash
# Show routing table
ip route show
ip r                     # Short form
ip -c route              # Colorized

# Show only default route
ip route show default

# Show route to specific destination
ip route get 8.8.8.8
ip route get google.com

# IPv6 routes
ip -6 route show
\`\`\`

#### Manage Routes

\`\`\`bash
# Add default gateway
ip route add default via 192.168.1.1
ip route add default via 192.168.1.1 dev eth0

# Add static route
ip route add 10.0.0.0/8 via 192.168.1.254
ip route add 172.16.0.0/12 via 192.168.1.253 dev eth0

# Add route with metric (priority)
ip route add default via 192.168.1.1 metric 100
ip route add default via 192.168.2.1 metric 200  # Backup gateway

# Delete route
ip route del 10.0.0.0/8
ip route del default via 192.168.1.1

# Replace route
ip route replace default via 192.168.1.2
\`\`\`

#### Production Routing Examples

\`\`\`bash
# Multi-path routing (load balancing)
ip route add default \\
  nexthop via 192.168.1.1 weight 1 \\
  nexthop via 192.168.2.1 weight 1

# Route specific subnet through VPN
ip route add 10.0.0.0/8 via 10.8.0.1 dev tun0

# Policy-based routing (route based on source IP)
ip rule add from 192.168.1.0/24 table 100
ip route add default via 192.168.1.1 table 100
\`\`\`

### Part 4: ss Command — Socket Statistics

**ss** replaces netstat, faster and more powerful.

#### Basic Usage

\`\`\`bash
# All TCP sockets
ss -t

# All UDP sockets
ss -u

# All listening sockets
ss -l

# Combine: TCP + UDP, listening + established
ss -tuln             # Most common: TCP/UDP, listening, numeric
ss -tulnp            # Include process info (needs root)

# All sockets (including UNIX)
ss -a

# Show summary statistics
ss -s
\`\`\`

#### Filtering

\`\`\`bash
# Specific port
ss -tuln sport = :80
ss -tuln dport = :3306
ss -tuln '( sport = :80 or sport = :443 )'

# Specific state
ss -t state established
ss -t state listening
ss -t state time-wait

# Source/destination IP
ss -t dst 8.8.8.8
ss -t src 192.168.1.0/24

# Process info
ss -tulnp | grep nginx
ss -tulnp | grep :22
\`\`\`

#### Production Examples

\`\`\`bash
# Find which process is using port 80
ss -tulnp | grep :80
# or
lsof -i :80

# Count connections by state
ss -tan | awk '{print $1}' | sort | uniq -c

# Find processes with most connections
ss -tunp | awk '{print $NF}' | grep -oP 'pid=\\K[0-9]+' | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn

# Check for TIME_WAIT buildup (DDoS indicator)
ss -tan | grep TIME_WAIT | wc -l

# Monitor new connections in real-time
watch -n 1 'ss -tan | grep ESTAB | wc -l'

# All listening services
ss -tulnp | grep LISTEN
\`\`\`

### Part 5: DNS Configuration

#### /etc/resolv.conf

\`\`\`bash
# View current DNS servers
cat /etc/resolv.conf

# Example configuration
nameserver 8.8.8.8          # Google DNS (primary)
nameserver 8.8.4.4          # Google DNS (secondary)
nameserver 1.1.1.1          # Cloudflare DNS
search example.com corp.local  # Search domains
options timeout:2 attempts:3   # Options
\`\`\`

**Important:** Many systems use NetworkManager or systemd-resolved, which auto-generate /etc/resolv.conf.

\`\`\`bash
# Check if resolv.conf is managed
ls -la /etc/resolv.conf
# If symlink to /run/systemd/resolve/stub-resolv.conf → systemd-resolved
# If symlink to /var/run/NetworkManager/resolv.conf → NetworkManager

# systemd-resolved configuration
cat /etc/systemd/resolved.conf
systemctl status systemd-resolved
resolvectl status

# NetworkManager DNS
nmcli device show eth0 | grep DNS
\`\`\`

#### DNS Testing Tools

\`\`\`bash
# dig — most detailed
dig google.com
dig google.com +short        # Just the IP
dig google.com A             # A record only
dig google.com MX            # MX records
dig google.com NS            # Nameservers
dig @8.8.8.8 google.com      # Use specific DNS server
dig google.com +trace        # Full resolution path

# nslookup — simple
nslookup google.com
nslookup google.com 8.8.8.8

# host — quick
host google.com
host -t MX google.com
host -a google.com           # All records

# getent (uses system resolver, not just DNS)
getent hosts google.com
getent ahosts google.com
\`\`\`

#### DNS Troubleshooting

\`\`\`bash
# Test different DNS servers
dig @8.8.8.8 example.com          # Google
dig @1.1.1.1 example.com          # Cloudflare
dig @208.67.222.222 example.com   # OpenDNS

# Check for DNS propagation
dig example.com @ns1.example.com  # Check authoritative NS

# Debug DNS resolution
dig +trace example.com

# Check reverse DNS
dig -x 8.8.8.8

# Test DNSSEC
dig example.com +dnssec

# Clear DNS cache (systemd-resolved)
resolvectl flush-caches

# Clear DNS cache (nscd)
systemctl restart nscd
\`\`\`

### Part 6: Persistent Network Configuration

#### NetworkManager (Modern)

**Used by:** RHEL 8+, CentOS 8+, Ubuntu 18.04+

\`\`\`bash
# List connections
nmcli connection show
nmcli con show

# Show active connections
nmcli connection show --active

# Device status
nmcli device status
nmcli dev status

# Add static IP
nmcli con add type ethernet con-name eth0-static ifname eth0 \\
  ipv4.addresses 192.168.1.100/24 \\
  ipv4.gateway 192.168.1.1 \\
  ipv4.dns "8.8.8.8 8.8.4.4" \\
  ipv4.method manual

# Modify existing connection
nmcli con mod eth0 ipv4.addresses 192.168.1.200/24
nmcli con mod eth0 ipv4.dns "1.1.1.1 1.0.0.1"

# DHCP configuration
nmcli con add type ethernet con-name eth0-dhcp ifname eth0 \\
  ipv4.method auto

# Apply changes
nmcli con up eth0
nmcli con reload

# Delete connection
nmcli con delete eth0-static
\`\`\`

#### ifcfg Files (Legacy RHEL/CentOS)

\`\`\`bash
# /etc/sysconfig/network-scripts/ifcfg-eth0

# Static IP
TYPE=Ethernet
BOOTPROTO=none
NAME=eth0
DEVICE=eth0
ONBOOT=yes
IPADDR=192.168.1.100
PREFIX=24
GATEWAY=192.168.1.1
DNS1=8.8.8.8
DNS2=8.8.4.4

# DHCP
TYPE=Ethernet
BOOTPROTO=dhcp
NAME=eth0
DEVICE=eth0
ONBOOT=yes

# Restart network
systemctl restart NetworkManager
# or
systemctl restart network  # Older systems
\`\`\`

#### Netplan (Ubuntu 18.04+)

\`\`\`yaml
# /etc/netplan/01-netcfg.yaml
network:
  version: 2
  renderer: networkd  # or NetworkManager
  ethernets:
    eth0:
      dhcp4: true

    eth1:
      addresses:
        - 192.168.1.100/24
      gateway4: 192.168.1.1
      nameservers:
        addresses:
          - 8.8.8.8
          - 8.8.4.4
        search:
          - example.com
\`\`\`

\`\`\`bash
# Apply netplan
netplan apply

# Test before applying (30s timeout)
netplan try

# Debug
netplan --debug generate
netplan --debug apply
\`\`\`

### Part 7: Network Troubleshooting Tools

#### Connectivity Testing

\`\`\`bash
# ping — ICMP echo
ping -c 4 google.com         # 4 packets
ping -i 0.2 -c 100 8.8.8.8   # Fast ping (200ms interval)
ping -f 8.8.8.8              # Flood ping (root only, testing)
ping -s 1472 8.8.8.8         # MTU testing (1472 + 28 = 1500)

# traceroute — path to destination
traceroute google.com
traceroute -n google.com     # No DNS resolution
traceroute -I google.com     # ICMP instead of UDP
traceroute -T -p 80 google.com  # TCP SYN to port 80

# mtr — combined ping + traceroute (live)
mtr google.com
mtr -r -c 10 google.com      # Report mode, 10 cycles
mtr -n google.com            # No DNS
\`\`\`

#### Port & Service Testing

\`\`\`bash
# telnet — TCP connection test
telnet google.com 80
# Type: GET / HTTP/1.0 and press Enter twice

# nc (netcat) — Swiss army knife
nc -zv google.com 80         # TCP port scan
nc -zuv google.com 53        # UDP port scan
nc -zv 192.168.1.100 1-1000  # Port range scan
nc -l 8080                   # Listen on port 8080
nc -l -u 514                 # Listen UDP (syslog)

# curl — HTTP testing
curl -v http://example.com
curl -I http://example.com   # Headers only
curl -o /dev/null -s -w 'Time: %{time_total}s\\n' http://example.com
curl --resolve example.com:80:1.2.3.4 http://example.com  # Test specific IP

# nmap — port scanner
nmap google.com
nmap -p 1-65535 192.168.1.100  # All ports
nmap -sV 192.168.1.0/24        # Service version detection
nmap -O 192.168.1.1            # OS detection
\`\`\`

#### ARP (Address Resolution Protocol)

\`\`\`bash
# View ARP cache
ip neigh show
ip n
arp -a                       # Legacy command

# Add static ARP entry
ip neigh add 192.168.1.1 lladdr 00:11:22:33:44:55 dev eth0

# Delete ARP entry
ip neigh del 192.168.1.1 dev eth0

# Flush ARP cache
ip neigh flush dev eth0
ip neigh flush all
\`\`\`

### Part 8: Production Scenarios

#### Scenario 1: Dual NIC Server (Separate Networks)

\`\`\`bash
# eth0 — public (internet)
ip addr add 203.0.113.10/24 dev eth0
ip route add default via 203.0.113.1 dev eth0

# eth1 — private (internal)
ip addr add 10.0.1.10/24 dev eth1
ip route add 10.0.0.0/8 via 10.0.1.1 dev eth1
\`\`\`

#### Scenario 2: AWS EC2 Secondary IP

\`\`\`bash
# Add secondary private IP
ip addr add 10.0.1.20/24 dev eth0 label eth0:1

# Persistent (add to ifcfg-eth0:1 or use nmcli)
nmcli con mod eth0 +ipv4.addresses 10.0.1.20/24
\`\`\`

#### Scenario 3: Network Unreachable Troubleshooting

\`\`\`bash
# 1. Check interface
ip link show eth0
# If DOWN: ip link set eth0 up

# 2. Check IP address
ip addr show eth0
# If no IP: DHCP or static config

# 3. Check default gateway
ip route show default
# If none: ip route add default via 192.168.1.1

# 4. Test gateway
ping -c 4 192.168.1.1

# 5. Test DNS
ping -c 4 8.8.8.8

# 6. Test DNS resolution
nslookup google.com

# 7. Check firewall
firewall-cmd --list-all
\`\`\`

### Best Practices

1. **Always test before making permanent**
   \`\`\`bash
   # Temporary change (lost on reboot)
   ip addr add 192.168.1.100/24 dev eth0
   # Test it!
   # Then make permanent with nmcli/netplan
   \`\`\`

2. **Use /32 for single IPs in scripts**
   \`\`\`bash
   ip addr add 10.0.1.5/32 dev lo  # No broadcast
   \`\`\`

3. **Monitor network performance**
   \`\`\`bash
   iftop -i eth0        # Real-time bandwidth
   nethogs              # Per-process bandwidth
   nload eth0           # Real-time graph
   \`\`\`

4. **Document network changes**
   \`\`\`bash
   # Always comment configs
   # /etc/sysconfig/network-scripts/ifcfg-eth0
   # Added by: admin on 2026-02-01 for project XYZ
   \`\`\`

### Production Checklist

- [ ] Document current network configuration
- [ ] Test changes in non-production first
- [ ] Have console access (out-of-band) ready
- [ ] Keep backup of network configs
- [ ] Set up monitoring (interface down alerts)
- [ ] Configure proper DNS (min 2 nameservers)
- [ ] Test failover scenarios (gateway, DNS)
        `,
        keyPoints: [
          "ip addr — modern interface configuration",
          "ip route — routing table management",
          "ss -tulnp — socket statistics with process info",
          "nmcli — NetworkManager command-line",
          "dig/nslookup — DNS troubleshooting",
          "/etc/resolv.conf — DNS configuration (often auto-generated)",
          "Always test network changes before making permanent"
        ]
      },
      {
        id: 2,
        title: "Firewall Configuration — firewalld & iptables",
        type: "theory",
        duration: "55 min",
        content: `
## Linux Firewall — Production Guide

### Part 1: Firewall Overview

#### netfilter Framework

Linux firewall is based on **netfilter** (kernel framework).

**User-space tools:**
- **iptables** — traditional, low-level
- **firewalld** — modern, high-level (RHEL/CentOS/Fedora)
- **ufw** — simple frontend (Ubuntu)
- **nftables** — modern replacement for iptables

### Part 2: firewalld (RHEL/CentOS/Rocky/Alma)

#### Concepts

**Zones** = trust level for network connections

| Zone | Use Case | Default Behavior |
|------|----------|------------------|
| drop | Paranoid | Drop all incoming |
| block | Very restrictive | Reject all incoming with icmp-host-prohibited |
| public | Default, untrusted | Reject incoming except selected |
| external | NAT/masquerading | Reject incoming except selected |
| dmz | DMZ servers | Reject incoming except selected |
| work | Work networks | Reject incoming except selected |
| home | Home networks | Reject incoming except selected |
| internal | Internal networks | Reject incoming except selected |
| trusted | Full trust | Accept all |

#### Basic Commands

\`\`\`bash
# Service status
systemctl status firewalld
systemctl enable --now firewalld
firewall-cmd --state

# Get information
firewall-cmd --get-zones                # All zones
firewall-cmd --get-default-zone         # Default zone (usually public)
firewall-cmd --get-active-zones         # Active zones
firewall-cmd --list-all                 # Current zone config
firewall-cmd --list-all --zone=public   # Specific zone

# Reload firewall
firewall-cmd --reload                   # Reload rules (keeps state)
firewall-cmd --complete-reload          # Full restart (drops state)
\`\`\`

#### Zone Management

\`\`\`bash
# Set default zone
firewall-cmd --set-default-zone=public

# Assign interface to zone
firewall-cmd --zone=internal --change-interface=eth1 --permanent

# List zone assignments
firewall-cmd --get-zone-of-interface=eth0
\`\`\`

#### Service Management

\`\`\`bash
# List available services
firewall-cmd --get-services

# View service definition
firewall-cmd --info-service=http
cat /usr/lib/firewalld/services/http.xml

# Add service (temporary - runtime only)
firewall-cmd --add-service=http
firewall-cmd --add-service=https

# Add service (permanent)
firewall-cmd --add-service=http --permanent
firewall-cmd --add-service=https --permanent
firewall-cmd --reload

# Or in one command (RHEL 8+)
firewall-cmd --add-service=http --permanent --reload

# Remove service
firewall-cmd --remove-service=http --permanent
firewall-cmd --reload

# Multiple services
firewall-cmd --add-service={http,https,ssh} --permanent
\`\`\`

#### Port Management

\`\`\`bash
# Add port
firewall-cmd --add-port=8080/tcp --permanent
firewall-cmd --add-port=161/udp --permanent
firewall-cmd --add-port=3000-3010/tcp --permanent  # Port range

# Remove port
firewall-cmd --remove-port=8080/tcp --permanent

# List ports
firewall-cmd --list-ports
\`\`\`

#### Rich Rules (Advanced)

\`\`\`bash
# Allow SSH only from specific IP
firewall-cmd --add-rich-rule='rule family="ipv4" source address="203.0.113.10" service name="ssh" accept' --permanent

# Allow port 80 from subnet
firewall-cmd --add-rich-rule='rule family="ipv4" source address="192.168.1.0/24" port port="80" protocol="tcp" accept' --permanent

# Rate limit SSH (DDoS protection)
firewall-cmd --add-rich-rule='rule service name="ssh" limit value="10/m" accept' --permanent

# Block specific IP
firewall-cmd --add-rich-rule='rule family="ipv4" source address="10.0.0.100" reject' --permanent

# Log dropped packets
firewall-cmd --add-rich-rule='rule family="ipv4" source address="0.0.0.0/0" log prefix="DROPPED: " level="info" drop' --permanent

# Allow HTTP from anywhere except specific IP
firewall-cmd --add-rich-rule='rule family="ipv4" source address="192.168.1.100" service name="http" reject' --permanent

# List rich rules
firewall-cmd --list-rich-rules
\`\`\`

#### NAT / Port Forwarding

\`\`\`bash
# Enable masquerading (NAT)
firewall-cmd --zone=external --add-masquerade --permanent

# Port forwarding (forward port 80 to 8080)
firewall-cmd --zone=public \\
  --add-forward-port=port=80:proto=tcp:toport=8080 \\
  --permanent

# Port forwarding to different host
firewall-cmd --zone=public \\
  --add-forward-port=port=80:proto=tcp:toaddr=192.168.1.100:toport=80 \\
  --permanent

# List forwards
firewall-cmd --list-forward-ports
\`\`\`

#### Custom Services

\`\`\`bash
# Create custom service
firewall-cmd --new-service=myapp --permanent
firewall-cmd --service=myapp --set-description="My Application" --permanent
firewall-cmd --service=myapp --set-short="MyApp" --permanent
firewall-cmd --service=myapp --add-port=8080/tcp --permanent
firewall-cmd --service=myapp --add-port=8443/tcp --permanent

# Use custom service
firewall-cmd --add-service=myapp --permanent
firewall-cmd --reload

# Service definition file
cat /etc/firewalld/services/myapp.xml
\`\`\`

#### Direct Rules (iptables compatibility)

\`\`\`bash
# Add direct iptables rule
firewall-cmd --direct --add-rule ipv4 filter INPUT 0 -p tcp --dport 9000 -j ACCEPT

# List direct rules
firewall-cmd --direct --get-all-rules
\`\`\`

### Part 3: iptables (Traditional)

#### Tables & Chains

**Tables:**
- **filter** — default, packet filtering (INPUT, OUTPUT, FORWARD)
- **nat** — NAT (PREROUTING, POSTROUTING, OUTPUT)
- **mangle** — packet alteration
- **raw** — connection tracking exemptions

**Chains (filter table):**
- **INPUT** — incoming packets to local process
- **OUTPUT** — outgoing packets from local process
- **FORWARD** — packets routed through system

#### Basic Commands

\`\`\`bash
# List rules
iptables -L                    # List filter table
iptables -L -n                 # Numeric (no DNS)
iptables -L -n -v              # Verbose (packet/byte counts)
iptables -L -n --line-numbers  # With line numbers
iptables -t nat -L -n          # NAT table

# Flush rules (DELETE ALL!)
iptables -F                    # Flush filter table
iptables -t nat -F             # Flush NAT table
iptables -X                    # Delete user chains

# Save/Restore
iptables-save > /etc/iptables.rules
iptables-restore < /etc/iptables.rules

# Persistent rules (RHEL/CentOS)
yum install iptables-services
systemctl enable iptables
iptables-save > /etc/sysconfig/iptables

# Persistent rules (Debian/Ubuntu)
apt install iptables-persistent
netfilter-persistent save
netfilter-persistent reload
\`\`\`

#### Rule Management

\`\`\`bash
# Append rule (-A)
iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 22 -j ACCEPT

# Insert at position (-I)
iptables -I INPUT 1 -p tcp --dport 22 -j ACCEPT

# Delete rule by number
iptables -D INPUT 5

# Delete specific rule
iptables -D INPUT -p tcp --dport 22 -j ACCEPT

# Replace rule
iptables -R INPUT 5 -p tcp --dport 80 -j ACCEPT

# Set policy (default action)
iptables -P INPUT DROP
iptables -P OUTPUT ACCEPT
iptables -P FORWARD DROP
\`\`\`

#### Common Rules

\`\`\`bash
# Allow established connections
iptables -A INPUT -m state --state ESTABLISHED,RELATED -j ACCEPT

# Allow loopback
iptables -A INPUT -i lo -j ACCEPT

# Allow SSH
iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 22 -j ACCEPT

# Allow HTTP/HTTPS
iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 80 -j ACCEPT
iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 443 -j ACCEPT

# Allow from specific IP
iptables -A INPUT -s 192.168.1.100 -j ACCEPT

# Allow from subnet
iptables -A INPUT -s 192.168.1.0/24 -p tcp --dport 3306 -j ACCEPT

# Block specific IP
iptables -A INPUT -s 10.0.0.100 -j DROP

# Allow ping (ICMP)
iptables -A INPUT -p icmp --icmp-type echo-request -j ACCEPT

# Drop all other
iptables -A INPUT -j DROP
\`\`\`

#### Production Web Server Example

\`\`\`bash
#!/bin/bash
# Firewall for web server

# Flush existing rules
iptables -F
iptables -X
iptables -t nat -F
iptables -t nat -X

# Default policies
iptables -P INPUT DROP
iptables -P OUTPUT ACCEPT
iptables -P FORWARD DROP

# Allow loopback
iptables -A INPUT -i lo -j ACCEPT

# Allow established connections
iptables -A INPUT -m state --state ESTABLISHED,RELATED -j ACCEPT

# Allow SSH (consider changing port)
iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 22 -j ACCEPT

# Allow HTTP/HTTPS
iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 80 -j ACCEPT
iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 443 -j ACCEPT

# Allow ping
iptables -A INPUT -p icmp --icmp-type echo-request -j ACCEPT

# Log dropped packets (optional, can be noisy)
iptables -A INPUT -m limit --limit 5/min -j LOG --log-prefix "iptables DROP: " --log-level 7

# Drop all other
iptables -A INPUT -j DROP

# Save rules
iptables-save > /etc/iptables.rules
\`\`\`

#### Rate Limiting (DDoS Protection)

\`\`\`bash
# Limit SSH connections (max 3/min per IP)
iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 22 -m state --state NEW -m recent --set
iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 22 -m state --state NEW -m recent --update --seconds 60 --hitcount 4 -j DROP

# Limit HTTP connections (max 100/min per IP)
iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 80 -m limit --limit 100/minute --limit-burst 200 -j ACCEPT
iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 80 -j DROP

# SYN flood protection
iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --syn -m limit --limit 1/s --limit-burst 3 -j ACCEPT
iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --syn -j DROP
\`\`\`

#### NAT Examples

\`\`\`bash
# Source NAT (SNAT) — outgoing traffic masquerading
iptables -t nat -A POSTROUTING -o eth0 -j MASQUERADE

# Or with specific IP
iptables -t nat -A POSTROUTING -o eth0 -j SNAT --to-source 203.0.113.10

# Destination NAT (DNAT) — port forwarding
iptables -t nat -A PREROUTING -p tcp --dport 80 -j DNAT --to-destination 192.168.1.100:8080

# Full port forwarding example
iptables -t nat -A PREROUTING -i eth0 -p tcp --dport 80 -j DNAT --to 192.168.1.100:80
iptables -A FORWARD -p tcp -d 192.168.1.100 --dport 80 -j ACCEPT
\`\`\`

### Part 4: ufw (Ubuntu)

\`\`\`bash
# Enable/disable
ufw enable
ufw disable
ufw status
ufw status verbose

# Default policies
ufw default deny incoming
ufw default allow outgoing

# Allow services
ufw allow ssh
ufw allow http
ufw allow https

# Allow ports
ufw allow 8080/tcp
ufw allow 161/udp

# Allow from IP
ufw allow from 192.168.1.100
ufw allow from 192.168.1.0/24 to any port 3306

# Delete rule
ufw delete allow 8080/tcp
ufw delete 5  # By number

# Reset (delete all rules)
ufw reset
\`\`\`

### Part 5: Production Scenarios

#### Scenario 1: Web Server (HTTPS + SSH)

\`\`\`bash
# firewalld
firewall-cmd --add-service={ssh,http,https} --permanent
firewall-cmd --reload

# iptables
iptables -A INPUT -m state --state ESTABLISHED,RELATED -j ACCEPT
iptables -A INPUT -i lo -j ACCEPT
iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 22 -j ACCEPT
iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 80 -j ACCEPT
iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 443 -j ACCEPT
iptables -P INPUT DROP
iptables-save > /etc/sysconfig/iptables
\`\`\`

#### Scenario 2: Database Server (Internal Only)

\`\`\`bash
# firewalld - only from app servers
firewall-cmd --add-rich-rule='rule family="ipv4" source address="10.0.1.0/24" port port="3306" protocol="tcp" accept' --permanent
firewall-cmd --add-service=ssh --permanent
firewall-cmd --reload

# iptables
iptables -A INPUT -m state --state ESTABLISHED,RELATED -j ACCEPT
iptables -A INPUT -i lo -j ACCEPT
iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 22 -j ACCEPT
iptables -A INPUT -s 10.0.1.0/24 -p tcp --dport 3306 -j ACCEPT
iptables -P INPUT DROP
\`\`\`

#### Scenario 3: AWS Security Groups + firewalld

\`\`\`bash
# AWS SG handles external, firewalld for additional protection

# Allow from AWS metadata service (169.254.169.254)
firewall-cmd --add-rich-rule='rule family="ipv4" source address="169.254.169.254/32" accept' --permanent

# Allow internal VPC
firewall-cmd --zone=internal --add-source=10.0.0.0/16 --permanent

# Set default zone
firewall-cmd --set-default-zone=public
firewall-cmd --reload
\`\`\`

### Part 6: Troubleshooting

\`\`\`bash
# Check if firewall is blocking
# 1. List rules
firewall-cmd --list-all
iptables -L -n -v

# 2. Test port
nc -zv localhost 80
telnet localhost 80

# 3. Check service listening
ss -tulnp | grep :80

# 4. Temporarily disable firewall (TESTING ONLY!)
systemctl stop firewalld
# Test...
systemctl start firewalld

# 5. Check logs
journalctl -u firewalld -f
tail -f /var/log/messages | grep iptables

# 6. Test rule with TRACE
iptables -t raw -A PREROUTING -p tcp --dport 80 -j TRACE
iptables -t raw -A OUTPUT -p tcp --sport 80 -j TRACE
# View: tail -f /var/log/messages
\`\`\`

### Best Practices

1. **Always allow SSH before setting DROP policy**
   \`\`\`bash
   iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 22 -j ACCEPT
   iptables -P INPUT DROP  # Do this last!
   \`\`\`

2. **Allow established connections first**
   \`\`\`bash
   iptables -A INPUT -m state --state ESTABLISHED,RELATED -j ACCEPT
   \`\`\`

3. **Test rules before making permanent**
   \`\`\`bash
   firewall-cmd --add-service=http  # Test first
   firewall-cmd --add-service=http --permanent  # Then make permanent
   \`\`\`

4. **Document rules with comments**
   \`\`\`bash
   # In iptables script
   # Allow web traffic for nginx
   iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 80 -j ACCEPT
   \`\`\`

5. **Use fail2ban for intrusion prevention**
   \`\`\`bash
   yum install fail2ban
   systemctl enable --now fail2ban
   \`\`\`

### Production Checklist

- [ ] Document all open ports and why
- [ ] Use principle of least privilege (deny by default)
- [ ] Enable only required services
- [ ] Implement rate limiting for public services
- [ ] Log dropped packets (with rate limiting)
- [ ] Regular firewall rule audit
- [ ] Test changes in staging first
- [ ] Have out-of-band access (console) ready
- [ ] Integrate with intrusion detection (fail2ban)
        `,
        keyPoints: [
          "firewalld zones — trust levels for network connections",
          "firewall-cmd --permanent — persistent configuration",
          "iptables chains: INPUT, OUTPUT, FORWARD",
          "Always allow SSH before setting DROP policy",
          "Rich rules — advanced firewalld filtering",
          "Rate limiting — DDoS protection",
          "Test before making permanent"
        ],
        commands: [
          { cmd: "firewall-cmd --list-all", desc: "Show all firewall rules" },
          { cmd: "iptables -L -n -v", desc: "List iptables rules" },
          { cmd: "ss -tulnp | grep :80", desc: "Check if port 80 is listening" },
          { cmd: "firewall-cmd --add-service=http --permanent", desc: "Allow HTTP permanently" }
        ]
      },
      {
        id: 3,
        title: "Network Troubleshooting & Diagnostics",
        type: "theory",
        duration: "50 min",
        content: `
## Network Troubleshooting — Production Methodology

### Part 1: Systematic Troubleshooting Approach

#### OSI Layer Troubleshooting (Bottom-Up)

\`\`\`
Layer 1 (Physical)   → Cable? Link up? LEDs?
Layer 2 (Data Link)  → MAC? ARP? Switch port?
Layer 3 (Network)    → IP? Gateway? Routing?
Layer 4 (Transport)  → Port open? Firewall?
Layer 5-7 (App)      → Service running? Config?
\`\`\`

### Part 2: Layer-by-Layer Diagnostics

#### Layer 1: Physical

\`\`\`bash
# Check link status
ip link show dev eth0
# Look for: state UP

# NIC details
ethtool eth0
# Look for: Link detected: yes, Speed: 1000Mb/s

# Check interface statistics (errors)
ip -s link show eth0
ethtool -S eth0

# Common issues:
# - TX/RX errors → bad cable, duplex mismatch
# - Dropped packets → buffer overflow, congestion
# - Collisions → duplex mismatch (should be 0 on full-duplex)
\`\`\`

#### Layer 2: Data Link (MAC, ARP)

\`\`\`bash
# View MAC address
ip link show dev eth0
# Look for: link/ether xx:xx:xx:xx:xx:xx

# ARP table (Layer 2 neighbors)
ip neigh show
arp -a

# Test if gateway MAC is learned
ip neigh show | grep $(ip route show default | awk '{print $3}')

# Clear ARP cache (if stale)
ip neigh flush dev eth0

# Capture packets (low level)
tcpdump -i eth0 -n arp
\`\`\`

#### Layer 3: Network (IP, Routing)

\`\`\`bash
# 1. Check IP configuration
ip addr show dev eth0
# Verify: IP, netmask, broadcast

# 2. Check routing table
ip route show
# Verify: default gateway, network routes

# 3. Test local network (gateway)
ping -c 4 $(ip route show default | awk '{print $3}')

# 4. Test external IP (internet)
ping -c 4 8.8.8.8

# 5. Traceroute to see path
traceroute -n 8.8.8.8
mtr -n 8.8.8.8

# 6. Check if source IP is correct
ip route get 8.8.8.8
# Shows: src IP that will be used
\`\`\`

#### Layer 4: Transport (TCP/UDP, Ports)

\`\`\`bash
# Check if service is listening
ss -tulnp | grep :80

# Test TCP connection
nc -zv localhost 80
telnet localhost 80

# Test UDP (harder)
nc -zuv localhost 53

# Check firewall
firewall-cmd --list-all
iptables -L -n -v

# Scan port range
nmap localhost -p 1-65535
\`\`\`

#### Layer 7: Application (DNS, HTTP, etc.)

\`\`\`bash
# DNS resolution
nslookup google.com
dig google.com
host google.com

# HTTP testing
curl -v http://example.com
wget -O /dev/null http://example.com

# Service logs
journalctl -u nginx -f
tail -f /var/log/httpd/error_log
\`\`\`

### Part 3: Essential Tools Deep Dive

#### ping — ICMP Echo

\`\`\`bash
# Basic ping
ping -c 4 google.com

# Fast ping (quick check)
ping -c 10 -i 0.2 8.8.8.8

# Ping with timestamp
ping -D google.com

# Set packet size (MTU testing)
ping -s 1472 google.com    # 1472 + 28 = 1500 (standard MTU)
ping -s 8972 google.com    # Test jumbo frames (9000 MTU)

# Don't fragment (MTU path discovery)
ping -M do -s 1472 google.com

# Ping specific interface
ping -I eth1 google.com

# Flood ping (testing, root only)
ping -f 192.168.1.1

# Until N successful replies
ping -c 100 -w 10 8.8.8.8  # Max 10 seconds
\`\`\`

**Common ping issues:**
\`\`\`bash
# "Destination Host Unreachable" → routing problem (no route)
# "Request timeout" → firewall blocking or host down
# "Network unreachable" → no default gateway
# High packet loss → congestion, bad link
\`\`\`

#### traceroute / tracepath — Path Discovery

\`\`\`bash
# Basic traceroute
traceroute google.com

# No DNS resolution (faster)
traceroute -n google.com

# Use ICMP instead of UDP
traceroute -I google.com

# Use TCP SYN packets (bypass some firewalls)
traceroute -T -p 80 google.com

# Max hops
traceroute -m 20 google.com

# tracepath (no root needed, shows MTU)
tracepath google.com
\`\`\`

**Reading traceroute output:**
\`\`\`
1  192.168.1.1    1.234 ms    → Your router
2  10.0.0.1       5.456 ms    → ISP first hop
3  * * *                      → Firewall blocking ICMP
4  8.8.8.8        20.123 ms   → Destination
\`\`\`

#### mtr — My TraceRoute (Combined ping + traceroute)

\`\`\`bash
# Interactive mode
mtr google.com

# Report mode (10 cycles)
mtr -r -c 10 google.com

# No DNS (faster)
mtr -n google.com

# Output to file
mtr -r -c 100 google.com > mtr-report.txt

# TCP mode
mtr -T -P 80 google.com

# Wide report (more columns)
mtr -rw -c 50 google.com
\`\`\`

#### tcpdump — Packet Capture

\`\`\`bash
# Capture on interface
tcpdump -i eth0

# Capture specific port
tcpdump -i eth0 port 80

# Capture to file
tcpdump -i eth0 -w /tmp/capture.pcap

# Read from file
tcpdump -r /tmp/capture.pcap

# Capture with more details
tcpdump -i eth0 -n -vv

# Capture DNS queries
tcpdump -i eth0 port 53

# Capture HTTP traffic
tcpdump -i eth0 -A port 80

# Capture between two hosts
tcpdump -i eth0 host 192.168.1.100 and host 192.168.1.200

# Capture SYN packets (connection attempts)
tcpdump -i eth0 'tcp[tcpflags] & tcp-syn != 0'

# Limit capture size and count
tcpdump -i eth0 -c 100 -s 65535 -w capture.pcap
\`\`\`

#### nmap — Network Scanner

\`\`\`bash
# Basic scan
nmap 192.168.1.1

# Scan port range
nmap -p 1-65535 192.168.1.1

# Common ports
nmap -F 192.168.1.1

# Service version detection
nmap -sV 192.168.1.1

# OS detection
nmap -O 192.168.1.1

# Scan subnet
nmap 192.168.1.0/24

# Fast scan (top 100 ports)
nmap -F -T4 192.168.1.0/24

# UDP scan
nmap -sU 192.168.1.1

# Check if host is up (ping scan)
nmap -sn 192.168.1.0/24
\`\`\`

### Part 4: DNS Troubleshooting

\`\`\`bash
# Check system DNS configuration
cat /etc/resolv.conf
resolvectl status

# Test DNS with different servers
dig @8.8.8.8 example.com        # Google DNS
dig @1.1.1.1 example.com        # Cloudflare
dig @208.67.222.222 example.com # OpenDNS

# Trace DNS resolution
dig +trace example.com

# Query specific record types
dig example.com A               # IPv4
dig example.com AAAA            # IPv6
dig example.com MX              # Mail servers
dig example.com NS              # Nameservers
dig example.com TXT             # TXT records
dig example.com ANY             # All records

# Reverse DNS lookup
dig -x 8.8.8.8
host 8.8.8.8

# Check DNS propagation
dig example.com @ns1.example.com
dig example.com @ns2.example.com

# Debug DNS
dig +all example.com
dig +trace +all example.com

# Test DNSSEC
dig example.com +dnssec

# Performance test
time dig google.com
\`\`\`

**Common DNS issues:**
\`\`\`bash
# SERVFAIL → DNS server couldn't resolve
# NXDOMAIN → Domain doesn't exist
# Timeout → DNS server unreachable or firewall
# REFUSED → DNS server refuses query

# Fix: Clear DNS cache
resolvectl flush-caches           # systemd-resolved
systemctl restart systemd-resolved
systemctl restart nscd            # nscd
\`\`\`

### Part 5: Production Troubleshooting Scenarios

#### Scenario 1: "Website is Down"

\`\`\`bash
# Step 1: Check service
systemctl status nginx
ss -tulnp | grep :80

# Step 2: Check firewall
firewall-cmd --list-all | grep -E 'http|80'
iptables -L -n | grep 80

# Step 3: Test locally
curl -v http://localhost
telnet localhost 80

# Step 4: Test from external
curl -v http://your-public-ip

# Step 5: Check logs
journalctl -u nginx -n 50
tail -f /var/log/nginx/error.log

# Step 6: DNS check
dig your-domain.com
\`\`\`

#### Scenario 2: "Slow Network Performance"

\`\`\`bash
# 1. Check interface errors
ip -s link show eth0
ethtool -S eth0

# 2. Check bandwidth
iftop -i eth0
nload eth0
iperf3 -s           # On server
iperf3 -c server-ip # On client

# 3. Check latency
ping -c 100 gateway-ip
mtr -r -c 50 remote-host

# 4. Check MTU
ip link show eth0 | grep mtu
ping -M do -s 1472 remote-host  # Should work
ping -M do -s 1473 remote-host  # Should fail if MTU=1500

# 5. Check for packet loss
ping -c 1000 -i 0.2 remote-host | grep loss

# 6. Check routing
ip route get remote-ip
traceroute -n remote-ip
\`\`\`

#### Scenario 3: "Can't Connect to Database"

\`\`\`bash
# 1. Check if MySQL is running
systemctl status mysql
ss -tulnp | grep 3306

# 2. Check bind address
grep bind-address /etc/my.cnf
# If 127.0.0.1 → only local connections

# 3. Test connection
mysql -h 192.168.1.100 -u user -p
nc -zv 192.168.1.100 3306
telnet 192.168.1.100 3306

# 4. Check firewall
firewall-cmd --list-all
iptables -L -n | grep 3306

# 5. Check SELinux (if RHEL/CentOS)
getsebool -a | grep mysql
setenforce 0  # Temporary disable to test

# 6. MySQL logs
tail -f /var/log/mysql/error.log
\`\`\`

#### Scenario 4: "Intermittent Connection Issues"

\`\`\`bash
# 1. Long-running ping test
ping -D -O remote-host > ping-$(date +%Y%m%d-%H%M%S).log

# 2. MTR report
mtr -r -c 500 remote-host > mtr-report.txt

# 3. Check for ARP issues
watch -n 1 'ip neigh show'

# 4. Monitor connections
watch -n 1 'ss -tan | grep ESTAB | wc -l'

# 5. Capture packets during issue
tcpdump -i eth0 -w /tmp/capture-$(date +%Y%m%d-%H%M%S).pcap

# 6. Check system logs
journalctl -f | grep -E 'network|eth0|link'
\`\`\`

### Part 6: Bandwidth & Performance Tools

\`\`\`bash
# iftop — real-time bandwidth by connection
iftop -i eth0
iftop -i eth0 -n           # No DNS
iftop -i eth0 -P           # Show ports

# nethogs — bandwidth per process
nethogs eth0

# nload — real-time graph
nload eth0

# iperf3 — bandwidth testing
# On server:
iperf3 -s

# On client:
iperf3 -c server-ip
iperf3 -c server-ip -t 60  # 60 seconds test
iperf3 -c server-ip -R     # Reverse (download)

# vnstat — network statistics
vnstat -i eth0
vnstat -i eth0 -h          # Hourly
vnstat -i eth0 -d          # Daily
\`\`\`

### Part 7: Common Issues & Solutions

| Issue | Symptoms | Quick Check | Solution |
|-------|----------|-------------|----------|
| No IP | \`ip addr\` shows no IP | \`systemctl status NetworkManager\` | \`dhclient eth0\` or static config |
| No gateway | Ping gateway fails | \`ip route show default\` | Add default route |
| DNS failure | \`ping 8.8.8.8\` works, \`ping google.com\` fails | \`cat /etc/resolv.conf\` | Fix DNS servers |
| Firewall blocking | Service running but can't connect | \`firewall-cmd --list-all\` | Add firewall rule |
| Port not listening | Connection refused | \`ss -tulnp \\| grep :PORT\` | Start service, check config |
| Routing issue | Can reach gateway but not internet | \`ip route get 8.8.8.8\` | Check routing, NAT |
| MTU mismatch | Some sites work, others timeout | \`ping -M do -s 1472 host\` | Adjust MTU |
| Duplex mismatch | Slow, many errors | \`ethtool eth0\` | Force full duplex |

### Production Troubleshooting Checklist

**Quick diagnostics (5 min):**
- [ ] \`ip addr\` — check IP configuration
- [ ] \`ip route\` — check default gateway
- [ ] \`ping gateway\` — test local network
- [ ] \`ping 8.8.8.8\` — test internet
- [ ] \`dig google.com\` — test DNS
- [ ] \`ss -tulnp\` — check listening services
- [ ] \`firewall-cmd --list-all\` — check firewall

**Deep diagnostics:**
- [ ] \`ip -s link\` — check for errors/drops
- [ ] \`ethtool eth0\` — check link status, speed, duplex
- [ ] \`tcpdump\` — capture packets
- [ ] \`mtr\` — check path and latency
- [ ] \`journalctl -xe\` — check system logs
- [ ] \`systemctl status service\` — check service status

**Documentation:**
- [ ] Save outputs: \`ip addr\`, \`ip route\`, \`ss -tulnp\`
- [ ] Save tcpdump: \`tcpdump -w issue.pcap\`
- [ ] Save mtr report: \`mtr -r -c 100 host > mtr.txt\`
- [ ] Screenshot/copy error messages
        `,
