## Network Configuration & Troubleshooting Lab

### Lab 1: Static IP Configuration (10 min)

**Task:** Configure static IP on eth0 interface.

\`\`\`bash
# Using nmcli (modern way)
# 1. Create new connection
sudo nmcli con add type ethernet con-name eth0-static ifname eth0 \\
  ipv4.addresses 192.168.1.50/24 \\
  ipv4.gateway 192.168.1.1 \\
  ipv4.dns "8.8.8.8 8.8.4.4" \\
  ipv4.method manual

# 2. Activate connection
sudo nmcli con up eth0-static

# 3. Verify
ip addr show eth0
ip route show
ping -c 4 192.168.1.1
ping -c 4 8.8.8.8

# 4. Test DNS
dig google.com

# Cleanup (restore DHCP)
sudo nmcli con delete eth0-static
sudo nmcli con add type ethernet con-name eth0 ifname eth0 ipv4.method auto
sudo nmcli con up eth0
\`\`\`

### Lab 2: Firewall Configuration (10 min)

**Task:** Configure firewall for web server.

\`\`\`bash
# 1. Install and start firewalld
sudo systemctl enable --now firewalld

# 2. Check status
sudo firewall-cmd --state
sudo firewall-cmd --get-default-zone
sudo firewall-cmd --list-all

# 3. Allow web traffic
sudo firewall-cmd --add-service=http --permanent
sudo firewall-cmd --add-service=https --permanent

# 4. Allow custom port
sudo firewall-cmd --add-port=8080/tcp --permanent

# 5. Reload
sudo firewall-cmd --reload

# 6. Verify
sudo firewall-cmd --list-services
sudo firewall-cmd --list-ports

# 7. Test with Python web server
python3 -m http.server 8080 &
PID=$!

# From another terminal or machine
curl http://localhost:8080

# Cleanup
kill $PID
sudo firewall-cmd --remove-port=8080/tcp --permanent
sudo firewall-cmd --reload
\`\`\`

### Lab 3: DNS Troubleshooting (10 min)

**Task:** Diagnose and fix DNS issues.

\`\`\`bash
# Simulate DNS issue
sudo mv /etc/resolv.conf /etc/resolv.conf.bak
echo "nameserver 192.0.2.1" | sudo tee /etc/resolv.conf

# 1. Test - this should fail or be slow
ping -c 2 google.com
# Ctrl+C if hangs

# 2. Diagnose
cat /etc/resolv.conf
dig google.com
# Should timeout

# 3. Test with different DNS
dig @8.8.8.8 google.com
# Should work

# 4. Fix - restore proper DNS
sudo mv /etc/resolv.conf.bak /etc/resolv.conf
# or
echo "nameserver 8.8.8.8" | sudo tee /etc/resolv.conf

# 5. Verify
ping -c 4 google.com
dig google.com
\`\`\`

### Lab 4: Network Troubleshooting Workflow (15 min)

**Task:** Systematically troubleshoot network connectivity.

\`\`\`bash
# Scenario: Can't access remote service

# Step 1: Check local interface
ip addr show
# Expected: IP address assigned

# Step 2: Check routing
ip route show
# Expected: default via X.X.X.X

# Step 3: Test local network (gateway)
GATEWAY=$(ip route show default | awk '{print $3}')
ping -c 4 $GATEWAY
# Expected: 0% packet loss

# Step 4: Test internet connectivity
ping -c 4 8.8.8.8
# Expected: 0% packet loss

# Step 5: Test DNS
dig google.com +short
ping -c 4 google.com
# Expected: IP address returned, ping works

# Step 6: Test specific service
# Example: Can't reach web server at example.com:80

# 6a. DNS resolution
dig example.com
IP=$(dig example.com +short | head -1)

# 6b. Ping test
ping -c 4 $IP

# 6c. Port test
nc -zv $IP 80
# or
telnet $IP 80

# 6d. HTTP test
curl -v http://$IP

# 6e. Traceroute
mtr -r -c 10 $IP

# 6f. Check local firewall
sudo firewall-cmd --list-all

# Step 7: Document findings
cat > troubleshooting-report.txt << EOF
Date: $(date)
Issue: Cannot connect to example.com:80

Tests performed:
- Local IP: $(ip addr show eth0 | grep 'inet ' | awk '{print $2}')
- Gateway: $GATEWAY ($(ping -c 4 $GATEWAY | grep 'packet loss'))
- Internet: 8.8.8.8 ($(ping -c 4 8.8.8.8 | grep 'packet loss'))
- DNS: google.com resolves to $(dig google.com +short | head -1)
- Target IP: $IP
- Port 80: $(nc -zv $IP 80 2>&1)

Conclusion: [Add your analysis here]
EOF

cat troubleshooting-report.txt
\`\`\`

### Challenge Labs (Optional)

#### Challenge 1: Port Forwarding

Setup port forwarding to forward external port 8080 to internal service on port 80.

\`\`\`bash
# Using firewalld
sudo firewall-cmd --zone=public \\
  --add-forward-port=port=8080:proto=tcp:toport=80 \\
  --permanent
sudo firewall-cmd --reload

# Test
python3 -m http.server 80 &
curl http://localhost:8080
\`\`\`

#### Challenge 2: Network Bonding

Configure network bonding (link aggregation) with two interfaces.

\`\`\`bash
# Using nmcli
sudo nmcli con add type bond con-name bond0 ifname bond0 mode active-backup
sudo nmcli con add type ethernet slave-type bond con-name bond0-eth0 ifname eth0 master bond0
sudo nmcli con add type ethernet slave-type bond con-name bond0-eth1 ifname eth1 master bond0
sudo nmcli con mod bond0 ipv4.addresses 192.168.1.100/24
sudo nmcli con mod bond0 ipv4.gateway 192.168.1.1
sudo nmcli con mod bond0 ipv4.method manual
sudo nmcli con up bond0
\`\`\`

#### Challenge 3: VLAN Configuration

Create VLAN interface (VLAN ID 100).

\`\`\`bash
# Using nmcli
sudo nmcli con add type vlan con-name vlan100 ifname vlan100 dev eth0 id 100
sudo nmcli con mod vlan100 ipv4.addresses 192.168.100.10/24
sudo nmcli con mod vlan100 ipv4.method manual
sudo nmcli con up vlan100

# Verify
ip addr show vlan100
\`\`\`

