        title: "Disk Partitioning & Filesystems",
        type: "theory",
        duration: "60 min",
        content: `
## Disk Partitioning & Filesystem Management — Complete Guide

### Part 1: Disk Fundamentals

#### Disk Types & Naming

| Device Type | Naming Convention | Example | Description |
|-------------|------------------|---------|-------------|
| SATA/SAS HDD | /dev/sd* | /dev/sda, /dev/sdb | SCSI disk (most common) |
| NVMe SSD | /dev/nvme* | /dev/nvme0n1 | NVMe storage |
| Virtual (KVM) | /dev/vd* | /dev/vda | Virtio disk |
| IDE (legacy) | /dev/hd* | /dev/hda | Old IDE disks |
| MMC/SD card | /dev/mmcblk* | /dev/mmcblk0 | SD cards |

**Partition naming:**
- SCSI/SATA: /dev/sda1, /dev/sda2
- NVMe: /dev/nvme0n1p1, /dev/nvme0n1p2
- Virtual: /dev/vda1, /dev/vda2

#### MBR vs GPT

| Feature | MBR (Master Boot Record) | GPT (GUID Partition Table) |
|---------|-------------------------|----------------------------|
| Max partitions | 4 primary (or 3 + extended) | 128 partitions |
| Max disk size | 2 TB | 9.4 ZB (essentially unlimited) |
| Boot mode | BIOS | UEFI (can work with BIOS) |
| Partition table | Single point of failure | Redundant (backup at end) |
| Use case | Legacy systems, small disks | Modern systems, large disks |

### Part 2: Viewing Disks & Partitions

\`\`\`bash
# lsblk — block devices tree view
lsblk
lsblk -f                    # With filesystem info
lsblk -o NAME,SIZE,TYPE,MOUNTPOINT,FSTYPE
lsblk -p                    # Full device paths

# fdisk — list all disks
fdisk -l
fdisk -l /dev/sda          # Specific disk
fdisk -l | grep Disk       # Summary

# parted — print all
parted -l

# Disk space usage
df -h                       # Human readable
df -hT                      # With filesystem type
df -i                       # Inode usage
du -sh /var/*              # Directory sizes
du -h --max-depth=1 /

# Disk information
lsscsi                      # SCSI devices
lspci | grep -i storage    # Storage controllers
hdparm -I /dev/sda         # Disk details
smartctl -a /dev/sda       # SMART info (health)
\`\`\`

### Part 3: fdisk — MBR Partitioning

#### Creating MBR Partitions

\`\`\`bash
# Start fdisk
fdisk /dev/sdb

# Interactive commands:
# m    - help menu
# p    - print partition table
# n    - new partition
# d    - delete partition
# t    - change partition type
# l    - list partition types
# w    - write changes and exit
# q    - quit without saving

# Example workflow:
fdisk /dev/sdb
# Press: n (new)
# Press: p (primary)
# Press: 1 (partition number)
# Press: Enter (default first sector)
# Press: +10G (size)
# Press: w (write)

# Verify
lsblk /dev/sdb
partprobe /dev/sdb          # Re-read partition table
\`\`\`

#### Common Partition Types

| Type Code | Name | Use |
|-----------|------|-----|
| 83 | Linux | Standard Linux filesystem |
| 82 | Linux swap | Swap space |
| 8e | Linux LVM | LVM physical volume |
| fd | Linux RAID | Software RAID |
| ef | EFI System | EFI/UEFI boot |

\`\`\`bash
# Change partition type
fdisk /dev/sdb
# Press: t (type)
# Press: 1 (partition number)
# Press: 8e (LVM)
# Press: w (write)
\`\`\`

### Part 4: parted — GPT Partitioning

#### GPT Partitioning with parted

\`\`\`bash
# Start parted (interactive)
parted /dev/sdb

# Or non-interactive
parted /dev/sdb print
parted /dev/sdb mklabel gpt
parted /dev/sdb mkpart primary 0% 50%

# Interactive workflow:
parted /dev/sdb
(parted) mklabel gpt        # Create GPT label (DESTROYS DATA!)
(parted) print              # Show current layout
(parted) mkpart primary ext4 0% 50%    # 0-50% of disk
(parted) mkpart primary xfs 50% 100%   # 50-100% of disk
(parted) print
(parted) quit

# Name partitions (GPT feature)
parted /dev/sdb name 1 "data-partition"

# Set flags
parted /dev/sdb set 1 boot on

# Delete partition
parted /dev/sdb rm 1

# Verify
lsblk /dev/sdb
partprobe /dev/sdb
\`\`\`

#### Production GPT Example

\`\`\`bash
# Create 3 partitions: boot (1GB), swap (8GB), root (remaining)
parted /dev/sdb mklabel gpt
parted /dev/sdb mkpart primary fat32 1MiB 1GiB
parted /dev/sdb set 1 esp on
parted /dev/sdb mkpart primary linux-swap 1GiB 9GiB
parted /dev/sdb mkpart primary ext4 9GiB 100%
parted /dev/sdb print
\`\`\`

### Part 5: gdisk — GPT Alternative

\`\`\`bash
# gdisk — fdisk-like interface for GPT
gdisk /dev/sdb

# Commands similar to fdisk:
# n - new partition
# d - delete
# p - print
# w - write
# q - quit

# Advantage: can convert MBR to GPT
gdisk /dev/sdb
# r - recovery/transformation
# f - convert MBR to GPT (preserves data)
\`\`\`

### Part 6: Filesystem Creation

#### ext4 — Default Linux Filesystem

\`\`\`bash
# Create ext4
mkfs.ext4 /dev/sdb1
mkfs.ext4 -L mydata /dev/sdb1           # With label
mkfs.ext4 -m 1 /dev/sdb1                # Reserve only 1% for root
mkfs.ext4 -b 4096 /dev/sdb1             # 4KB block size
mkfs.ext4 -i 16384 /dev/sdb1            # Fewer inodes (larger files)

# View ext4 info
dumpe2fs /dev/sdb1
dumpe2fs /dev/sdb1 | grep -i 'block size'
tune2fs -l /dev/sdb1

# Set label
e2label /dev/sdb1 mydata
tune2fs -L mydata /dev/sdb1

# Tune ext4
tune2fs -m 1 /dev/sdb1                  # Change reserved blocks
tune2fs -c 0 /dev/sdb1                  # Disable fsck on mount count
tune2fs -i 0 /dev/sdb1                  # Disable fsck on time interval
tune2fs -O has_journal /dev/sdb1        # Enable journaling
\`\`\`

#### XFS — High-Performance Filesystem

\`\`\`bash
# Create XFS
mkfs.xfs /dev/sdb2
mkfs.xfs -L datavol /dev/sdb2           # With label
mkfs.xfs -f /dev/sdb2                   # Force (if already has filesystem)
mkfs.xfs -b size=4096 /dev/sdb2         # 4KB blocks
mkfs.xfs -i size=512 /dev/sdb2          # 512-byte inodes

# View XFS info
xfs_info /dev/sdb2
xfs_admin -l /dev/sdb2                  # Show label

# Set XFS label
xfs_admin -L datavol /dev/sdb2

# XFS cannot shrink! Only grow.
# Use XFS for large files, databases, high I/O
\`\`\`

#### btrfs — Advanced Filesystem

\`\`\`bash
# Create btrfs
mkfs.btrfs /dev/sdb3
mkfs.btrfs -L mybtrfs /dev/sdb3
mkfs.btrfs -m raid1 -d raid1 /dev/sdb3 /dev/sdc3  # RAID

# Features: snapshots, compression, online resize, subvolumes
\`\`\`

#### vfat/FAT32 — USB, EFI

\`\`\`bash
# Create FAT32
mkfs.vfat /dev/sdb4
mkfs.vfat -F 32 /dev/sdb4               # Force FAT32
mkfs.vfat -n MYUSB /dev/sdb4            # With label
\`\`\`

### Part 7: Filesystem Checking & Repair

#### ext4 Filesystem Check

\`\`\`bash
# fsck — filesystem check (ext2/3/4)
# IMPORTANT: NEVER fsck mounted filesystem!

# Unmount first
umount /dev/sdb1

# Check filesystem
fsck /dev/sdb1
fsck -n /dev/sdb1                       # Dry run (no changes)
fsck -y /dev/sdb1                       # Auto yes to prompts
fsck -f /dev/sdb1                       # Force check (even if clean)

# e2fsck (ext-specific)
e2fsck -f /dev/sdb1
e2fsck -p /dev/sdb1                     # Automatic repair (safe)
e2fsck -y /dev/sdb1                     # Yes to all prompts
e2fsck -c /dev/sdb1                     # Check for bad blocks

# Check on boot
tune2fs -c 1 /dev/sdb1                  # Check every mount
tune2fs -i 1d /dev/sdb1                 # Check daily
\`\`\`

#### XFS Filesystem Repair

\`\`\`bash
# xfs_repair
umount /dev/sdb2
xfs_repair /dev/sdb2
xfs_repair -n /dev/sdb2                 # Dry run
xfs_repair -L /dev/sdb2                 # Zero log (last resort)

# XFS check
xfs_check /dev/sdb2                     # Deprecated, use xfs_repair -n
\`\`\`

### Part 8: Filesystem Resize

#### ext4 Resize

\`\`\`bash
# Grow ext4 (can be online if mounted)
resize2fs /dev/sdb1
resize2fs /dev/sdb1 20G                 # Resize to 20GB

# Shrink ext4 (MUST be unmounted!)
umount /dev/sdb1
e2fsck -f /dev/sdb1                     # Check first
resize2fs /dev/sdb1 10G                 # Shrink to 10GB
# Then shrink partition
\`\`\`

#### XFS Grow (cannot shrink!)

\`\`\`bash
# XFS only grows (online)
xfs_growfs /mnt/data                    # Use mount point!
xfs_growfs -d /mnt/data                 # Grow to max
\`\`\`

### Part 9: Production Scenarios

#### Scenario 1: Add New Disk to Server

\`\`\`bash
# 1. Identify new disk
lsblk
fdisk -l | grep '^Disk /dev/'

# 2. Create partition
parted /dev/sdb mklabel gpt
parted /dev/sdb mkpart primary ext4 0% 100%

# 3. Create filesystem
mkfs.ext4 -L appdata /dev/sdb1

# 4. Create mount point
mkdir -p /mnt/appdata

# 5. Mount
mount /dev/sdb1 /mnt/appdata

# 6. Add to fstab (next lesson)
# 7. Verify
df -h /mnt/appdata
\`\`\`

#### Scenario 2: AWS EBS Volume

\`\`\`bash
# After attaching EBS volume in AWS console

# 1. Identify (new disk appears as /dev/xvdf or /dev/nvme1n1)
lsblk

# 2. Check if has filesystem (new EBS is blank)
file -s /dev/xvdf
# If "data" — no filesystem

# 3. Create filesystem (no partition needed for EBS)
mkfs.ext4 -L ebs-data /dev/xvdf

# 4. Mount
mkdir -p /mnt/ebs-data
mount /dev/xvdf /mnt/ebs-data

# 5. Persistent (use UUID in fstab)
blkid /dev/xvdf
\`\`\`

#### Scenario 3: Expand Existing Partition

\`\`\`bash
# Example: /dev/sda3 needs more space

# 1. Check current size
lsblk
df -h

# 2. Delete and recreate partition (DATA SAFE if same start)
fdisk /dev/sda
# d - delete partition 3
# n - new partition 3
# [Enter] - same start sector
# [Enter] - use all remaining space
# w - write

# 3. Re-read partition table
partprobe /dev/sda
# or reboot if kernel doesn't pick up

# 4. Resize filesystem
resize2fs /dev/sda3              # ext4
xfs_growfs /mount/point          # xfs
\`\`\`

### Part 10: Filesystem Comparison

| Feature | ext4 | XFS | btrfs |
|---------|------|-----|-------|
| Max file size | 16 TB | 8 EB | 16 EB |
| Max filesystem | 1 EB | 8 EB | 16 EB |
| Online grow | Yes | Yes | Yes |
| Online shrink | No | No | Yes |
| Offline shrink | Yes | No | Yes |
| Snapshots | No | No | Yes |
| Compression | No | No | Yes |
| Deduplication | No | No | Yes |
| Best for | General purpose | Large files, databases | Advanced features |
| Stability | Mature, stable | Mature, stable | Stable (newer) |

### Part 11: Best Practices

1. **Always use GPT for disks >2TB**
   \`\`\`bash
   parted /dev/sdb mklabel gpt
   \`\`\`

2. **Use UUID instead of device names in fstab**
   \`\`\`bash
   blkid /dev/sdb1
   # Use UUID in /etc/fstab
   \`\`\`

3. **Label filesystems for easy identification**
   \`\`\`bash
   mkfs.ext4 -L descriptive-name /dev/sdb1
   \`\`\`

4. **Never fsck a mounted filesystem**
   \`\`\`bash
   umount /dev/sdb1
   fsck /dev/sdb1
   \`\`\`

5. **Align partitions for SSD performance**
   \`\`\`bash
   parted /dev/sdb mkpart primary 1MiB 100%  # Aligned
   \`\`\`

6. **Test backups before filesystem operations**
   \`\`\`bash
   tar czf /backup/data.tar.gz /mnt/data
   \`\`\`

### Production Checklist

- [ ] Identify disk type (SATA, NVMe, virtual)
- [ ] Choose partition table (GPT for >2TB)
- [ ] Choose filesystem (ext4 general, XFS for large files)
- [ ] Label filesystem for identification
- [ ] Test mount before adding to fstab
- [ ] Document partition layout
- [ ] Monitor disk health (SMART)
- [ ] Plan for growth (LVM recommended)
        `,
        keyPoints: [
          "MBR — max 2TB, 4 primary partitions; GPT — unlimited size, 128 partitions",
          "fdisk — MBR partitioning, parted/gdisk — GPT partitioning",
          "ext4 — general purpose, can shrink; XFS — high performance, only grows",
          "lsblk — view block devices, blkid — view UUIDs",
          "Never fsck mounted filesystem",
          "Use UUID in fstab, not device names",
          "partprobe — re-read partition table without reboot"
        ]
      },
      {
        id: 2,
        title: "LVM — Logical Volume Manager",
        type: "theory",
        duration: "65 min",
        content: `
## LVM — Complete Production Guide

### Part 1: LVM Concepts

#### What is LVM?

**LVM (Logical Volume Manager)** = flexible disk management layer.

**Key benefits:**
- Dynamic resizing (grow/shrink without downtime)
- Snapshots (backup, testing)
- Span volumes across multiple disks
- Easy disk migration
- Striping for performance

#### LVM Architecture (3 Layers)

\`\`\`
Physical Volumes (PV)  →  Volume Groups (VG)  →  Logical Volumes (LV)
├── /dev/sdb                  ├── data_vg            ├── app_lv (10GB)
├── /dev/sdc                  │                      ├── db_lv (20GB)
└── /dev/sdd                  │                      └── logs_lv (5GB)
    (Physical disks)          (Pool of space)        (Virtual partitions)
\`\`\`

**Physical Volume (PV):**
- Physical disk or partition
- Building block for VG
- Can be /dev/sdb or /dev/sdb1

**Volume Group (VG):**
- Pool of PVs
- Like a "virtual disk"
- Contains one or more LVs

**Logical Volume (LV):**
- Virtual partition
- Created from VG space
- Where you create filesystem
- Can span multiple PVs

#### LVM Components

| Component | Purpose | Commands |
|-----------|---------|----------|
| Physical Volume (PV) | Physical disk/partition | pvcreate, pvs, pvdisplay, pvremove |
| Volume Group (VG) | Pool of PVs | vgcreate, vgs, vgdisplay, vgextend |
| Logical Volume (LV) | Virtual partition | lvcreate, lvs, lvdisplay, lvextend |
| Physical Extent (PE) | Smallest allocatable unit (default 4MB) | - |
| Logical Extent (LE) | Maps to PE | - |

### Part 2: Creating LVM — Step by Step

#### Step 1: Create Physical Volumes

\`\`\`bash
# Identify disks
lsblk
fdisk -l

# Create PV on entire disk
pvcreate /dev/sdb
pvcreate /dev/sdc
pvcreate /dev/sdd

# Or create PV on partition
fdisk /dev/sdb  # Create partition, type 8e (Linux LVM)
pvcreate /dev/sdb1

# Multiple at once
pvcreate /dev/sd{b,c,d}

# View PVs
pvs                           # Brief
pvdisplay                     # Detailed
pvdisplay /dev/sdb            # Specific PV

# PV details
pvs -o +pv_used,pv_free
\`\`\`

#### Step 2: Create Volume Group

\`\`\`bash
# Create VG from PVs
vgcreate data_vg /dev/sdb /dev/sdc

# Custom PE size (default 4MB)
vgcreate -s 8M data_vg /dev/sdb

# View VGs
vgs                           # Brief
vgdisplay                     # Detailed
vgdisplay data_vg             # Specific VG

# VG details
vgs -o +vg_size,vg_free
\`\`\`

#### Step 3: Create Logical Volumes

\`\`\`bash
# Create LV with specific size
lvcreate -n app_lv -L 10G data_vg
lvcreate -n db_lv -L 20G data_vg

# Create LV with percentage
lvcreate -n logs_lv -l 50%VG data_vg      # 50% of VG
lvcreate -n temp_lv -l 100%FREE data_vg   # All remaining space

# Create striped LV (performance)
lvcreate -n fast_lv -L 30G -i 2 data_vg  # Stripe across 2 PVs

# View LVs
lvs                           # Brief
lvdisplay                     # Detailed
lvdisplay /dev/data_vg/app_lv # Specific LV

# LV details
lvs -o +lv_size,lv_path
\`\`\`

#### Step 4: Create Filesystem

\`\`\`bash
# Create filesystem on LV
mkfs.ext4 /dev/data_vg/app_lv
mkfs.xfs /dev/data_vg/db_lv
mkfs.ext4 -L app-data /dev/data_vg/app_lv  # With label

# View LV path
ls -l /dev/data_vg/
ls -l /dev/mapper/data_vg-app_lv  # Alternative path
\`\`\`

#### Step 5: Mount

\`\`\`bash
# Create mount points
mkdir -p /mnt/{app,db,logs}

# Mount
mount /dev/data_vg/app_lv /mnt/app
mount /dev/data_vg/db_lv /mnt/db

# Verify
df -h
lsblk
\`\`\`

### Part 3: Extending LVM

#### Extend Logical Volume

\`\`\`bash
# Extend LV by adding size
lvextend -L +5G /dev/data_vg/app_lv

# Extend to specific size
lvextend -L 20G /dev/data_vg/app_lv

# Extend by percentage
lvextend -l +50%FREE /dev/data_vg/app_lv

# Extend to use all free space
lvextend -l +100%FREE /dev/data_vg/app_lv

# Extend and resize filesystem in one command
lvextend -L +5G -r /dev/data_vg/app_lv    # -r = resize filesystem
\`\`\`

#### Resize Filesystem After Extend

\`\`\`bash
# ext4 (can be online)
resize2fs /dev/data_vg/app_lv

# xfs (can be online, use mount point)
xfs_growfs /mnt/db

# btrfs
btrfs filesystem resize max /mnt/btrfs
\`\`\`

#### Extend Volume Group

\`\`\`bash
# Add new PV to VG
pvcreate /dev/sdd
vgextend data_vg /dev/sdd

# Verify
vgs
vgdisplay data_vg
pvs
\`\`\`

### Part 4: Reducing LVM

#### Reduce Logical Volume (ext4 only!)

\`\`\`bash
# WARNING: Can cause data loss if done incorrectly!
# XFS cannot be reduced!

# 1. Unmount
umount /mnt/app

# 2. Check filesystem
e2fsck -f /dev/data_vg/app_lv

# 3. Reduce filesystem first
resize2fs /dev/data_vg/app_lv 5G

# 4. Reduce LV
lvreduce -L 5G /dev/data_vg/app_lv

# Or combine (interactive)
lvreduce -L 5G -r /dev/data_vg/app_lv

# 5. Mount back
mount /dev/data_vg/app_lv /mnt/app
\`\`\`

### Part 5: LVM Snapshots

#### Creating Snapshots

\`\`\`bash
# Create snapshot (10% of original size for changes)
lvcreate -s -n app_lv_snap -L 2G /dev/data_vg/app_lv

# Create snapshot with percentage
lvcreate -s -n db_lv_snap -l 10%ORIGIN /dev/data_vg/db_lv

# View snapshots
lvs -o +lv_size,origin

# Mount snapshot (read-only)
mkdir /mnt/app_snapshot
mount -o ro /dev/data_vg/app_lv_snap /mnt/app_snapshot

# Use case: backup from snapshot
tar czf /backup/app.tar.gz /mnt/app_snapshot
\`\`\`

#### Snapshot Workflow for Backup

\`\`\`bash
# 1. Create snapshot
lvcreate -s -n db_lv_snap -L 5G /dev/data_vg/db_lv

# 2. Mount snapshot
mkdir /mnt/db_snapshot
mount -o ro /dev/data_vg/db_lv_snap /mnt/db_snapshot

# 3. Backup from snapshot (database stays online!)
mysqldump --all-databases > /backup/mysql.sql
# or
tar czf /backup/db.tar.gz /mnt/db_snapshot

# 4. Remove snapshot
umount /mnt/db_snapshot
lvremove /dev/data_vg/db_lv_snap
\`\`\`

#### Restoring from Snapshot

\`\`\`bash
# Merge snapshot back to origin (reverts changes)
umount /mnt/app
lvconvert --merge /dev/data_vg/app_lv_snap
# Reboot or re-activate to complete merge
\`\`\`

### Part 6: LVM Removal

\`\`\`bash
# Remove in reverse order

# 1. Unmount
umount /mnt/app

# 2. Remove LV
lvremove /dev/data_vg/app_lv
# Confirm: y

# 3. Remove VG (after removing all LVs)
vgremove data_vg

# 4. Remove PV
pvremove /dev/sdb
\`\`\`

### Part 7: LVM Rename

\`\`\`bash
# Rename LV
lvrename data_vg old_name new_name
lvrename /dev/data_vg/old_lv /dev/data_vg/new_lv

# Rename VG
vgrename old_vg new_vg

# Update fstab after rename!
\`\`\`

### Part 8: LVM Migration

#### Move Data Between PVs

\`\`\`bash
# Add new PV to VG
pvcreate /dev/sdd
vgextend data_vg /dev/sdd

# Move data from /dev/sdb to /dev/sdd
pvmove /dev/sdb /dev/sdd

# Remove old PV
vgreduce data_vg /dev/sdb
pvremove /dev/sdb
\`\`\`

### Part 9: Production Scenarios

#### Scenario 1: Database Server Running Out of Space

\`\`\`bash
# Current state
df -h /var/lib/mysql
lvs

# Solution: Extend LV
# 1. Check VG free space
vgs

# 2. If VG has space, extend LV
lvextend -L +20G -r /dev/vg_db/lv_mysql

# 3. If VG is full, add new disk
pvcreate /dev/sdd
vgextend vg_db /dev/sdd
lvextend -L +50G -r /dev/vg_db/lv_mysql

# 4. Verify
df -h /var/lib/mysql
\`\`\`

#### Scenario 2: AWS EC2 — Add EBS and Extend Root

\`\`\`bash
# After attaching new EBS volume (appears as /dev/xvdf)

# 1. Create PV
pvcreate /dev/xvdf

# 2. Extend existing VG
vgextend vg_root /dev/xvdf

# 3. Extend root LV
lvextend -l +100%FREE -r /dev/vg_root/lv_root

# 4. Verify
df -h /
vgs
\`\`\`

#### Scenario 3: Pre-Production Testing with Snapshot

\`\`\`bash
# Before applying updates

# 1. Create snapshot
lvcreate -s -n app_pre_update -L 10G /dev/vg_app/lv_app

# 2. Apply updates
yum update -y
systemctl restart myapp

# 3. Test application
# If OK: remove snapshot
lvremove /dev/vg_app/app_pre_update

# If FAIL: revert
umount /var/www
lvconvert --merge /dev/vg_app/app_pre_update
reboot
\`\`\`

### Part 10: LVM Thin Provisioning

#### Thin Pools (Advanced)

\`\`\`bash
# Create thin pool
lvcreate -L 100G -T data_vg/thin_pool

# Create thin volumes (can over-provision!)
lvcreate -V 50G -T data_vg/thin_pool -n thin_lv1
lvcreate -V 50G -T data_vg/thin_pool -n thin_lv2
lvcreate -V 50G -T data_vg/thin_pool -n thin_lv3
# Total: 150GB thin LVs in 100GB pool!

# Monitor thin pool usage
lvs -o +thin_count,lv_layout
\`\`\`

### Part 11: LVM Monitoring

\`\`\`bash
# Show all LVM components
pvs && echo && vgs && echo && lvs

# Detailed view
pvdisplay -m     # Show PV to VG mapping
vgdisplay -v     # Show VG contents
lvdisplay -m     # Show LV to PV mapping

# Monitor LV usage
lvs -o +lv_size,data_percent,metadata_percent

# Check VG free space
vgs -o +vg_free,vg_free_count

# Scan for changes
pvscan
vgscan
lvscan
\`\`\`

### Part 12: LVM Best Practices

1. **Use descriptive names**
   \`\`\`bash
   vgcreate vg_database /dev/sdb
   lvcreate -n lv_mysql -L 50G vg_database
   \`\`\`

2. **Always leave some VG free space (10-20%)**
   \`\`\`bash
   # For snapshots and emergencies
   lvcreate -l 80%VG -n lv_data vg_app
   \`\`\`

3. **Use -r flag when extending to auto-resize filesystem**
   \`\`\`bash
   lvextend -L +10G -r /dev/vg/lv
   \`\`\`

4. **Monitor snapshot usage (can fill up!)**
   \`\`\`bash
   lvs -o +data_percent
   # If >80%, extend or remove snapshot
   \`\`\`

5. **Document LVM layout**
   \`\`\`bash
   pvs > /root/lvm-layout.txt
   vgs >> /root/lvm-layout.txt
   lvs >> /root/lvm-layout.txt
   \`\`\`

6. **Use UUIDs in fstab for LVs**
   \`\`\`bash
   blkid /dev/vg/lv
   # Add to fstab using UUID
   \`\`\`

### Production Checklist

- [ ] Plan VG names and sizes
- [ ] Leave 10-20% VG free space
- [ ] Document LVM architecture
- [ ] Test extend procedures in non-prod
- [ ] Monitor snapshot usage
- [ ] Use -r flag when extending LVs
- [ ] Keep backups before major LVM changes
- [ ] Use meaningful VG/LV names
- [ ] Monitor thin pool usage if using thin provisioning
        `,
        keyPoints: [
          "LVM layers: PV (physical) → VG (pool) → LV (virtual partition)",
          "lvextend -L +5G -r — extend LV and resize filesystem",
          "vgextend — add new disk to volume group",
          "lvcreate -s — create snapshot for backups",
          "XFS cannot shrink, only ext4 can be reduced",
          "Snapshots use copy-on-write, monitor usage",
          "Always leave 10-20% VG free space"
        ],
        commands: [
          { cmd: "pvs && vgs && lvs", desc: "Show all LVM components" },
          { cmd: "lvextend -L +5G -r /dev/vg/lv", desc: "Extend LV and filesystem" },
          { cmd: "lvcreate -s -n snap -L 2G /dev/vg/lv", desc: "Create snapshot" },
          { cmd: "vgextend vg /dev/sdc", desc: "Add disk to volume group" }
        ]
      },
      {
        id: 3,
        title: "Mount, fstab & Swap Management",
        type: "theory",
        duration: "55 min",
        content: `
## Mounting Filesystems & Swap — Complete Guide

### Part 1: Mount Command Basics

\`\`\`bash
# Basic mount
mount /dev/sdb1 /mnt/data

# Show all mounts
mount
mount | grep '^/dev'
mount | column -t           # Formatted output

# Show specific filesystem type
mount -t ext4
mount -t xfs

# View mount options
mount | grep /mnt/data
\`\`\`

### Part 2: Mount Options

#### Common Mount Options

| Option | Effect | Use Case |
|--------|--------|----------|
| defaults | rw,suid,dev,exec,auto,nouser,async | Standard mount |
| ro | Read-only | Prevent modifications |
| rw | Read-write | Normal operation |
| noexec | No execute | Security (prevent scripts) |
| nosuid | Ignore setuid/setgid | Security |
| nodev | No device files | Security |
| noatime | Don't update access time | Performance |
| nodiratime | Don't update dir access time | Performance |
| relatime | Update atime relative to mtime | Performance (default) |
| sync | Synchronous I/O | Data integrity (slow) |
| async | Asynchronous I/O | Performance (default) |
| user | Allow non-root mount | USB drives |
| nouser | Only root can mount | Security (default) |
| auto | Mount with mount -a | Normal filesystems |
| noauto | Don't mount with mount -a | On-demand mounts |
| remount | Remount with new options | Change options without unmount |

\`\`\`bash
# Mount read-only
mount -o ro /dev/sdb1 /mnt/data

# Mount with multiple options
mount -o ro,noexec,nosuid /dev/sdb1 /mnt/data

# Mount without execute permissions
mount -o noexec /dev/sdb1 /mnt/temp

# Mount for performance
mount -o noatime,nodiratime /dev/sdb1 /mnt/data

# Remount with new options (no unmount needed)
mount -o remount,rw /mnt/data
mount -o remount,ro /mnt/data

# Mount all from fstab
mount -a

# Mount specific fstab entry
mount /mnt/data              # If defined in fstab
\`\`\`

### Part 3: Unmounting

\`\`\`bash
# Basic unmount
umount /mnt/data
umount /dev/sdb1

# Lazy unmount (detach now, clean up when not busy)
umount -l /mnt/data

# Force unmount (dangerous, can cause data loss!)
umount -f /mnt/data

# Check what's keeping mount busy
lsof /mnt/data
fuser -vm /mnt/data

# Kill processes using mount
fuser -km /mnt/data          # Send SIGKILL

# Find mount point of device
findmnt /dev/sdb1
findmnt /mnt/data
\`\`\`

### Part 4: /etc/fstab — Persistent Mounts

#### fstab Format

\`\`\`
# <device>    <mount point>    <type>    <options>    <dump>    <pass>
UUID=xxx      /                ext4      defaults     1         1
UUID=yyy      /home            xfs       defaults     0         2
UUID=zzz      /var/www         ext4      noatime      0         2
/swapfile     none             swap      sw           0         0
\`\`\`

**Fields explained:**
1. **device** — UUID, LABEL, or /dev/* (UUID preferred)
2. **mount point** — Where to mount
3. **type** — Filesystem type (ext4, xfs, swap, nfs, etc.)
4. **options** — Mount options (defaults, ro, noatime, etc.)
5. **dump** — Backup (0=no, 1=yes) [rarely used]
6. **pass** — fsck order (0=skip, 1=root, 2=other)

#### Using UUIDs (Recommended)

\`\`\`bash
# Find UUID
blkid /dev/sdb1
lsblk -f
ls -l /dev/disk/by-uuid/

# Example output:
# /dev/sdb1: UUID="a1b2c3d4-e5f6-7890-abcd-ef1234567890" TYPE="ext4"

# Add to fstab
UUID=a1b2c3d4-e5f6-7890-abcd-ef1234567890  /mnt/data  ext4  defaults  0  2
\`\`\`

#### Using Labels

\`\`\`bash
# Create label
e2label /dev/sdb1 datavolume       # ext4
xfs_admin -L datavolume /dev/sdb1  # xfs

# Find label
blkid /dev/sdb1
lsblk -f

# Use in fstab
LABEL=datavolume  /mnt/data  ext4  defaults  0  2
\`\`\`

#### fstab Examples

\`\`\`bash
# Root filesystem
UUID=xxx  /  ext4  defaults  1  1

# Home directory (separate partition)
UUID=yyy  /home  ext4  defaults,noatime  0  2

# Data volume (LVM)
/dev/vg_data/lv_data  /mnt/data  xfs  defaults  0  2

# USB drive (auto-mount)
UUID=zzz  /media/usb  vfat  defaults,user,noauto  0  0

# NFS mount
192.168.1.100:/exports/data  /mnt/nfs  nfs  defaults,_netdev  0  0

# CIFS (Windows share)
//server/share  /mnt/windows  cifs  credentials=/root/.smbcreds,uid=1000  0  0

# Tmpfs (RAM disk)
tmpfs  /mnt/ramdisk  tmpfs  size=512M,mode=1777  0  0

# Bind mount
/var/www/site1  /home/user/site1  none  bind  0  0
\`\`\`

#### Testing fstab

\`\`\`bash
# CRITICAL: Always test before reboot!

# 1. Backup fstab
cp /etc/fstab /etc/fstab.backup

# 2. Edit fstab
vi /etc/fstab

# 3. Test syntax
findmnt --verify

# 4. Test mount (without actually mounting all)
mount -a -v              # Verbose
mount -a --fake          # Dry run (show what would happen)

# 5. If OK, mount for real
mount -a

# 6. Verify
df -h
mount | grep /mnt/data

# If error: restore backup
cp /etc/fstab.backup /etc/fstab
\`\`\`

### Part 5: Swap Management

#### What is Swap?

**Swap** = disk space used as virtual RAM when physical RAM is full.

**Types:**
- Swap partition (faster, traditional)
- Swap file (flexible, easy to resize)

**When to use:**
- Physical RAM < 2GB → swap = 2x RAM
- Physical RAM > 2GB → swap = RAM size
- Hibernation → swap >= RAM size

#### Swap Partition

\`\`\`bash
# Create swap partition (during fdisk/parted)
fdisk /dev/sdb
# Create partition, type 82 (Linux swap)

# Format as swap
mkswap /dev/sdb2
mkswap -L myswap /dev/sdb2    # With label

# Enable swap
swapon /dev/sdb2

# Verify
swapon --show
free -h

# Add to fstab
UUID=xxx  none  swap  sw  0  0

# Disable swap
swapoff /dev/sdb2
\`\`\`

#### Swap File (Recommended for flexibility)

\`\`\`bash
# Create swap file (4GB example)
dd if=/dev/zero of=/swapfile bs=1M count=4096
# or faster:
fallocate -l 4G /swapfile

# Set permissions (IMPORTANT!)
chmod 600 /swapfile

# Format as swap
mkswap /swapfile

# Enable
swapon /swapfile

# Verify
swapon --show
free -h

# Add to fstab
/swapfile  none  swap  sw  0  0

# Test fstab
swapoff /swapfile
swapon -a                # Enable all swap from fstab
\`\`\`

#### Resize Swap File

\`\`\`bash
# Disable current swap
swapoff /swapfile

# Resize (8GB example)
fallocate -l 8G /swapfile
# or
dd if=/dev/zero of=/swapfile bs=1M count=8192

# Reformat
mkswap /swapfile

# Enable
swapon /swapfile

# Verify
free -h
\`\`\`

#### Swap Priority

\`\`\`bash
# Multiple swap devices can have priority
# Higher priority = used first

# Enable with priority
swapon -p 10 /swapfile
swapon -p 5 /dev/sdb2

# In fstab
/swapfile  none  swap  sw,pri=10  0  0
/dev/sdb2  none  swap  sw,pri=5   0  0

# View priority
swapon --show
\`\`\`

#### Swap Monitoring

\`\`\`bash
# View swap usage
free -h
swapon --show
cat /proc/swaps

# Swap usage by process
for file in /proc/*/status ; do
  awk '/VmSwap|Name/{printf $2 " " $3}END{ print ""}' $file
done | sort -k 2 -n -r | head -10

# Clear swap (move back to RAM)
swapoff -a && swapon -a
\`\`\`

#### Swappiness

**Swappiness** = kernel's tendency to swap (0-100)
- 0 = avoid swapping unless necessary
- 100 = swap aggressively

\`\`\`bash
# Check current swappiness
cat /proc/sys/vm/swappiness
# Default: 60

# Set temporarily
sysctl vm.swappiness=10

# Set permanently
echo "vm.swappiness=10" >> /etc/sysctl.conf
sysctl -p

# Recommended values:
# Desktop: 10-20
# Server: 10
# Database server: 1-5
\`\`\`

### Part 6: Production Scenarios

#### Scenario 1: Add Persistent Mount

\`\`\`bash
# 1. Identify device
lsblk
blkid /dev/sdb1

# 2. Create mount point
mkdir -p /mnt/data

# 3. Test mount
mount /dev/sdb1 /mnt/data
df -h /mnt/data

# 4. Get UUID
blkid /dev/sdb1 | grep -o 'UUID="[^"]*"'

# 5. Add to fstab
echo 'UUID=xxx  /mnt/data  ext4  defaults,noatime  0  2' >> /etc/fstab

# 6. Test fstab
umount /mnt/data
mount -a
df -h /mnt/data
\`\`\`

#### Scenario 2: Emergency Read-Only Root

\`\`\`bash
# If root filesystem has errors

# 1. Boot to rescue mode
# In GRUB: add ro to kernel parameters

# 2. Or remount read-only
mount -o remount,ro /

# 3. Fix issues
fsck -f /dev/sda1

# 4. Remount read-write
mount -o remount,rw /
\`\`\`

#### Scenario 3: Mount NFS Share

\`\`\`bash
# Install NFS client
yum install nfs-utils -y  # RHEL/CentOS
apt install nfs-common -y # Ubuntu

# Mount manually
mount -t nfs 192.168.1.100:/exports/data /mnt/nfs

# Persistent in fstab
192.168.1.100:/exports/data  /mnt/nfs  nfs  defaults,_netdev  0  0

# _netdev = wait for network before mounting
\`\`\`

#### Scenario 4: AWS EC2 — Add Swap

\`\`\`bash
# EC2 instances don't have swap by default

# Create 4GB swap file
sudo fallocate -l 4G /swapfile
sudo chmod 600 /swapfile
sudo mkswap /swapfile
sudo swapon /swapfile

# Verify
free -h

# Make permanent
echo '/swapfile  none  swap  sw  0  0' | sudo tee -a /etc/fstab

# Set swappiness
echo 'vm.swappiness=10' | sudo tee -a /etc/sysctl.conf
sudo sysctl -p
\`\`\`

### Part 7: Troubleshooting

\`\`\`bash
# Mount fails — device busy
lsof /mnt/data
fuser -vm /mnt/data
# Kill processes or use lazy unmount

# Mount fails — wrong filesystem type
blkid /dev/sdb1
mount -t auto /dev/sdb1 /mnt/data

# fstab error — boot fails
# Boot to emergency mode
# Fix /etc/fstab
# Or comment out problematic line with #

# Swap not activating
swapon -a -v              # Verbose
swapon --show
free -h

# Mount point doesn't exist
mkdir -p /mnt/data
mount -a
\`\`\`

### Best Practices

1. **Always use UUID in fstab**
   \`\`\`bash
   UUID=xxx  /mnt/data  ext4  defaults  0  2
   # NOT: /dev/sdb1 (can change!)
   \`\`\`

2. **Test fstab before reboot**
   \`\`\`bash
   mount -a
   df -h
   \`\`\`

3. **Use noatime for performance**
   \`\`\`bash
   UUID=xxx  /mnt/data  ext4  defaults,noatime  0  2
   \`\`\`

4. **Backup fstab before changes**
   \`\`\`bash
   cp /etc/fstab /etc/fstab.$(date +%Y%m%d)
   \`\`\`

5. **Add swap for systems with <4GB RAM**
   \`\`\`bash
   # Swap = 1x to 2x RAM size
   \`\`\`

6. **Set appropriate swappiness for server**
   \`\`\`bash
   vm.swappiness=10
   \`\`\`

### Production Checklist

- [ ] Use UUID or LABEL in fstab, not /dev/sdX
- [ ] Test fstab with mount -a before reboot
- [ ] Backup fstab before editing
- [ ] Set noatime for better performance
- [ ] Configure swap (file or partition)
- [ ] Set swappiness appropriate for workload
- [ ] Document all mount points
- [ ] Monitor swap usage
- [ ] Use _netdev for network filesystems
        `,
        keyPoints: [
          "/etc/fstab — persistent mounts (use UUID!)",
          "mount -a — mount all from fstab (test before reboot!)",
          "noatime — performance option, don't update access time",
          "Swap file — flexible, easy to resize",
          "swappiness — controls swap tendency (10 for servers)",
          "findmnt --verify — test fstab syntax",
          "Always backup fstab before editing"
        ]
      },
      {
        id: 4,
        title: "RAID Basics & Storage Troubleshooting",
        type: "theory",
        duration: "45 min",
        content: `
## RAID & Storage Troubleshooting

### Part 1: RAID Overview

#### What is RAID?

**RAID (Redundant Array of Independent Disks)** = combine multiple disks for:
- **Performance** (striping)
- **Redundancy** (mirroring)
- **Both** (combination)

#### RAID Levels

| RAID | Name | Min Disks | Usable Space | Redundancy | Performance | Use Case |
|------|------|-----------|--------------|------------|-------------|----------|
| 0 | Striping | 2 | 100% | None | Fast R/W | Performance (no backup) |
| 1 | Mirroring | 2 | 50% | 1 disk fail | Fast R | Critical data |
| 5 | Striping + Parity | 3 | (n-1)/n | 1 disk fail | Fast R | General purpose |
| 6 | Dual Parity | 4 | (n-2)/n | 2 disk fail | Fast R | High availability |
| 10 | Mirror + Stripe | 4 | 50% | 1 disk per mirror | Fast R/W | Database, critical |

**Example with 4x 1TB disks:**
- RAID 0: 4TB (no redundancy)
- RAID 1: 2TB (50% usable)
- RAID 5: 3TB (75% usable)
- RAID 6: 2TB (50% usable)
- RAID 10: 2TB (50% usable)

### Part 2: Software RAID (mdadm)

\`\`\`bash
# Install mdadm
yum install mdadm -y      # RHEL/CentOS
apt install mdadm -y      # Ubuntu

# Create RAID 1 (mirror)
mdadm --create /dev/md0 --level=1 --raid-devices=2 /dev/sdb /dev/sdc

# Create RAID 5
mdadm --create /dev/md0 --level=5 --raid-devices=3 /dev/sdb /dev/sdc /dev/sdd

# Create RAID 10
mdadm --create /dev/md0 --level=10 --raid-devices=4 /dev/sd{b,c,d,e}

# View RAID status
cat /proc/mdstat
mdadm --detail /dev/md0

# Save RAID configuration
mdadm --detail --scan >> /etc/mdadm.conf

# Create filesystem
mkfs.ext4 /dev/md0

# Mount
mkdir /mnt/raid
mount /dev/md0 /mnt/raid

# fstab entry
/dev/md0  /mnt/raid  ext4  defaults  0  2
\`\`\`

#### RAID Management

\`\`\`bash
# Monitor RAID
watch cat /proc/mdstat

# Mark disk as failed
mdadm --manage /dev/md0 --fail /dev/sdb

# Remove failed disk
mdadm --manage /dev/md0 --remove /dev/sdb

# Add new disk
mdadm --manage /dev/md0 --add /dev/sdf

# Stop RAID
umount /mnt/raid
mdadm --stop /dev/md0

# Assemble RAID
mdadm --assemble /dev/md0 /dev/sdb /dev/sdc
\`\`\`

### Part 3: Disk Health Monitoring

#### SMART Monitoring

\`\`\`bash
# Install smartmontools
yum install smartmontools -y

# Check if SMART is enabled
smartctl -i /dev/sda

# Enable SMART
smartctl -s on /dev/sda

# Run health test
smartctl -H /dev/sda

# View all SMART attributes
smartctl -a /dev/sda

# Run short self-test
smartctl -t short /dev/sda

# Run long self-test
smartctl -t long /dev/sda

# View test results
smartctl -l selftest /dev/sda

# Monitor key attributes
smartctl -A /dev/sda | grep -E 'Reallocated|Current_Pending|Offline_Uncorrectable'
\`\`\`

#### Critical SMART Attributes

| ID | Attribute | Warning Threshold | Meaning |
|----|-----------|-------------------|---------|
| 5 | Reallocated_Sector_Ct | >0 | Bad sectors remapped |
| 187 | Reported_Uncorrect | >0 | Read errors |
| 188 | Command_Timeout | >0 | Command timeouts |
| 197 | Current_Pending_Sector | >0 | Sectors waiting to remap |
| 198 | Offline_Uncorrectable | >0 | Uncorrectable errors |

### Part 4: Storage Troubleshooting

#### Disk Full Issues

\`\`\`bash
# Check disk usage
df -h
df -i                    # Inode usage

# Find large files
du -sh /* | sort -rh | head -20
find / -type f -size +100M -exec ls -lh {} \\; 2>/dev/null

# Find large directories
du -h --max-depth=1 / | sort -rh | head -20

# Find deleted files still held by processes
lsof +L1
lsof | grep deleted

# Clear journal logs
journalctl --vacuum-time=7d
journalctl --vacuum-size=100M

# Clear package cache
yum clean all            # RHEL
apt clean                # Ubuntu

# Find and delete old logs
find /var/log -name "*.gz" -mtime +30 -delete
find /var/log -name "*.old" -mtime +30 -delete
\`\`\`

#### Slow Disk Performance

\`\`\`bash
# Check I/O wait
top                      # Look at 'wa' (wait)
iostat -x 1              # Detailed I/O stats
iotop                    # Per-process I/O

# Test disk speed
dd if=/dev/zero of=/tmp/testfile bs=1G count=1 oflag=direct
# Read speed
dd if=/tmp/testfile of=/dev/null bs=1G count=1 iflag=direct

# Check for disk errors
dmesg | grep -i error
dmesg | grep -i 'I/O error'

# Check filesystem errors
fsck -n /dev/sdb1        # Dry run (safe)
\`\`\`

#### Read-Only Filesystem

\`\`\`bash
# Filesystem remounted read-only due to errors

# 1. Check dmesg
dmesg | tail -50
dmesg | grep -i 'remount.*read-only'

# 2. Unmount and check
umount /mnt/data
fsck -y /dev/sdb1

# 3. If can't unmount (root)
# Boot to rescue mode or:
mount -o remount,rw /
\`\`\`

#### LVM Issues

\`\`\`bash
# LV not showing up
lvs
lvscan
vgscan --mknodes

# Activate LV
lvchange -ay /dev/vg/lv

# VG not found after reboot
vgscan
vgchange -ay

# PV UUID conflict (cloned VM)
pvs -o +uuid
vgimportclone /dev/sdb
\`\`\`

### Part 5: Disk Benchmarking

\`\`\`bash
# Simple dd benchmark
dd if=/dev/zero of=/tmp/test bs=1G count=1 oflag=dsync
# dsync = synchronous (realistic)

# fio (advanced benchmarking)
yum install fio -y

# Random read test
fio --name=randread --ioengine=libaio --iodepth=16 --rw=randread \\
    --bs=4k --direct=1 --size=1G --numjobs=4 --runtime=60 --group_reporting

# Sequential write test
fio --name=seqwrite --ioengine=libaio --iodepth=1 --rw=write \\
    --bs=1M --direct=1 --size=1G --numjobs=1 --runtime=60 --group_reporting

# bonnie++ (comprehensive)
yum install bonnie++ -y
bonnie++ -d /mnt/test -u root
\`\`\`

### Part 6: Production Scenarios

#### Scenario 1: Disk Running Out of Space

\`\`\`bash
# 1. Identify the issue
df -h
# /dev/sda1 is 95% full

# 2. Find what's using space
du -sh /* | sort -rh | head -10
# /var is using most space

# 3. Drill down
du -sh /var/* | sort -rh | head -10
# /var/log is large

# 4. Check logs
ls -lhS /var/log | head -10
# messages.old is 5GB

# 5. Clean up
gzip /var/log/messages.old
find /var/log -name "*.gz" -mtime +30 -delete

# 6. If still not enough — extend LV or add disk
lvextend -L +10G -r /dev/vg/lv_root
\`\`\`

#### Scenario 2: Failing Disk in RAID

\`\`\`bash
# 1. Check RAID status
cat /proc/mdstat
# Shows: [UU_] — one disk failed!

# 2. Identify failed disk
mdadm --detail /dev/md0

# 3. Prepare replacement disk
# Physically replace disk /dev/sdc

# 4. Add to RAID
mdadm --manage /dev/md0 --add /dev/sdc

# 5. Monitor rebuild
watch cat /proc/mdstat

# 6. After rebuild complete
mdadm --detail /dev/md0
# Should show [UUU]
\`\`\`

#### Scenario 3: Inode Exhaustion

\`\`\`bash
# df -h shows space available but can't create files
# "No space left on device" error

# Check inodes
df -i
# /dev/sda1 shows 100% IUse%

# Find directories with many files
for i in /*; do echo $i; find $i -xdev | wc -l; done

# Common culprit: /var/spool/postfix or /tmp
find /tmp -type f | wc -l

# Clean up
find /tmp -type f -mtime +7 -delete
\`\`\`

### Part 7: Best Practices

1. **Monitor disk health regularly**
   \`\`\`bash
   smartctl -H /dev/sda
   # Set up smartd daemon for alerts
   \`\`\`

2. **Set up monitoring alerts**
   \`\`\`bash
   # Alert when disk >80% full
   # Alert when SMART errors occur
   # Alert when RAID degrades
   \`\`\`

3. **Test backups regularly**
   \`\`\`bash
   # Restore test monthly
   \`\`\`

4. **Document storage layout**
   \`\`\`bash
   lsblk > /root/storage-layout.txt
   lvs >> /root/storage-layout.txt
   \`\`\`

5. **Plan for growth**
   \`\`\`bash
   # Use LVM for flexibility
   # Leave free space in VG
   \`\`\`

### Production Checklist

- [ ] Monitor disk space (alert at 80%)
- [ ] Monitor SMART status weekly
- [ ] Monitor RAID status if applicable
- [ ] Check for filesystem errors monthly
- [ ] Document storage layout
- [ ] Test disaster recovery procedures
- [ ] Keep spare disks for RAID
- [ ] Monitor I/O performance
- [ ] Clean up old logs regularly
        `,
        keyPoints: [
          "RAID 1 — mirroring, RAID 5 — striping + parity, RAID 10 — mirror + stripe",
          "mdadm — Linux software RAID",
          "smartctl — disk health monitoring (SMART)",
          "df -i — check inode usage",
          "iostat/iotop — I/O monitoring",
          "Monitor SMART attributes: Reallocated_Sector_Ct, Current_Pending_Sector"
        ]
      },
