      {
        id: 1,
        title: "RPM & YUM/DNF — RHEL/CentOS Package Management",
        type: "theory",
        duration: "55 min",
        content: `
## RHEL/CentOS Package Management — Complete Guide

### Part 1: RPM — Low-Level Package Management

#### RPM Basics

**RPM (Red Hat Package Manager)** = low-level package tool
- Manages .rpm files
- NO automatic dependency resolution
- Used by yum/dnf internally

\`\`\`bash
# Package naming convention:
# package-name-version-release.architecture.rpm
# Example: nginx-1.20.1-9.el8.x86_64.rpm
\`\`\`

#### RPM Installation

\`\`\`bash
# Install package
rpm -ivh package.rpm
# i = install, v = verbose, h = hash progress

# Install without dependencies check (dangerous!)
rpm -ivh --nodeps package.rpm

# Install with force (dangerous!)
rpm -ivh --force package.rpm

# Upgrade package
rpm -Uvh package.rpm        # Install or upgrade
rpm -Fvh package.rpm        # Only upgrade if already installed

# Downgrade
rpm -Uvh --oldpackage older-package.rpm
\`\`\`

#### RPM Removal

\`\`\`bash
# Remove package
rpm -e package-name

# Remove without dependencies check
rpm -e --nodeps package-name

# Remove with test (dry run)
rpm -e --test package-name
\`\`\`

#### RPM Query

\`\`\`bash
# List all installed packages
rpm -qa
rpm -qa | grep nginx
rpm -qa | wc -l             # Count installed packages

# Query package info
rpm -qi nginx               # Installed package info
rpm -qip package.rpm        # Package file info

# List files in package
rpm -ql nginx               # Installed package files
rpm -qlp package.rpm        # Files in .rpm file

# Find which package owns a file
rpm -qf /usr/bin/vim
rpm -qf /etc/nginx/nginx.conf

# List documentation files
rpm -qd nginx

# List configuration files
rpm -qc nginx

# Query package dependencies
rpm -qR nginx               # Requires (dependencies)
rpm -q --requires nginx
rpm -q --provides nginx     # What package provides

# Query package changelog
rpm -q --changelog nginx | head -20

# Query scripts
rpm -q --scripts nginx
\`\`\`

#### RPM Verification

\`\`\`bash
# Verify installed package
rpm -V nginx
# Output codes:
# S = Size differs
# M = Mode differs (permissions)
# 5 = MD5 sum differs (file modified)
# D = Device major/minor differs
# L = Symlink path differs
# U = User ownership differs
# G = Group ownership differs
# T = mTime differs
# P = Capabilities differ

# Verify all packages
rpm -Va

# Verify against package file
rpm -Vp package.rpm
\`\`\`

#### RPM Database

\`\`\`bash
# Rebuild RPM database (if corrupted)
rpm --rebuilddb

# RPM database location
ls -lh /var/lib/rpm/

# Import GPG key
rpm --import /etc/pki/rpm-gpg/RPM-GPG-KEY-redhat-release
\`\`\`

### Part 2: YUM — High-Level Package Management (RHEL 7)

\`\`\`bash
# Search packages
yum search nginx
yum search all nginx        # Search name, description, summary

# Package info
yum info nginx
yum info nginx-*            # All nginx packages

# Install package
yum install nginx -y
yum install -y nginx httpd php  # Multiple packages

# Install specific version
yum install nginx-1.20.1

# Install package group
yum groupinstall "Development Tools" -y
yum grouplist

# Reinstall package
yum reinstall nginx

# Downgrade package
yum downgrade nginx

# Update packages
yum update                  # All packages
yum update nginx            # Specific package
yum update -y              # Without prompts

# Check for updates
yum check-update
yum list updates

# Remove package
yum remove nginx
yum remove nginx httpd

# Remove with dependencies
yum autoremove nginx

# List packages
yum list installed
yum list installed | grep nginx
yum list available
yum list all

# Show package dependencies
yum deplist nginx

# Find which package provides a file
yum provides /usr/bin/vim
yum whatprovides */semanage

# Transaction history
yum history
yum history list
yum history info 5          # Details of transaction 5
yum history undo 5          # Undo transaction
yum history redo 5          # Redo transaction

# Clean cache
yum clean packages          # Downloaded packages
yum clean metadata          # Metadata
yum clean all               # Everything
yum makecache              # Rebuild cache
\`\`\`

### Part 3: DNF — Modern Package Manager (RHEL 8+)

**DNF** = Dandified YUM (faster, better dependency resolution)

\`\`\`bash
# Most yum commands work with dnf

# Search
dnf search nginx

# Info
dnf info nginx

# Install
dnf install nginx -y
dnf install @python39      # Module install

# Update
dnf upgrade               # Preferred over 'update'
dnf upgrade nginx

# Remove
dnf remove nginx
dnf autoremove            # Remove unused dependencies

# List
dnf list installed
dnf list available
dnf list upgrades

# Repoquery (powerful query tool)
dnf repoquery --requires nginx
dnf repoquery --whatrequires nginx
dnf repoquery --file /usr/bin/vim

# History
dnf history
dnf history undo last
dnf history rollback 5

# Clean
dnf clean all
dnf makecache

# Check for problems
dnf check

# Module streams (RHEL 8+)
dnf module list
dnf module list nodejs
dnf module install nodejs:14
dnf module enable nodejs:14
dnf module reset nodejs
\`\`\`

### Part 4: Repository Management

#### List Repositories

\`\`\`bash
# YUM
yum repolist
yum repolist all
yum repolist enabled
yum repolist disabled

# DNF
dnf repolist
dnf repolist --all
\`\`\`

#### Add Repository

\`\`\`bash
# Method 1: yum-config-manager
yum install yum-utils -y
yum-config-manager --add-repo https://download.docker.com/linux/centos/docker-ce.repo

# DNF
dnf config-manager --add-repo URL

# Method 2: Create repo file manually
cat > /etc/yum.repos.d/nginx.repo << 'EOF'
[nginx-stable]
name=nginx stable repo
baseurl=http://nginx.org/packages/centos/$releasever/$basearch/
gpgcheck=1
enabled=1
gpgkey=https://nginx.org/keys/nginx_signing.key
EOF
\`\`\`

#### Enable/Disable Repositories

\`\`\`bash
# Enable
yum-config-manager --enable epel
dnf config-manager --set-enabled epel

# Disable
yum-config-manager --disable epel
dnf config-manager --set-disabled epel

# Install from specific repo
yum install --enablerepo=epel package-name
dnf install --enablerepo=epel package-name

# Exclude package from updates
yum install yum-plugin-versionlock
yum versionlock add nginx
yum versionlock list
yum versionlock delete nginx
\`\`\`

#### EPEL Repository (Extra Packages for Enterprise Linux)

\`\`\`bash
# RHEL 8/9
dnf install epel-release -y

# RHEL 7
yum install epel-release -y

# Or manual
yum install https://dl.fedoraproject.org/pub/epel/epel-release-latest-8.noarch.rpm
\`\`\`

### Part 5: Production Scenarios

#### Scenario 1: Install Nginx from Official Repo

\`\`\`bash
# 1. Add nginx repo
cat > /etc/yum.repos.d/nginx.repo << 'EOF'
[nginx]
name=nginx repo
baseurl=http://nginx.org/packages/centos/8/$basearch/
gpgcheck=0
enabled=1
EOF

# 2. Install
dnf install nginx -y

# 3. Verify
rpm -qi nginx
nginx -v
\`\`\`

#### Scenario 2: Update All Packages Safely

\`\`\`bash
# 1. Check for updates
dnf check-update

# 2. Test update (dry run)
dnf update --assumeno

# 3. Update (if OK)
dnf update -y

# 4. Reboot if kernel updated
needs-restarting -r
# If needed:
reboot
\`\`\`

#### Scenario 3: Fix Broken Dependencies

\`\`\`bash
# YUM
yum install yum-utils -y
package-cleanup --problems
package-cleanup --dupes
yum-complete-transaction

# DNF
dnf check
dnf autoremove
dnf distro-sync

# Nuclear option — rebuild RPM DB
rpm --rebuilddb
\`\`\`

#### Scenario 4: Local Repository

\`\`\`bash
# Create local repo from mounted ISO

# 1. Mount ISO
mkdir /mnt/cdrom
mount -o loop RHEL-8.5.iso /mnt/cdrom

# 2. Create repo file
cat > /etc/yum.repos.d/local.repo << 'EOF'
[local]
name=Local Repository
baseurl=file:///mnt/cdrom/BaseOS
enabled=1
gpgcheck=0
EOF

# 3. Clean and verify
dnf clean all
dnf repolist
\`\`\`

### Part 6: Package Troubleshooting

\`\`\`bash
# Failed transaction
yum-complete-transaction --cleanup-only

# Duplicate packages
package-cleanup --cleandupes

# Orphaned dependencies
package-cleanup --leaves

# Check for problems
rpm -Va                     # Verify all packages
yum check
dnf check

# Fix corrupted RPM database
rm -f /var/lib/rpm/__db.*
rpm --rebuilddb

# Clear cache and retry
yum clean all
yum makecache
\`\`\`

### Part 7: Best Practices

1. **Always use -y for automation**
   \`\`\`bash
   dnf install nginx -y
   \`\`\`

2. **Lock critical packages**
   \`\`\`bash
   dnf install python3-dnf-plugin-versionlock
   dnf versionlock add kernel
   \`\`\`

3. **Regular updates**
   \`\`\`bash
   dnf upgrade -y
   needs-restarting -r
   \`\`\`

4. **Use EPEL for extra packages**
   \`\`\`bash
   dnf install epel-release -y
   \`\`\`

5. **Document custom repos**
   \`\`\`bash
   # Add comments in /etc/yum.repos.d/
   \`\`\`

### Production Checklist

- [ ] Configure EPEL repository
- [ ] Set up automatic security updates
- [ ] Document all third-party repos
- [ ] Test updates in staging first
- [ ] Lock kernel and critical packages
- [ ] Clean cache regularly
- [ ] Monitor package update logs
- [ ] Keep repo files backed up
        `,
        keyPoints: [
          "rpm — low-level, no dependency resolution; yum/dnf — high-level, auto deps",
          "dnf — modern, faster than yum (RHEL 8+)",
          "rpm -qa — query all, rpm -qf file — find package",
          "dnf history undo — rollback transactions",
          "/etc/yum.repos.d/ — repository configuration",
          "EPEL — extra packages for enterprise Linux",
          "dnf versionlock — prevent package updates"
        ]
      },
      {
        id: 2,
        title: "APT & DPKG — Debian/Ubuntu Package Management",
        type: "theory",
        duration: "50 min",
        content: `
## Debian/Ubuntu Package Management — Complete Guide

### Part 1: DPKG — Low-Level Package Management

#### DPKG Basics

**DPKG (Debian Package)** = low-level package tool for .deb files
- NO automatic dependency resolution
- Used by APT internally

\`\`\`bash
# Package naming:
# package_version-release_architecture.deb
# Example: nginx_1.18.0-6ubuntu14.3_amd64.deb
\`\`\`

#### DPKG Installation & Removal

\`\`\`bash
# Install package
dpkg -i package.deb

# Install multiple packages
dpkg -i *.deb

# Remove package (keep configs)
dpkg -r package-name

# Purge package (remove configs too)
dpkg -P package-name

# Force install (dangerous!)
dpkg -i --force-all package.deb
\`\`\`

#### DPKG Query

\`\`\`bash
# List all installed packages
dpkg -l
dpkg -l | grep nginx
dpkg -l | wc -l             # Count

# List files in installed package
dpkg -L nginx

# List files in .deb file
dpkg -c package.deb

# Find which package owns file
dpkg -S /usr/bin/vim
dpkg -S /etc/nginx/nginx.conf

# Show package info
dpkg -s nginx               # Installed
dpkg -I package.deb         # From .deb file

# List uninstalled but configured packages
dpkg -l | grep ^rc

# Package status
dpkg -s nginx | grep Status
\`\`\`

#### DPKG Configuration

\`\`\`bash
# Reconfigure package (interactive)
dpkg-reconfigure package-name
dpkg-reconfigure tzdata     # Timezone
dpkg-reconfigure locales    # Locales

# Fix broken dependencies
dpkg --configure -a

# Unpack but don't configure
dpkg --unpack package.deb
\`\`\`

### Part 2: APT — High-Level Package Management

#### APT Basics

**APT (Advanced Package Tool)** = high-level package manager
- Automatic dependency resolution
- Repository management
- Modern replacement for apt-get

\`\`\`bash
# Update package index
apt update

# Upgrade packages
apt upgrade                 # Safe upgrade (no removals)
apt full-upgrade            # Smart upgrade (may remove)
apt dist-upgrade            # Alias for full-upgrade

# Install package
apt install nginx -y
apt install nginx=1.18.0-6ubuntu14.3  # Specific version
apt install nginx httpd     # Multiple packages

# Reinstall
apt reinstall nginx

# Download without install
apt download nginx

# Remove package
apt remove nginx            # Keep configs
apt purge nginx             # Remove configs
apt autoremove              # Remove unused dependencies
apt autoremove --purge      # Remove + purge unused

# Search packages
apt search nginx
apt search "web server"

# Show package info
apt show nginx
apt show nginx -a           # All versions

# List packages
apt list --installed
apt list --installed | grep nginx
apt list --upgradable
apt list --all-versions nginx

# Check dependencies
apt depends nginx
apt rdepends nginx          # Reverse dependencies

# Policy (version info)
apt policy nginx
apt-cache policy nginx
\`\`\`

#### APT Troubleshooting

\`\`\`bash
# Fix broken packages
apt --fix-broken install
apt -f install

# Fix missing dependencies
apt install -f

# Reconfigure packages
dpkg --configure -a

# Clean package cache
apt clean                   # Remove all .deb files
apt autoclean               # Remove only obsolete .deb

# Check for errors
apt check
\`\`\`

### Part 3: Repository Management

#### sources.list Format

\`\`\`bash
# /etc/apt/sources.list
# Format: deb|deb-src URI distribution components

# Example Ubuntu 20.04:
deb http://archive.ubuntu.com/ubuntu focal main restricted
deb http://archive.ubuntu.com/ubuntu focal-updates main restricted
deb http://security.ubuntu.com/ubuntu focal-security main restricted

# Example Debian 11:
deb http://deb.debian.org/debian bullseye main contrib non-free
deb http://deb.debian.org/debian-security bullseye-security main
\`\`\`

#### Add Repository

\`\`\`bash
# Method 1: add-apt-repository (PPA for Ubuntu)
add-apt-repository ppa:nginx/stable -y
apt update

# Method 2: Manual entry
echo "deb [arch=amd64] https://download.docker.com/linux/ubuntu focal stable" | \\
  tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/docker.list

# Method 3: Direct edit
vi /etc/apt/sources.list

# Add GPG key
curl -fsSL https://download.docker.com/linux/ubuntu/gpg | \\
  gpg --dearmor -o /usr/share/keyrings/docker-archive-keyring.gpg

# With signed-by
echo "deb [arch=amd64 signed-by=/usr/share/keyrings/docker-archive-keyring.gpg] \\
  https://download.docker.com/linux/ubuntu focal stable" | \\
  tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/docker.list
\`\`\`

#### Remove Repository

\`\`\`bash
# Remove PPA
add-apt-repository --remove ppa:nginx/stable -y

# Remove file
rm /etc/apt/sources.list.d/docker.list
apt update
\`\`\`

### Part 4: APT vs APT-GET

| Feature | apt | apt-get |
|---------|-----|---------|
| User-friendly | Yes | No |
| Progress bar | Yes | No |
| Color output | Yes | No |
| Combines tools | Yes | No |
| Stable interface | No | Yes |
| Scripting | Not recommended | Recommended |

\`\`\`bash
# Use 'apt' for interactive
apt install nginx

# Use 'apt-get' for scripts
apt-get install -y nginx
\`\`\`

### Part 5: Production Scenarios

#### Scenario 1: Install Docker on Ubuntu

\`\`\`bash
# 1. Update and install prerequisites
apt update
apt install -y ca-certificates curl gnupg lsb-release

# 2. Add Docker GPG key
curl -fsSL https://download.docker.com/linux/ubuntu/gpg | \\
  gpg --dearmor -o /usr/share/keyrings/docker-archive-keyring.gpg

# 3. Add repository
echo "deb [arch=$(dpkg --print-architecture) \\
  signed-by=/usr/share/keyrings/docker-archive-keyring.gpg] \\
  https://download.docker.com/linux/ubuntu \\
  $(lsb_release -cs) stable" | \\
  tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/docker.list > /dev/null

# 4. Install
apt update
apt install -y docker-ce docker-ce-cli containerd.io

# 5. Verify
docker --version
systemctl status docker
\`\`\`

#### Scenario 2: Upgrade Ubuntu Release

\`\`\`bash
# 1. Update current system
apt update && apt upgrade -y
apt dist-upgrade -y
apt autoremove -y

# 2. Install update manager
apt install -y update-manager-core

# 3. Run upgrade
do-release-upgrade
# or for LTS only:
do-release-upgrade -d

# 4. Reboot
reboot
\`\`\`

#### Scenario 3: Hold/Unhold Package

\`\`\`bash
# Hold package (prevent updates)
apt-mark hold nginx
apt-mark showhold

# Unhold
apt-mark unhold nginx

# Manual hold (alternative)
echo "nginx hold" | dpkg --set-selections
dpkg --get-selections | grep hold
\`\`\`

#### Scenario 4: Fix Broken Dependencies

\`\`\`bash
# 1. Try automatic fix
apt --fix-broken install

# 2. Reconfigure packages
dpkg --configure -a

# 3. If still broken, remove problematic package
apt remove problematic-package
apt autoremove

# 4. Reinstall
apt install problematic-package

# 5. Nuclear option — remove lock files
rm /var/lib/dpkg/lock-frontend
rm /var/lib/dpkg/lock
rm /var/cache/apt/archives/lock
dpkg --configure -a
apt update
\`\`\`

### Part 6: APT Configuration

\`\`\`bash
# APT config: /etc/apt/apt.conf.d/

# Example: Assume yes for all prompts
cat > /etc/apt/apt.conf.d/99local << 'EOF'
APT::Get::Assume-Yes "true";
APT::Get::allow-unauthenticated "false";
EOF

# Example: Keep old config files
cat > /etc/apt/apt.conf.d/50unattended-upgrades << 'EOF'
Dpkg::Options {
   "--force-confdef";
   "--force-confold";
}
EOF
\`\`\`

### Part 7: Unattended Upgrades

\`\`\`bash
# Install
apt install -y unattended-upgrades

# Configure
dpkg-reconfigure -plow unattended-upgrades

# Config file
vi /etc/apt/apt.conf.d/50unattended-upgrades

# Enable auto security updates
Unattended-Upgrade::Allowed-Origins {
    "\${distro_id}:\${distro_codename}-security";
};

# Email notifications
Unattended-Upgrade::Mail "admin@example.com";

# Auto reboot
Unattended-Upgrade::Automatic-Reboot "true";
Unattended-Upgrade::Automatic-Reboot-Time "02:00";
\`\`\`

### Part 8: Best Practices

1. **Always update before install**
   \`\`\`bash
   apt update && apt install nginx
   \`\`\`

2. **Use full-upgrade regularly**
   \`\`\`bash
   apt update && apt full-upgrade -y
   \`\`\`

3. **Clean cache periodically**
   \`\`\`bash
   apt autoclean
   apt autoremove
   \`\`\`

4. **Hold critical packages**
   \`\`\`bash
   apt-mark hold kernel-image
   \`\`\`

5. **Use unattended-upgrades for security**
   \`\`\`bash
   apt install unattended-upgrades -y
   \`\`\`

6. **Test in staging first**
   \`\`\`bash
   # Always test major upgrades
   \`\`\`

### Production Checklist

- [ ] Configure unattended security updates
- [ ] Set up automatic cleanup (autoremove, autoclean)
- [ ] Document all third-party repositories
- [ ] Hold kernel and critical packages
- [ ] Monitor update logs
- [ ] Test upgrades in staging
- [ ] Keep backup before major upgrades
- [ ] Configure email notifications
        `,
        keyPoints: [
          "dpkg — low-level, no deps; apt — high-level, auto deps",
          "apt update — refresh package index (always first!)",
          "apt upgrade — safe updates; full-upgrade — smart updates",
          "apt-mark hold — prevent package updates",
          "/etc/apt/sources.list — main repo config",
          "add-apt-repository — add PPAs (Ubuntu)",
          "unattended-upgrades — automatic security updates"
        ]
      },
      {
        id: 3,
        title: "Package Lab & Quiz",
        type: "lab",
        duration: "30 min",
        content: `
## Package Management Lab

### Lab 1: RPM/YUM/DNF (RHEL/CentOS)

\`\`\`bash
# 1. Search for package
dnf search htop

# 2. Install package
sudo dnf install -y htop

# 3. Verify installation
rpm -qi htop
which htop
htop --version

# 4. List files from package
rpm -ql htop

# 5. Find which package owns a file
rpm -qf /usr/bin/htop

# 6. Add EPEL repository
sudo dnf install -y epel-release
sudo dnf repolist

# 7. Check for updates
dnf check-update

# 8. View history
dnf history

# 9. Remove package
sudo dnf remove -y htop
\`\`\`

### Lab 2: APT/DPKG (Ubuntu/Debian)

\`\`\`bash
# 1. Update package index
sudo apt update

# 2. Search for package
apt search htop

# 3. Show package info
apt show htop

# 4. Install
sudo apt install -y htop

# 5. Verify
dpkg -l | grep htop
which htop

# 6. List files
dpkg -L htop

# 7. Find owner
dpkg -S /usr/bin/htop

# 8. Check upgradable
apt list --upgradable

# 9. Hold package
sudo apt-mark hold htop
apt-mark showhold

# 10. Unhold and remove
sudo apt-mark unhold htop
sudo apt remove -y htop
sudo apt autoremove -y
\`\`\`

### Quiz Questions

**Q1:** rpm -qf /etc/nginx/nginx.conf командаси нима қилади?

A) nginx пакетини ўрнатади
B) /etc/nginx/nginx.conf файл қайси пакетга тегишли эканлигини кўрсатади ✓
C) nginx конфигурацияни текширади
D) nginx пакет версиясини кўрсатади

**Q2:** apt update ва apt upgrade орасида қандай фарқ бор?

A) apt update — ўрнатади, apt upgrade — янгилайди
B) apt update — package индексни янгилайди, apt upgrade — пакетларни янгилайди ✓
C) Фарқ йўқ, иккаласи бир хил
D) apt update — тезроқ ишлайди

**Q3:** Пакетни янгиланишдан тўхтатиш учун қандай command ишлатилади?

A) dnf versionlock add package (RHEL)  ✓
B) dnf freeze package
C) dnf stop package
D) dnf disable package

**Q4:** /etc/yum.repos.d/ директорияда нима сақланади?

A) Ўрнатилган пакетлар
B) Repository конфигурация файллари ✓
C) Пакет кэши
D) GPG калитлар

**Q5:** apt full-upgrade ва apt upgrade орасида қандай фарқ бор?

A) full-upgrade тезроқ ишлайди
B) full-upgrade керак бўлса пакетларни ўчириши мумкин (smart upgrade) ✓
C) Фарқ йўқ
D) full-upgrade фақат security янгиланишларни ўрнатади
\`\`\`
