        id: 1,
        title: "System Logging — Complete Guide",
        type: "theory",
        duration: "60 min",
        content: `
## System Logging — journalctl, rsyslog & logrotate

### Part 1: journalctl — systemd Journal (Modern)

#### Basic Usage

\`\`\`bash
# View all logs
journalctl

# Follow logs (real-time)
journalctl -f

# Reverse order (newest first)
journalctl -r

# Show only last N lines
journalctl -n 50
journalctl --lines=100

# Output format
journalctl -o json          # JSON format
journalctl -o json-pretty   # Pretty JSON
journalctl -o verbose       # Full details
journalctl -o cat           # Only message content
\`\`\`

#### Boot Logs

\`\`\`bash
# Current boot
journalctl -b
journalctl -b 0

# Previous boots
journalctl -b -1            # Last boot
journalctl -b -2            # 2 boots ago

# List all boots
journalctl --list-boots

# Specific boot ID
journalctl -b 4ae8b3f2...
\`\`\`

#### Filtering by Service/Unit

\`\`\`bash
# Specific service
journalctl -u nginx
journalctl -u nginx.service
journalctl -u ssh

# Multiple services
journalctl -u nginx -u mysql

# Follow service logs
journalctl -u nginx -f

# Service logs with priority
journalctl -u nginx -p err
\`\`\`

#### Priority Filtering

\`\`\`bash
# Priority levels (0-7):
# 0 emerg    — System is unusable
# 1 alert    — Action must be taken immediately
# 2 crit     — Critical conditions
# 3 err      — Error conditions
# 4 warning  — Warning conditions
# 5 notice   — Normal but significant
# 6 info     — Informational
# 7 debug    — Debug messages

journalctl -p err           # Errors and above (0-3)
journalctl -p warning       # Warnings and above (0-4)
journalctl -p info          # Info and above (0-6)
journalctl -p 3             # Same as -p err
\`\`\`

#### Time-Based Filtering

\`\`\`bash
# Absolute time
journalctl --since "2026-01-01"
journalctl --since "2026-01-01 10:00:00"
journalctl --until "2026-01-31"

# Relative time
journalctl --since "1 hour ago"
journalctl --since "2 days ago"
journalctl --since "10 minutes ago"

# Combined
journalctl --since "09:00" --until "17:00"
journalctl --since yesterday --until today

# Date shortcuts
journalctl --since today
journalctl --since yesterday
\`\`\`

#### Kernel & System Logs

\`\`\`bash
# Kernel messages only
journalctl -k
journalctl -k -b            # Kernel from current boot

# Kernel from previous boot
journalctl -k -b -1

# System messages (PID 1)
journalctl _PID=1
\`\`\`

#### Filtering by Process

\`\`\`bash
# By PID
journalctl _PID=1234

# By executable
journalctl /usr/bin/bash
journalctl /usr/sbin/sshd

# By user ID
journalctl _UID=1000

# By Group ID
journalctl _GID=1000
\`\`\`

#### Disk Usage & Cleanup

\`\`\`bash
# Check disk usage
journalctl --disk-usage

# Vacuum by size
journalctl --vacuum-size=500M
journalctl --vacuum-size=1G

# Vacuum by time
journalctl --vacuum-time=7d
journalctl --vacuum-time=30d

# Vacuum by number of files
journalctl --vacuum-files=10

# Verify journal
journalctl --verify
\`\`\`

#### Persistent Journal

\`\`\`bash
# Make journal persistent (survives reboot)
mkdir -p /var/log/journal
systemctl restart systemd-journald

# Configure in /etc/systemd/journald.conf
[Journal]
Storage=persistent
SystemMaxUse=500M
SystemKeepFree=1G
MaxRetentionSec=1month
\`\`\`

### Part 2: rsyslog — Traditional Logging

#### rsyslog Configuration

\`\`\`bash
# Main config
/etc/rsyslog.conf

# Drop-in configs
/etc/rsyslog.d/*.conf

# Restart rsyslog
systemctl restart rsyslog
systemctl status rsyslog
\`\`\`

#### rsyslog.conf Format

\`\`\`
facility.priority   action

# Examples:
*.info;mail.none;authpriv.none;cron.none  /var/log/messages
authpriv.*                                 /var/log/secure
mail.*                                     /var/log/maillog
cron.*                                     /var/log/cron
*.emerg                                    :omusrmsg:*
\`\`\`

#### Facilities

| Facility | Description |
|----------|-------------|
| auth/authpriv | Security/authorization |
| cron | Cron daemon |
| daemon | System daemons |
| kern | Kernel messages |
| mail | Mail system |
| user | User-level messages |
| local0-local7 | Custom applications |

#### Custom rsyslog Rules

\`\`\`bash
# /etc/rsyslog.d/nginx.conf
# Log nginx to separate file
if $programname == 'nginx' then /var/log/nginx/nginx.log
& stop

# Log all errors to separate file
*.err  /var/log/errors.log

# Remote logging
*.* @@remote-server:514      # TCP
*.* @remote-server:514        # UDP
\`\`\`

### Part 3: logrotate — Log Rotation

#### Configuration

\`\`\`bash
# Main config
/etc/logrotate.conf

# Per-application configs
/etc/logrotate.d/

# Test config (dry run)
logrotate -d /etc/logrotate.conf

# Force rotation
logrotate -f /etc/logrotate.d/nginx

# Verbose mode
logrotate -v /etc/logrotate.conf
\`\`\`

#### logrotate Directives

\`\`\`bash
# /etc/logrotate.d/nginx
/var/log/nginx/*.log {
    daily                    # Rotate daily (hourly, weekly, monthly)
    rotate 14                # Keep 14 old logs
    compress                 # Compress old logs
    delaycompress           # Compress on 2nd rotation
    notifempty              # Don't rotate if empty
    missingok               # OK if log missing
    create 0640 nginx nginx # New file permissions
    sharedscripts           # Run scripts once for all logs
    postrotate              # After rotation
        systemctl reload nginx > /dev/null 2>&1 || true
    endscript
}
\`\`\`

#### Common Directives

| Directive | Purpose |
|-----------|---------|
| daily/weekly/monthly | Rotation frequency |
| rotate N | Keep N old logs |
| size 100M | Rotate when >100MB |
| maxage 30 | Delete logs older than 30 days |
| compress | gzip old logs |
| delaycompress | Don't compress latest |
| notifempty | Skip if empty |
| missingok | Don't error if missing |
| create mode owner group | New file permissions |
| copytruncate | Copy then truncate (no reload needed) |
| sharedscripts | Run postrotate once |

#### Production Examples

\`\`\`bash
# Apache
/var/log/httpd/*.log {
    daily
    rotate 30
    compress
    delaycompress
    missingok
    notifempty
    sharedscripts
    postrotate
        /bin/systemctl reload httpd.service > /dev/null 2>&1 || true
    endscript
}

# MySQL slow query log
/var/log/mysql/slow-query.log {
    weekly
    rotate 4
    compress
    delaycompress
    notifempty
    create 0640 mysql mysql
    postrotate
        mysqladmin flush-logs
    endscript
}

# Custom app with size-based rotation
/var/log/myapp/*.log {
    size 100M
    rotate 10
    compress
    delaycompress
    notifempty
    copytruncate        # App doesn't support reload
}
\`\`\`

### Part 4: Traditional Log Files

\`\`\`bash
# RHEL/CentOS
/var/log/messages       # General system messages
/var/log/secure         # Authentication logs
/var/log/maillog        # Mail server logs
/var/log/cron           # Cron job logs
/var/log/boot.log       # Boot messages

# Debian/Ubuntu
/var/log/syslog         # General system messages
/var/log/auth.log       # Authentication logs
/var/log/mail.log       # Mail server logs
/var/log/kern.log       # Kernel logs

# Common to all
/var/log/dmesg          # Kernel ring buffer
/var/log/wtmp           # Login records
/var/log/btmp           # Failed login attempts
/var/log/lastlog        # Last login info
\`\`\`

### Part 5: Log Analysis Techniques

\`\`\`bash
# Find errors in logs
grep -i error /var/log/messages
grep -i "failed\|error\|critical" /var/log/syslog

# Count occurrences
grep "authentication failure" /var/log/secure | wc -l

# Failed SSH logins
grep "Failed password" /var/log/secure | awk '{print $(NF-3)}' | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn

# Failed sudo attempts
grep "authentication failure" /var/log/secure | grep sudo

# Disk errors
dmesg | grep -i "I/O error"
grep -i "I/O error" /var/log/messages

# OOM (Out of Memory) killer
dmesg | grep -i "out of memory"
grep "Out of memory" /var/log/messages

# Segmentation faults
grep "segfault" /var/log/messages

# Service restarts
journalctl -u nginx | grep -i "start\|stop\|restart"
\`\`\`

### Part 6: Production Scenarios

#### Scenario 1: Investigate Service Failure

\`\`\`bash
# 1. Check service status
systemctl status nginx

# 2. View recent logs
journalctl -u nginx -n 100

# 3. Check for errors
journalctl -u nginx -p err -b

# 4. Follow logs in real-time
journalctl -u nginx -f

# 5. Check config syntax
nginx -t

# 6. Check traditional logs
tail -100 /var/log/nginx/error.log
\`\`\`

#### Scenario 2: Disk Full — Logs Too Large

\`\`\`bash
# 1. Check disk usage
df -h
du -sh /var/log/*

# 2. Find largest logs
find /var/log -type f -size +100M -exec ls -lh {} \\;
du -h /var/log | sort -rh | head -20

# 3. Clean old logs
find /var/log -name "*.gz" -mtime +30 -delete
find /var/log -name "*.old" -mtime +30 -delete

# 4. Clean journal
journalctl --disk-usage
journalctl --vacuum-size=100M

# 5. Force log rotation
logrotate -f /etc/logrotate.conf

# 6. Configure limits
vi /etc/systemd/journald.conf
# SystemMaxUse=500M
systemctl restart systemd-journald
\`\`\`

#### Scenario 3: Centralized Logging

\`\`\`bash
# Server side — receive logs
# /etc/rsyslog.conf
module(load="imtcp")
input(type="imtcp" port="514")

# Client side — send logs
# /etc/rsyslog.d/remote.conf
*.* @@logserver.example.com:514

# Restart
systemctl restart rsyslog
\`\`\`

### Part 7: Best Practices

1. **Monitor log disk usage**
   \`\`\`bash
   df -h /var/log
   journalctl --disk-usage
   \`\`\`

2. **Set up log rotation properly**
   \`\`\`bash
   # Test before deploying
   logrotate -d /etc/logrotate.d/myapp
   \`\`\`

3. **Keep security logs longer**
   \`\`\`bash
   /var/log/secure {
       weekly
       rotate 52        # 1 year
       compress
   }
   \`\`\`

4. **Use persistent journal**
   \`\`\`bash
   mkdir -p /var/log/journal
   systemctl restart systemd-journald
   \`\`\`

5. **Set up alerts for critical errors**
   \`\`\`bash
   # Monitor for specific errors
   journalctl -u nginx -p err --since "1 minute ago"
   \`\`\`

### Production Checklist

- [ ] Configure persistent journal
- [ ] Set journal size limits (SystemMaxUse)
- [ ] Configure logrotate for all apps
- [ ] Test log rotation (logrotate -d)
- [ ] Monitor /var/log disk usage
- [ ] Set up centralized logging for critical servers
- [ ] Keep security logs for compliance period
- [ ] Document log locations for all services
- [ ] Set up alerts for critical errors
